WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.726
Patrick Bateman was born in October 1961, which we can calculate because he

00:00:03.748 --> 00:00:05.950
turns 27 in October 1988.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:09.806
In March 1966, when Patrick was four years old, his younger brother Sean was born.

00:00:09.828 --> 00:00:13.038
The two brothers would not get along and were opposites in many ways.

00:00:13.124 --> 00:00:16.158
The Bateman family may have lived somewhere in the south, if we're going to

00:00:16.164 --> 00:00:18.122
believe what Sean tells his college classmates.

00:00:18.186 --> 00:00:21.002
Then again, he often lies to them and tells them he's poor.

00:00:21.066 --> 00:00:23.338
Plus, neither brother has a Southern accent.

00:00:23.434 --> 00:00:26.882
They do, however, have a family home in Newport, which is in Rhode Island,

00:00:26.946 --> 00:00:29.686
their father's long term hotel suite in New York City, 

00:00:29.708 --> 00:00:32.742
and their grandfather's home in Connecticut, all of which are in the northeast United States.

00:00:32.796 --> 00:00:36.438
Patrick was studious and took after his father, eventually ending up

00:00:36.444 --> 00:00:38.562
at a job with connections to his dad's circles.

00:00:38.626 --> 00:00:42.218
Sean, on the other hand, was a terrible student and was more interested in a

00:00:42.224 --> 00:00:43.702
rock and roll type of lifestyle.

00:00:43.766 --> 00:00:44.550
– [Sean Bateman] Rock and roll.

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:48.054
Although they did not get along, the two participated in upscale activities together. 

00:00:48.102 --> 00:00:50.470
like riding horses and playing tennis.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.558
What the two Bateman brothers do have in common is a natural charm with

00:00:53.584 --> 00:00:56.638
women, despite both being incredibly sleazy in their dating lives.

00:00:56.724 --> 00:00:59.962
In Sean's case, he's just an extremely selfish, conceited idiot.

00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:04.094
Patrick, on the other hand, has a better reputation, but in reality, he is a

00:01:04.132 --> 00:01:06.090
complete raging psychopath.

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:09.294
There are many elements that make up Patrick Bateman's psychopathy.

00:01:09.342 --> 00:01:12.782
So many, in fact, that you could not fit them all on one tape.

00:01:12.846 --> 00:01:15.794
So I've collected each of them and put them together to paint the true

00:01:15.832 --> 00:01:17.182
picture of his character.

00:01:17.246 --> 00:01:20.802
If you want to hear about Patrick's life before and after American Psycho,

00:01:20.866 --> 00:01:24.482
the answer to important questions like were his killings real or imagined?

00:01:24.546 --> 00:01:28.118
And which parts of Patrick Bateman were inspired from real life?

00:01:28.134 --> 00:01:30.220
Then stick  around to the end of this video.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.220
♪ Metal Music ♪

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:39.754
Welcome to Horror History, the show where I fully analyze the minds and

00:01:39.792 --> 00:01:42.214
timelines of characters and villains in the horror genre.

00:01:42.262 --> 00:01:46.014
My name is Marcus Halberstam, and today I'll be covering the entire story of

00:01:46.052 --> 00:01:46.954
Patrick Bateman.

00:01:47.002 --> 00:01:50.458
Patrick is most known from American Psycho, the modern classic by 

00:01:50.474 --> 00:01:53.818
Brett Easton Ellis, which was brought to the screen in 2000 by director Mary Harrington.

00:01:53.834 --> 00:01:57.794
However, his first appearance predates that in Ellis's 1987 novel, 

00:01:57.832 --> 00:01:59.310
The Rules of Attraction.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:02.478
As usual, the books are considered canon, so I'll be analyzing Bateman's

00:02:02.494 --> 00:02:03.506
story from the page.

00:02:03.608 --> 00:02:07.586
Patrick is certainly one of the, if not the craziest, characters I've ever

00:02:07.608 --> 00:02:08.818
covered on Horror History.

00:02:08.904 --> 00:02:12.434
His psychosis becomes so bad that it can be impossible to determine what's

00:02:12.482 --> 00:02:15.062
real and what's hallucinatory when the story is being told

00:02:15.116 --> 00:02:17.990
From his point of view. One of the most asked questions about American Psycho is,

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:22.070
 Did Patrick Bateman kill anyone? Not even the author himself knows.

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:26.666
– [Brett Easton Ellis] But it's the question that I am constantly asked at every reading I give

00:02:26.688 --> 00:02:30.634
– [Brett Easton Ellis] and there is no answer to it, because I don't have an answer to it.

00:02:30.672 --> 00:02:32.474
– [Brett Easton Ellis] I didn't have an answer when I was writing it.

00:02:32.512 --> 00:02:33.082
– [Brett Easton Ellis] I didn't know.

00:02:33.136 --> 00:02:35.118
– [Brett Easton Ellis] I mean, I thought it could go either way.

00:02:35.204 --> 00:02:38.734
– [Brett Easton Ellis] I kept flip flopping while I was writing the book, and that became to me,

00:02:38.772 --> 00:02:39.982
– [Brett Easton Ellis] something very interesting.

00:02:40.116 --> 00:02:43.086
However, after reading the book a couple of times and researching some other

00:02:43.108 --> 00:02:45.866
things the author has said publicly, I think I've come up with a pretty

00:02:45.908 --> 00:02:46.946
satisfying answer.

00:02:47.048 --> 00:02:50.642
That being said, it's still impossible to determine what is and isn't taking

00:02:50.696 --> 00:02:52.706
place in Bateman's imagination at times.

00:02:52.808 --> 00:02:56.466
So as a general rule, just to make this video possible, I'll try to take

00:02:56.488 --> 00:02:59.734
Bateman's word unless he gives us a reason to doubt him, which you'll find is quite often. 

00:02:59.772 --> 00:03:01.746
Part of my analysis.

00:03:01.778 --> 00:03:04.966
In a typical episode of Horror History is figuring out the timeline of events.

00:03:04.988 --> 00:03:08.486
Exactly when did everything take place? While we have a pretty good

00:03:08.508 --> 00:03:12.086
idea for most of the story, Patrick seems to lose track of time towards the end.

00:03:12.108 --> 00:03:15.078
So at a certain point, I'll have to use other means to determine the

00:03:15.084 --> 00:03:16.982
date rather than taking his word for it.

00:03:17.036 --> 00:03:20.174
But in order to get into the mysteries of his character, I'm going to hit

00:03:20.252 --> 00:03:24.094
rewind and go back to the formation of Patrick Bateman's insanity during his

00:03:24.132 --> 00:03:25.680
early teenage years.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.680
(Impact)

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.680
♪ Christmas Music ♪

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:36.114
Twas the night before Christmas, 1975, when a young Patrick Bateman found

00:03:36.152 --> 00:03:40.386
trouble in the night, he got a bit too frisky and wanted to play, and it

00:03:40.408 --> 00:03:43.330
came at the expense of one of the maids.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:48.866
Patrick attended a school called Exeter, where he first met

00:03:48.898 --> 00:03:51.698
Christopher Armstrong, who would eventually work at his company in their adulthood.

00:03:51.794 --> 00:03:55.010
After this, he attended Harvard University in Cambridge, Massachusetts,

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:56.950
eventually transferring into their business school.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:00.162
This seems to be about the time that his homicidal thoughts and possibly

00:04:00.226 --> 00:04:01.282
actions took root.

00:04:01.346 --> 00:04:04.266
Because nobody seems to know that he's a psychopath, it makes sense that

00:04:04.288 --> 00:04:06.986
this tendency would develop once he's out on his own, where he would

00:04:07.008 --> 00:04:08.550
presumably have some more privacy.

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:12.022
The earliest mentioned kill he makes came during his junior year at Harvard,

00:04:12.086 --> 00:04:15.738
after a freshman girl told him that life is full of endless possibilities,

00:04:15.834 --> 00:04:19.546
and he was so exasperated that he decapitated her, hung her head on a tree,

00:04:19.578 --> 00:04:21.514
and chucked her body into the Charles River.

00:04:21.562 --> 00:04:24.846
Whether his kills are real or imaginary doesn't matter when discussing the

00:04:24.868 --> 00:04:25.854
motivation behind them.

00:04:25.892 --> 00:04:28.882
Brett Easton Ellis has written seven novels, the first of which was called

00:04:28.936 --> 00:04:31.614
Less Than Zero, which is about the idea of passivity.

00:04:31.742 --> 00:04:34.654
– [Brett Easton Ellis] There's the passive man in the culture.

00:04:34.702 --> 00:04:39.054
– [Brett Easton Ellis] Is he really the sane one because of the insane chaos that surrounds him, or

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:42.870
– [Brett Easton Ellis] is he just as bad as the insane chaos around him because he doesn't act?

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:46.402
Taken at face value, American Psycho seems to be the exact opposite.

00:04:46.466 --> 00:04:50.134
An insane, chaotic psychopath living in the dull banality of corporate

00:04:50.172 --> 00:04:50.790
Wall Street life.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.546
Ellis states that he initially wrote the novel based on feelings of

00:04:53.568 --> 00:04:55.962
loneliness and alienation that he was having at the time.

00:04:56.016 --> 00:04:59.146
He considers most of his work to be fiction inspired by aspects of his

00:04:59.168 --> 00:05:02.494
actual life, and he interestingly cites American Psycho as his most

00:05:02.532 --> 00:05:03.930
autobiographical novel.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:09.326
– [Brett Easton Ellis] I think it really became a book about my problems with being a young man

00:05:09.428 --> 00:05:11.610
– [Brett Easton Ellis] with a lot of money in Manhattan.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:15.098
You can kind of imagine Ellis sitting at a bar and some dumb girl telling

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:18.786
him life is full of endless possibilities, and he's just so cynical that he

00:05:18.808 --> 00:05:19.922
wants to chop her head off.

00:05:19.976 --> 00:05:21.442
But obviously he can't do that.

00:05:21.496 --> 00:05:24.498
So instead he goes home and has Patrick Bateman do it instead.

00:05:24.584 --> 00:05:27.522
Venting through his art, Bateman's outbursts in the story are often

00:05:27.576 --> 00:05:29.510
triggered by something that upsets him like this.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:33.106
He enjoys reading about serial killers like Ted Bundy, Ed Gein and Charlie Manson,

00:05:33.138 --> 00:05:36.898
and takes inspiration from each of them before debatably surpassing

00:05:36.914 --> 00:05:37.414
them all.

00:05:37.436 --> 00:05:40.514
It was also at Harvard where Bateman started dating a girl named Bethany.

00:05:40.562 --> 00:05:43.666
He would write long, dark poems for her, and it's possible, but not

00:05:43.708 --> 00:05:46.630
confirmed, that she was on the receiving end of some of his rage.

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:49.786
He describes a lunch where she dumped him with her arm in a sling and a

00:05:49.808 --> 00:05:51.094
faint bruise above her cheek.

00:05:51.142 --> 00:05:54.006
Bethany leaves a lasting impression on him, and he treats her differently

00:05:54.038 --> 00:05:54.982
than all other women.

00:05:55.056 --> 00:05:58.286
There seems to be a deep yearning for approval from her, as opposed to the

00:05:58.308 --> 00:06:01.566
other girls he dates who he disrespects and couldn't care less about.

00:06:01.668 --> 00:06:04.826
It's clear that most of his targets are female, but we're never explicitly

00:06:04.858 --> 00:06:07.262
told where Patrick's mindset about women came from.

00:06:07.316 --> 00:06:10.274
But there is a line in The Rules of Attraction where Sean notes that their

00:06:10.312 --> 00:06:13.954
father is usually quite adept at picking up women, despite most likely being married.

00:06:13.992 --> 00:06:17.026
If he's willing to cheat on his wife quite often in front of their kids,

00:06:17.048 --> 00:06:20.198
it's not a stretch to assume that he's not the best role model when it

00:06:20.204 --> 00:06:21.202
comes to relationships.

00:06:21.266 --> 00:06:24.534
Both Patrick and Sean are serial cheaters in their own dating lives.

00:06:24.652 --> 00:06:26.534
There's no evidence to suggest that Mr. Bateman

00:06:26.572 --> 00:06:30.050
also inspired Patrick to take part in physically abusive behavior,

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:32.982
but there's also no evidence to suggest that he didn't.

00:06:33.046 --> 00:06:35.638
When talking about some of the elements of American Psycho that were drawn

00:06:35.654 --> 00:06:38.710
from his own life, Brett Easton Ellis brings up his own father.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:42.190
– [Brett Easton Ellis] American Psycho is in many ways an autobiographical book.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:46.158
– [Brett Easton Ellis] I really have to look back and look at my father in a lot of ways.

00:06:46.244 --> 00:06:52.026
– [Brett Easton Ellis] His attitude towards women, which did border on the misogynistic, influenced

00:06:52.058 --> 00:06:53.006
– [Brett Easton Ellis] and informed this book.

00:06:53.028 --> 00:06:55.838
– [Brett Easton Ellis] And I think in a lot of ways, when I was writing about Patrick Bateman, it

00:06:55.844 --> 00:06:57.950
– [Brett Easton Ellis] was really a lot of it was about my dad.

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:01.346
If Patrick did inherit some undesirable traits from his father, he may have

00:07:01.368 --> 00:07:03.330
also inherited unwanted traits from his mom.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:06.386
She seems to have some kind of severe mental illness as well, though we

00:07:06.408 --> 00:07:09.986
never get any good details on it other than being a psycho or a psychopath,

00:07:10.018 --> 00:07:13.014
as the title suggests, there are plenty of other disorders that seem to fit

00:07:13.052 --> 00:07:16.754
his behavior, like antisocial personality disorder, narcissistic personality disorder,

00:07:16.802 --> 00:07:19.910
a borderline personality disorder, and probably others.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:21.094
I'm not going to go through them all.

00:07:21.132 --> 00:07:24.134
It's more efficient if I just talk about the symptoms.

00:07:24.182 --> 00:07:27.274
In adulthood, Patrick works at the investment banking firm in New York City

00:07:27.312 --> 00:07:30.054
and lives in a luxury apartment on the Upper West Side of Manhattan.

00:07:30.102 --> 00:07:33.206
He probably got this job due to his upper class education and his father's

00:07:33.238 --> 00:07:34.294
influence in the industry.

00:07:34.342 --> 00:07:37.674
However, he clearly didn't want to work too closely with his dad, otherwise,

00:07:37.722 --> 00:07:39.402
he might have just worked for him directly.

00:07:39.466 --> 00:07:42.586
Patrick is also very particular about his appearance and has a detailed

00:07:42.618 --> 00:07:45.194
grooming routine in addition to a rigorous workout schedule.

00:07:45.242 --> 00:07:48.018
At some point, he had a personal trainer, but when the trainer started to

00:07:48.024 --> 00:07:50.974
come on to him, he fired him and started developing his own exercise program.

00:07:51.022 --> 00:07:54.994
The word homophobic gets used incorrectly a lot these days in place

00:07:55.032 --> 00:07:58.914
of the word heterosexist, but I am making no mistake when I say Patrick is homophobic.

00:07:58.952 --> 00:08:02.406
The exercise program is part of an obsession he has with his appearance.

00:08:02.428 --> 00:08:05.782
It's likely that this obsession is part of his desire to fit in,

00:08:05.836 --> 00:08:08.162
which is also why he's obsessed with popular culture.

00:08:08.226 --> 00:08:11.254
He rents so many movies that he becomes a Gold Circle member at the video

00:08:11.292 --> 00:08:14.458
store, allowing him to theoretically rent as many videos as he wants on a

00:08:14.464 --> 00:08:17.254
given night, provided he doesn't have any already overdue.

00:08:17.302 --> 00:08:20.618
The need to fit in with his peers also eventually results in him getting a

00:08:20.624 --> 00:08:23.686
girlfriend, a blonde named Evelyn Richards, who also comes from a wealthy family.

00:08:23.718 --> 00:08:27.070
Evelyn is an executive at a financial services company, American Express,

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:30.174
and they most likely met through their group of mutual friends.

00:08:30.292 --> 00:08:33.134
In some ways, Evelyn is the perfect match for him because they're both

00:08:33.172 --> 00:08:36.570
extremely shallow and only in the relationship for superficial reasons.

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:40.258
Evelyn for wealth and a fancy wedding, and Patrick for a trophy girl that he

00:08:40.264 --> 00:08:42.110
can walk all over with no repercussions.

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:45.282
In practice, they still manage to torture each other, but they stay together

00:08:45.336 --> 00:08:46.254
for social reasons.

00:08:46.302 --> 00:08:48.830
It's practical because they both run in the same circles.

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:50.886
We don't know exactly when they got together.

00:08:50.988 --> 00:08:55.218
In 1986 or 1987, Patrick attends the Kentucky Derby with a bimbo named

00:08:55.234 --> 00:08:56.114
Alison Poole.

00:08:56.162 --> 00:09:00.006
That doesn't necessarily mean he was cheating on Evelyn at the time, but it

00:09:00.028 --> 00:09:03.006
certainly wouldn't be above him if he was cheating on her with Alison.

00:09:03.058 --> 00:09:05.146
It wouldn't be the last time that he cheated on her

00:09:05.168 --> 00:09:06.262
at a Kentucky Derby.

00:09:06.326 --> 00:09:09.894
In January 1987, Patrick and Sean's mother is committed to a mental

00:09:09.942 --> 00:09:13.514
institution called Sandstone, and Patrick's only reaction is it was about time.

00:09:13.552 --> 00:09:16.846
Two months later, around the time of Sean's 21st birthday, he

00:09:16.868 --> 00:09:18.490
discovers that his father is dying.

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:21.530
Mr. Bateman's face turns yellow, and he becomes extremely thin.

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:24.638
During this time, Patrick starts to take over some responsibilities as the

00:09:24.644 --> 00:09:25.482
family patriarch.

00:09:25.546 --> 00:09:29.394
For example, he puts $7,000 in Sean's bank account, so he's taken care of at college.

00:09:29.432 --> 00:09:32.094
Do not mistake this for him caring about his brother.

00:09:32.142 --> 00:09:35.506
He's only doing this so he can hold it above his head later, which he does.

00:09:35.608 --> 00:09:39.438
Patrick turns 26 years old in October of 87 and goes to the Bahamas with

00:09:39.464 --> 00:09:41.862
Evelyn, which is a really weird time to go.

00:09:41.916 --> 00:09:44.322
I would never go somewhere that sunny during spooky season.

00:09:44.386 --> 00:09:47.766
And before you say anything, yes, LA is an exception because there are,

00:09:47.788 --> 00:09:49.222
like, 30 haunts to experience.

00:09:49.356 --> 00:09:52.374
By early November, Patrick's father is on his deathbed, so he makes

00:09:52.412 --> 00:09:54.682
arrangements for Sean to come in and visit one last time.

00:09:54.736 --> 00:09:57.798
But when Sean is late, he thinks that he must be off doing drugs and holds

00:09:57.814 --> 00:09:59.734
him in disdain for his lack of responsibility.

00:09:59.862 --> 00:10:03.146
As we will find out, Patrick has a problem with drugs of his own, and he

00:10:03.168 --> 00:10:05.574
often holds others to a higher standard than himself.

00:10:05.702 --> 00:10:08.446
As he waits in the hospital lobby, he makes multiple attempts to call

00:10:08.468 --> 00:10:10.570
Evelyn, but she refuses to come to the hospital.

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:13.758
At one point, she claims to be asleep, but he hears faint music in the

00:10:13.764 --> 00:10:16.862
background, the earliest of his suspicions that she isn't faithful to him either.

00:10:16.916 --> 00:10:19.586
It's pretty messed up to cheat on someone who is about to be

00:10:19.608 --> 00:10:22.734
mourning the loss of a parent, but this is another example of hypocrisy,

00:10:22.782 --> 00:10:25.218
because Bateman is seemingly never faithful to her.

00:10:25.304 --> 00:10:28.174
Another hypocritical moment can be seen when Sean arrives at the hospital

00:10:28.222 --> 00:10:31.158
and Patrick grills him on his lack of direction in life, telling him he

00:10:31.164 --> 00:10:32.998
should try to become the son that his father wanted.

00:10:33.084 --> 00:10:36.134
Despite this, Patrick is planning to go against his father's wishes after

00:10:36.172 --> 00:10:38.310
his death by firing the aides that he doesn't like.

00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:41.242
He also seems to hint that he'll be cutting off funding for Sean's college degeneracy.

00:10:41.296 --> 00:10:45.194
He would be pleased to know that you're taking, let's call it a

00:10:45.232 --> 00:10:47.910
leave of absence from that place, he explains.

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:51.962
This causes Sean to storm out, claiming he'll go to Utah or Europe before

00:10:52.016 --> 00:10:53.850
taking the limo back to his dad's place.

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:57.118
Throughout the hospital scene, Patrick is never shown to spend any time with

00:10:57.124 --> 00:10:57.642
his father.

00:10:57.706 --> 00:11:01.006
For him, it's all about making arrangements and the business of running the family.

00:11:01.028 --> 00:11:03.854
There are a couple outbreaks on his timeline over the next few

00:11:03.892 --> 00:11:07.258
months. That Christmas, he went to Aspen, Colorado, for some skiing and

00:11:07.284 --> 00:11:09.342
violated a waitress with a can of hairspray.

00:11:09.406 --> 00:11:12.814
In the spring, he videotapes the torture and demise of two escort girls.

00:11:12.862 --> 00:11:15.742
And that takes us up to the beginning of American Psycho.

00:11:15.806 --> 00:11:20.018
The events of the novel began on April 1 988, April Fool's Day, which is a

00:11:20.024 --> 00:11:23.186
fitting day to start a story where we have no idea what's real and what's

00:11:23.218 --> 00:11:26.146
not half the time. Bateman is spending the day with his coworker Timothy Price

00:11:26.178 --> 00:11:29.366
by having lunch, playing squash, and getting drinks at Harry's, where

00:11:29.388 --> 00:11:32.638
Price continually rants about the Fisher account that Paul Owen is handling.

00:11:32.754 --> 00:11:35.946
Something about this day spent with Price seems to leave a big impression on

00:11:35.968 --> 00:11:39.082
Bateman, and from this point on, he almost seems to model himself after Price.

00:11:39.136 --> 00:11:41.946
For one, he becomes obsessed with the Fisher account.

00:11:42.048 --> 00:11:45.434
During a cab ride, Price complains about not making enough money at Pierce and Pierce,

00:11:45.482 --> 00:11:49.178
as well as all the issues plaguing their city, primarily the beggars

00:11:49.274 --> 00:11:51.354
for these wealthy Wall Street investment bankers.

00:11:51.402 --> 00:11:55.086
The world revolves around social status, which is basically dictated by money.

00:11:55.108 --> 00:11:58.126
The homeless have no money, so they're at the bottom of the totem pole.

00:11:58.158 --> 00:12:00.466
Bateman and Price look down on them as a result.

00:12:00.568 --> 00:12:02.290
I mean, the man's last name is Price.

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:04.078
It's almost too on the nose.

00:12:04.174 --> 00:12:07.534
And speaking of name etymology, Patrick's girlfriend is named Evelyn Richards,

00:12:07.582 --> 00:12:08.962
and she's rich.

00:12:09.026 --> 00:12:13.026
Patrick himself, the star of American Psycho, has the last name Bateman,

00:12:13.058 --> 00:12:16.646
which sounds very similar to Bates, as in in Norman Bates, the main bad from

00:12:16.668 --> 00:12:17.954
Alfred Hitchcock's Psycho.

00:12:18.002 --> 00:12:20.614
There's even a little reference in the book when one of Bateman's colleagues

00:12:20.662 --> 00:12:21.654
goes to Phoenix.

00:12:21.782 --> 00:12:24.070
Phoenix? Janet Lee was from Phoenix.

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:25.626
I stall, then continue.

00:12:25.728 --> 00:12:27.190
She got stabbed in the shower.

00:12:27.270 --> 00:12:28.262
Disappointing scene.

00:12:28.326 --> 00:12:29.094
I pause.

00:12:29.142 --> 00:12:30.054
Blood looked fake.

00:12:30.102 --> 00:12:32.922
But as Bateman and Price are riding in the cab, there's a newspaper story

00:12:32.976 --> 00:12:35.886
about two people who disappeared on a yacht party hosted by a New York

00:12:35.908 --> 00:12:38.878
socialite where blood residue and smash champagne glasses are found.

00:12:38.964 --> 00:12:42.330
Foul play is suspected, but it's not clear if Bateman is responsible.

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:45.726
In truth, I'd like to talk about all of his attacks in this video, but many

00:12:45.748 --> 00:12:48.846
of them are mentioned in passing, as if they are a minor detail, leading me

00:12:48.868 --> 00:12:51.274
to feel like he doesn't even mention most of them to the reader.

00:12:51.322 --> 00:12:54.446
Upon getting out of the taxi, they tease a beggar by mockingly offering the

00:12:54.468 --> 00:12:57.522
cab up to him and asking if he takes American Express credit cards.

00:12:57.586 --> 00:13:00.646
Patrick and his associates constantly bait the homeless like this every time

00:13:00.668 --> 00:13:01.350
they see one.

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:04.518
At the very least, it's a cruel prank, and at the worst, it's a sign of the

00:13:04.524 --> 00:13:07.142
mercilessness they have towards those they see as lower than them.

00:13:07.196 --> 00:13:10.658
In Patrick's case, this translates to a brutal lack of empathy for victims

00:13:10.674 --> 00:13:11.650
of all types.

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:14.886
Price can be heard grumbling about how he forgot to turn the tapes he rented

00:13:14.918 --> 00:13:15.930
last night to Video Haven.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:19.146
And if you've seen anything related to American Psycho, you know that

00:13:19.168 --> 00:13:22.870
returning videotapes becomes a major aspect of Patrick's personality.

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:25.854
Either he's taking after Price, or the two are being portrayed as

00:13:25.892 --> 00:13:29.374
interchangeable, once again highlighting the dull banality of the upper class.

00:13:29.492 --> 00:13:32.746
They're headed to Evelyn's house for dinner, where we see what I think is

00:13:32.788 --> 00:13:35.562
one of the most psychopathic traits Bateman has.

00:13:35.636 --> 00:13:39.566
He encyclopedically identifies what type of clothing every single character

00:13:39.598 --> 00:13:43.074
wears and what brand each piece of clothing is from each time we see them

00:13:43.112 --> 00:13:44.798
for the entire novel.

00:13:44.894 --> 00:13:48.274
This is perhaps the earliest example of Bateman's fervent obsession with

00:13:48.312 --> 00:13:49.138
material things.

00:13:49.224 --> 00:13:52.582
If I had to describe Patrick Bateman in just a few words, it would be a man

00:13:52.636 --> 00:13:55.378
driven to psychopathy in his pursuit of materialism.

00:13:55.474 --> 00:13:58.758
At the end of the day, he's really just an exaggerated product of his time.

00:13:58.844 --> 00:14:01.650
Here on this channel, we've already talked about many horror properties in

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:04.582
the 1980s that are driven by the rabid consumer culture.

00:14:04.646 --> 00:14:07.946
Halloween Three is about a sinister salesman who uses advertising and

00:14:07.968 --> 00:14:10.186
product frenzy to brainwash and kill children.

00:14:10.288 --> 00:14:13.354
Stranger Things Three, which has not yet released a videotape like the first

00:14:13.392 --> 00:14:16.894
Two Seasons, uses a shopping mall as a cover for a secret terrorist base.

00:14:17.012 --> 00:14:20.426
The original Child's Play even presents subtextual themes of the harmful

00:14:20.458 --> 00:14:23.226
nature of product and toy mania during the holiday season.

00:14:23.338 --> 00:14:26.346
American Psycho takes a slightly different approach by making the consumer

00:14:26.378 --> 00:14:27.974
the villain rather than the victim.

00:14:28.042 --> 00:14:31.186
In short, the main reason for the boost in product sales, commercials and

00:14:31.208 --> 00:14:34.574
the commodification of material goods in the 1980s was the booming economy

00:14:34.622 --> 00:14:37.838
and in the United States, reduced taxes and government regulations.

00:14:37.934 --> 00:14:41.830
Encyclopedia.com has a page on 1980s commerce that offers the perfect

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:43.858
explanation for Patrick Bateman's behavior.

00:14:43.954 --> 00:14:47.286
For the average American, the booming economy of the 1980s meant a rise in

00:14:47.308 --> 00:14:50.370
disposable income the money that people have to spend on items that they do

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:51.466
not need but want.

00:14:51.568 --> 00:14:55.578
Many Americans engage in conspicuous consumption, purchasing luxury goods to

00:14:55.584 --> 00:14:56.970
show others that they're doing well.

00:14:57.040 --> 00:15:00.234
In American Psycho, we see Patrick Bateman become so obsessed with

00:15:00.272 --> 00:15:03.642
conspicuous consumption that it basically dictates every decision he ever makes

00:15:03.696 --> 00:15:07.166
In the novel, we see that he is essentially brainwashed to notice how

00:15:07.188 --> 00:15:10.110
decadent the clothing others are wearing is above anything else.

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:14.266
I do not exaggerate when I say probably 25% of the book is just Bateman

00:15:14.298 --> 00:15:16.746
describing clothing and other luxury items.

00:15:16.858 --> 00:15:19.330
This is his primary barometer in judging people.

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:22.098
They arrive at Evelyn's and they're greeted by her friend Courtney, who

00:15:22.104 --> 00:15:23.022
flirts with Patrick.

00:15:23.086 --> 00:15:26.238
The plan is to have a sushi night in which annoys the men because they won't

00:15:26.254 --> 00:15:29.022
be able to show off their wealth by dining out in a fancy restaurant.

00:15:29.086 --> 00:15:32.566
Eating in will not increase their social status and won't give Patrick the

00:15:32.588 --> 00:15:35.926
rush of dopamine he gets from flashing his Platinum American Express card at

00:15:35.948 --> 00:15:36.850
a busy restaurant.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:40.182
They're also joined by two of Evelyn's artist friends who are basically

00:15:40.236 --> 00:15:43.146
metal heads, and Bateman looks down on them, thinking the male friend would

00:15:43.168 --> 00:15:46.186
probably be unable to secure a table at Camols, which I'm assuming is a

00:15:46.208 --> 00:15:47.594
restaurant or club in New York.

00:15:47.712 --> 00:15:51.046
Getting reservations and getting into high profile events provides Patrick

00:15:51.078 --> 00:15:52.502
with a similar rush of dopamine.

00:15:52.566 --> 00:15:55.754
Due to my career as a content creator, the feeling associated with getting

00:15:55.792 --> 00:15:59.226
into VIP events is one of the few aspects of the character that I can relate

00:15:59.258 --> 00:16:02.318
to, the difference being that I don't have the social battery to go out all

00:16:02.324 --> 00:16:02.740
the time.

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:05.406
However, I know that these people exist because I do see them.

00:16:05.428 --> 00:16:08.594
Whenever I go to creator events, there are certain people who seem to be at

00:16:08.632 --> 00:16:10.418
everyone. You know who you are.

00:16:10.504 --> 00:16:13.826
During the dinner at Evelyn's, Patrick goes on a manifesto speech about the

00:16:13.848 --> 00:16:15.794
problems currently facing their society.

00:16:15.922 --> 00:16:21.702
– [Patrick Bateman] We have to end apartheid, for one, and slow down the nuclear arms race, stop

00:16:21.756 --> 00:16:24.258
– [Patrick Bateman] terrorism and world hunger.

00:16:24.354 --> 00:16:27.894
However, many of the concerns he lists are completely hypocritical, and his

00:16:27.932 --> 00:16:29.490
actions actively say otherwise.

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:32.466
He is extremely classist, so he doesn't care about apartheid.

00:16:32.498 --> 00:16:35.190
He even later admits that he has a pro apartheid stance.

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:38.666
He claims we need to care for the elderly, yet his mother suffers in some

00:16:38.688 --> 00:16:42.330
kind of elderly care facility or mental hospital, and he rarely visits her.

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:45.594
Despite claiming we need to stop crime and drugs on our streets, he commits

00:16:45.642 --> 00:16:47.674
crimes and buys illegal drugs as a hobby.

00:16:47.722 --> 00:16:50.878
He advocates for training and jobs for the unemployed and sheltering the

00:16:50.884 --> 00:16:54.254
homeless, then spends the entire book torturing the homeless for not having

00:16:54.292 --> 00:16:54.798
a job.

00:16:54.884 --> 00:16:58.258
He raves about freedom of choice in terms of abortion, but later forces a

00:16:58.264 --> 00:16:59.870
woman he's seeing to get an abortion.

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:04.066
He says we have to curb graphic sex and violence on TV, in movies, and in

00:17:04.088 --> 00:17:08.726
popular music, and most ironically, 

00:17:08.828 --> 00:17:11.906
– [Patrick Bateman] We have to promote general social concern... and less materialism.

00:17:12.018 --> 00:17:15.142
Everything he's saying just consists of talking points that he's probably

00:17:15.196 --> 00:17:18.246
heard or read somewhere, which he doesn't believe in whatsoever, but he

00:17:18.268 --> 00:17:20.310
wants to use to gain favor with his colleagues.

00:17:20.390 --> 00:17:23.514
His never ending quest to increase his social status extends beyond just

00:17:23.552 --> 00:17:24.362
flashing his money.

00:17:24.416 --> 00:17:30.842
His entire social life revolves around fitting in 
– [Patrick Bateman] because I want to fit in.

00:17:30.976 --> 00:17:34.086
Courtney and the two artist types leave, so Evelyn, Price and Bateman

00:17:34.118 --> 00:17:37.306
continue to chat over some drinks, where Bateman notices his vodka cranberry

00:17:37.338 --> 00:17:40.926
looking like a glass of watery blood, the very earliest and most subdued of

00:17:40.948 --> 00:17:43.966
his violent thoughts and fantasies, which we'll get into more later in his story.

00:17:43.988 --> 00:17:47.058
Price and Evelyn openly flirt in front of him, leading him to

00:17:47.064 --> 00:17:48.542
conclude that they're having an affair.

00:17:48.606 --> 00:17:51.474
He thinks Price is the most interesting man he knows, yet their

00:17:51.512 --> 00:17:54.578
conversations only revolve around the discussion of superficial things.

00:17:54.664 --> 00:17:57.906
In the hierarchy of Wall Street investment guys, Price is considered more

00:17:57.928 --> 00:18:01.142
valuable than him, leading him to believe that Evelyn would prefer Price.

00:18:01.276 --> 00:18:04.406
Eventually, Price leaves, and after 15 minutes of trying to coerce his

00:18:04.428 --> 00:18:07.606
girlfriend to go to bed with him, Bateman gives up and goes home to take

00:18:07.628 --> 00:18:08.482
care of himself.

00:18:08.626 --> 00:18:12.758
Five weeks after this, on May 7, 1988, he joined Evelyn and her parents in a

00:18:12.764 --> 00:18:16.346
trip back to the Kentucky Derby, where he cheated on her again and won a lot

00:18:16.368 --> 00:18:18.582
of money off a horse called Indecent Exposure.

00:18:18.646 --> 00:18:21.766
We next check in on him later that month as he does his morning stretches

00:18:21.798 --> 00:18:24.558
and goes through his unbelievably detailed grooming routine, showing the

00:18:24.564 --> 00:18:27.726
true extent of his obsession with its appearance, and more importantly, the

00:18:27.748 --> 00:18:30.042
money that goes into upholding his perfect image.

00:18:30.106 --> 00:18:33.486
Bateman also has a bizarre fixation with the daytime television program

00:18:33.508 --> 00:18:34.430
The Patty Winter Show.

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:37.778
Throughout the story, we see him interact with many pop culture phenomena of

00:18:37.784 --> 00:18:38.674
the 1980s.

00:18:38.712 --> 00:18:42.002
Because his entire personality revolves around fitting in, he doesn't form

00:18:42.056 --> 00:18:45.666
any of his own opinions about pop culture, he just likes whatever is hip, a

00:18:45.688 --> 00:18:47.742
term that comes up many times throughout the novel.

00:18:47.806 --> 00:18:50.150
It turns out Bateman is not a bass man.

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:53.318
The Patty Winter Show is the fictional equivalent of daytime talk shows that

00:18:53.324 --> 00:18:57.298
blew up in the 80s, like Sally Geraldo, and most notably The Oprah Winfrey Show.

00:18:57.314 --> 00:18:59.362
Which was probably the basis for Patty Winters.

00:18:59.426 --> 00:19:02.154
They both interviewed Donald Trump in the late eighties and share a few

00:19:02.192 --> 00:19:03.530
other common segments as well.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:07.094
Patrick makes note of many Patty Winters topics in his interior monologue,

00:19:07.142 --> 00:19:09.306
but they cannot always be taken at face value.

00:19:09.408 --> 00:19:12.586
This morning's show is about women with multiple personalities, which seems

00:19:12.608 --> 00:19:14.874
like a believable topic for a daytime talk host.

00:19:14.922 --> 00:19:18.606
Oprah herself covered it in 1990, but throughout the story, the segments on

00:19:18.628 --> 00:19:22.078
Patty Winters get stranger and stranger, and many have suggested that this

00:19:22.084 --> 00:19:24.842
is a reflection of Patrick's own sanity starting to slip.

00:19:24.906 --> 00:19:28.066
Patrick even admits at one point that he might have been hallucinating while

00:19:28.088 --> 00:19:28.690
watching it.

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:32.146
As I mentioned, Bateman's reliability as a narrator comes into question at

00:19:32.168 --> 00:19:35.374
some point as his mind delves into strange and unbelievable fantasies.

00:19:35.422 --> 00:19:38.946
So the topic of each Patty Winter Show may help us differentiate fact from

00:19:38.968 --> 00:19:41.366
fantasy at times, which I'll get into later.

00:19:41.468 --> 00:19:45.174
But first, we would find out just how wild a night out on the town can get

00:19:45.212 --> 00:19:47.566
when money is no object and the drinks are flowing.

00:19:47.618 --> 00:19:49.370
Early at Harry's
(Impact)
♪ Classical Music ♪

00:19:57.950 --> 00:20:01.718
Bateman goes with Timothy Price to meet with two other associates, David Van Patten 

00:20:01.734 --> 00:20:02.966
and Craig McDermott.

00:20:02.998 --> 00:20:06.282
For drinks at Harry's, they quiz him about style tips, and Bateman and Price

00:20:06.336 --> 00:20:08.986
battle to impress their friends with their knowledge of high end clothing etiquette.

00:20:09.018 --> 00:20:11.694
Price seems to have the upper hand. At this point.

00:20:11.732 --> 00:20:13.838
I'd consider Price to be the leader of the group.

00:20:13.924 --> 00:20:17.274
When another colleague, Preston, joins the conversation, Price makes a joke

00:20:17.322 --> 00:20:20.314
about blowing his moron friends heads off with a 38 Magnum.

00:20:20.362 --> 00:20:23.426
While Patrick would eventually not hesitate to say outlandish stuff like

00:20:23.448 --> 00:20:25.170
this, he is more reserved early on.

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:28.318
It's possible that seeing Price get away with making a joke about killing

00:20:28.334 --> 00:20:31.298
his friends is what gives Bateman the confidence to openly speak out the

00:20:31.304 --> 00:20:33.638
dark thoughts going on in his brain later on.

00:20:33.724 --> 00:20:36.354
These comments always get brushed off as a joke or misheard.

00:20:36.402 --> 00:20:38.966
He definitely takes after Price in many other ways, too.

00:20:39.068 --> 00:20:42.342
In fact, just about everyone in their community is trying to be like someone

00:20:42.396 --> 00:20:45.586
richer and more powerful than themselves, which leads to an entire industry

00:20:45.618 --> 00:20:49.046
of guys in their late 20s all trying to look the same, with no personality

00:20:49.078 --> 00:20:49.642
of their own.

00:20:49.696 --> 00:20:52.714
We see this immediately after as Price mistakes a man standing at the bar

00:20:52.752 --> 00:20:53.910
for one of their coworkers.

00:20:53.990 --> 00:20:57.438
This is something that comes up time and time again the idea of mistaking a

00:20:57.444 --> 00:20:58.282
person's identity.

00:20:58.346 --> 00:21:01.550
Patrick makes a mental note and uses this to his advantage later on.

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:05.086
The constant misidentification among colleagues satirizes the society of

00:21:05.108 --> 00:21:08.570
yuppies, or young urban professionals a term that arose in the early eighties

00:21:08.570 --> 00:21:11.746
to describe the growing culture of young adults with high paying jobs and

00:21:11.768 --> 00:21:13.038
fashionable lifestyles.

00:21:13.134 --> 00:21:16.226
In fact, if you look at the portrayal of yuppies in books, magazines, or

00:21:16.248 --> 00:21:19.314
television at the time you might have a hard time distinguishing them from

00:21:19.352 --> 00:21:20.734
something out of American Psycho.

00:21:20.782 --> 00:21:24.386
The topic of conversation changes to Paul Owen, the man handling the coveted

00:21:24.418 --> 00:21:25.222
Fisher account.

00:21:25.356 --> 00:21:28.966
Bateman calls out Preston for making an antisemitic remark about Owen and

00:21:28.988 --> 00:21:32.454
later is the only one who refuses to laugh at Preston's racist joke. 

00:21:32.492 --> 00:21:35.446
Which the others chalk up to Patrick trying to take the high ground because Evelyn

00:21:35.478 --> 00:21:37.606
is part of the American Civil Liberties Union.

00:21:37.718 --> 00:21:41.050
– [Craig] Oh, I forgot Bateman's dating someone from the ACLU.

00:21:41.710 --> 00:21:44.682
After ditching Preston, they get into a cab and go to dinner only then

00:21:44.736 --> 00:21:48.106
realizing nobody has made a reservation anywhere and Patrick describes his

00:21:48.128 --> 00:21:49.438
feeling of panic when this happens.

00:21:49.524 --> 00:21:52.558
While most would not consider this a big deal Bateman doesn't want to be

00:21:52.564 --> 00:21:55.598
seen in public with no dinner reservation because that doesn't align with

00:21:55.604 --> 00:21:56.762
his high powered image.

00:21:56.826 --> 00:21:59.982
They make reservations at Pastels and when they show up a few minutes later

00:22:00.036 --> 00:22:03.250
they're able to be seated immediately because McDermott knows the maître d.

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:06.674
Bateman is envious of this because that's the end goal for him using his

00:22:06.712 --> 00:22:09.966
power, money, and connections to get whatever he wants in life and further

00:22:09.998 --> 00:22:10.910
boost his image.

00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:14.486
The table even gets a free round of bellinis from the maître d but Price has

00:22:14.508 --> 00:22:17.110
them sent away to reestablish his dominance over the group.

00:22:17.180 --> 00:22:19.954
They're approached by yet another fellow banker named Scott Montgomery.

00:22:20.002 --> 00:22:23.974
But Montgomery's status by far outweighs their own and the group oggles over

00:22:24.012 --> 00:22:27.094
how much money he's worth and how attractive his date is after he walks away.

00:22:27.142 --> 00:22:29.706
In their eyes, these things go hand in hand.

00:22:29.808 --> 00:22:32.262
Everything, including women, is a status symbol.

00:22:32.326 --> 00:22:35.114
Bateman tries to take back some of the attention by showing off his new

00:22:35.152 --> 00:22:35.770
business card.

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:38.458
In the book, the business card scene takes place with them still in the

00:22:38.464 --> 00:22:41.278
restaurant Pastel but in the movie, it happens in their office.

00:22:41.364 --> 00:22:42.718
So I'm just going to show that.

00:22:42.804 --> 00:22:44.010
Use your imagination.

00:22:44.090 --> 00:22:46.974
Each of the men tries to one up Bateman by showing off their own business

00:22:47.012 --> 00:22:50.386
cards to which Bateman describes himself as being overcome with a spasm of jealousy.

00:22:50.408 --> 00:22:55.390
– [Patrick Bateman] I can't believe that Bryce prefers Van Patten's card to mine.

00:22:55.470 --> 00:22:57.538
To a normal person, they all look the same.

00:22:57.624 --> 00:22:59.502
However, they're all chasing yuppy trends.

00:22:59.566 --> 00:23:03.130
Just like how their clothing and haircuts make them look indistinguishable

00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:04.294
from one another.

00:23:04.412 --> 00:23:07.606
To them, in a world where everyone in their circles is rich, everyone is

00:23:07.628 --> 00:23:09.302
good looking, and everyone has everything

00:23:09.356 --> 00:23:12.630
In terms of material goods, these subtle differences are a huge deal.

00:23:12.700 --> 00:23:14.342
Another status symbol to quarrel over.

00:23:14.396 --> 00:23:16.842
We see it affect Patrick seemingly more than anyone else.

00:23:16.896 --> 00:23:20.282
His entire self worth is shattered by not having the best card, leaving him

00:23:20.336 --> 00:23:23.738
unexpectedly depressed and causing him to have a huge angry outburst at

00:23:23.744 --> 00:23:26.954
McDermott, where he explains why the Red Snapper Pizza isn't good at this restaurant. 

00:23:26.992 --> 00:23:29.946
Like his assertions about what clothing can be paired

00:23:29.978 --> 00:23:33.166
together, his knowledge of what menu items are hip comes from magazines or

00:23:33.188 --> 00:23:34.058
restaurant guides.

00:23:34.154 --> 00:23:37.486
There's no need to decide whether he likes the pizza for himself, because it

00:23:37.508 --> 00:23:40.010
would not be a good look to go against the mainstream opinion.

00:23:40.090 --> 00:23:41.694
Staying hip is all that matters.

00:23:41.742 --> 00:23:44.558
In fact, when their appetizers come, they don't even eat them before they're

00:23:44.574 --> 00:23:47.746
taken away, which I see as the men just wanting to be seen receiving the appetizers. 

00:23:47.768 --> 00:23:51.266
Bateman also grabs a waitress and tells her they're planning to

00:23:51.288 --> 00:23:54.406
spend a lot of money while slipping her a $50 bill to ensure that their

00:23:54.428 --> 00:23:57.266
table be allowed to smoke cigars in the restaurant without any complaints

00:23:57.298 --> 00:23:59.010
from fellow patrons being considered.

00:23:59.090 --> 00:24:02.662
This power move impresses the other men all as well as they continue to talk

00:24:02.716 --> 00:24:05.862
while ogling at hardbody Waitresses, a term that they use to describe

00:24:05.926 --> 00:24:09.402
attractive women taken from the 1984 movie Hard Bodies, once again

00:24:09.456 --> 00:24:12.186
demonstrating the control that pop culture has over them.

00:24:12.288 --> 00:24:14.582
– [Grant ]Hard Bodies little foxes down on the beach.

00:24:14.646 --> 00:24:18.054
They receive a champagne bottle from Scott Montgomery, and Price is insulted

00:24:18.102 --> 00:24:20.442
because it's non vintage, which he sees as cheap.

00:24:20.506 --> 00:24:23.258
The forehead do a nightclub called Tunnel, where they don't meet the dress

00:24:23.274 --> 00:24:25.230
code, but Price manages to get them in anyway.

00:24:25.300 --> 00:24:27.850
Bateman assumes he does this by offering a generous tip.

00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:31.006
McDermott is still complaining about not getting the Red Snapper Pizzas, and

00:24:31.028 --> 00:24:33.538
Bateman has an urge to knife him and slice his face open.

00:24:33.624 --> 00:24:36.798
But Price sends McDermott and Van Patten away with the two VIP basement

00:24:36.814 --> 00:24:39.694
passes he received, allowing him to take Bateman to buy some cocaine.

00:24:39.742 --> 00:24:41.890
And Price knows all the right people to talk to.

00:24:41.960 --> 00:24:45.826
Keep in mind that while the characters value drugs, I am in no way promoting

00:24:45.858 --> 00:24:46.934
or glorifying them.

00:24:46.972 --> 00:24:50.566
I am simply discussing what happened in a documentary context so that I can

00:24:50.588 --> 00:24:53.330
better explain the role that they play in the character's downfall.

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:54.562
After doing coke in the bathroom.

00:24:54.626 --> 00:24:57.046
They're disappointed with the strength of the drug and the amount they

00:24:57.068 --> 00:24:59.926
received, demonstrating that this is a very regular thing for them and

00:24:59.948 --> 00:25:02.806
alluding to the addiction problems that Bateman would eventually face, not

00:25:02.828 --> 00:25:05.674
just with drugs, but with everything that gives him pleasure, from dining

00:25:05.722 --> 00:25:08.890
out to adult films to hacking up and slashing hard bodies.

00:25:08.970 --> 00:25:11.578
He goes back to the bar to get them more drinks, and is told that the drink

00:25:11.594 --> 00:25:14.366
tickets that they received had expired and it would be a cash bar for the

00:25:14.388 --> 00:25:15.326
rest of the night.

00:25:15.508 --> 00:25:16.900
– [Bartender] Twenty five dollars.

00:25:22.550 --> 00:25:26.494
– [Patrick Bateman] I want to stab you to death and then play around with your blood.

00:25:26.622 --> 00:25:29.586
This is the first time that Patrick's Homicidal thoughts slip out, but his

00:25:29.608 --> 00:25:32.446
voice is drowned out by the music, so he faces no consequences.

00:25:32.558 --> 00:25:35.282
However, the more he does this, the more careless he becomes.

00:25:35.346 --> 00:25:36.790
So we'll come back to it.

00:25:36.860 --> 00:25:39.826
Price, who has been agitated the entire night, tells Bateman that he's

00:25:39.858 --> 00:25:43.126
leaving, which Bateman, perhaps because of the drugs, doesn't understand.

00:25:43.308 --> 00:25:46.014
No, Bateman, I'm serious, you dumb son of a bitch.

00:25:46.082 --> 00:25:49.018
Leaving, disappearing and disappear he does.

00:25:49.104 --> 00:25:52.614
Price stands up on a railing with a champagne glass and then says, Goodbye,

00:25:52.662 --> 00:25:53.334
fuck heads.

00:25:53.382 --> 00:25:56.294
Before running away into the train tunnel, after which the club is themed.

00:25:56.342 --> 00:25:59.658
We don't see Price again for almost three years after this, which coincides

00:25:59.674 --> 00:26:02.762
with Bateman spending the next three years in a downward spiral.

00:26:02.826 --> 00:26:05.486
The next morning, Patrick arrives at work and has an encounter in the

00:26:05.508 --> 00:26:07.738
elevator, where he's confused for someone named Marcus.

00:26:07.834 --> 00:26:10.926
When he arrives, he tells the secretary, Jean that he came late due to an

00:26:10.948 --> 00:26:13.778
aerobics class, which is most likely a lie that he devises to hide the

00:26:13.784 --> 00:26:15.986
hangover he probably had from the previous night.

00:26:16.088 --> 00:26:19.186
He's aware that Jean is in love with him and he thinks he'll probably end up

00:26:19.208 --> 00:26:19.842
marrying her.

00:26:19.896 --> 00:26:23.190
He is correct, but we'll talk more about that after the events of American Psycho.

00:26:23.260 --> 00:26:26.326
 Jean updates him on the day's meetings, which he tells her to cancel.

00:26:26.348 --> 00:26:28.214
– [Patrick Bateman] Just say no.

00:26:28.332 --> 00:26:31.286
He instead has her make lunch and dinner reservations for him, causing her

00:26:31.308 --> 00:26:33.042
to ask if the dinner is something romantic.

00:26:33.106 --> 00:26:35.458
He tells her no, and just says he'll make them himself.

00:26:35.554 --> 00:26:38.506
The truth is that he's taking his mistress Courtney out for a date that evening. 

00:26:38.528 --> 00:26:41.946
He may be shy about this because he actually likes Jean, and when

00:26:41.968 --> 00:26:44.938
she follows his orders, he even mentions how he's touched and flattered by

00:26:44.944 --> 00:26:46.698
her almost total devotion to him.

00:26:46.784 --> 00:26:49.758
There's never a point where he thinks of Evelyn in such a positive light.

00:26:49.844 --> 00:26:52.606
Despite this, he scolds Jean for the way that she's dressed and gives her

00:26:52.628 --> 00:26:55.866
specific instructions to wear a dress or skirt with high heels simply

00:26:55.898 --> 00:26:58.606
because he likes them and probably because he feels the need to control her

00:26:58.628 --> 00:27:00.478
in some way and knows that she'll comply.

00:27:00.574 --> 00:27:03.886
He spends his time at the office doing everything but working, rearranging

00:27:03.918 --> 00:27:07.598
the decor, listening to music, reading Sports Illustrated and watching TV.

00:27:07.694 --> 00:27:11.074
The question of how Patrick still has a job if he never works is never

00:27:11.112 --> 00:27:12.206
explicitly detailed.

00:27:12.238 --> 00:27:14.982
But at one point, the fact that his family owns some kind of large company

00:27:15.036 --> 00:27:17.654
is brought up, which I took as his father's company, having a good

00:27:17.692 --> 00:27:20.406
relationship with the company that he works at, essentially meaning that his

00:27:20.428 --> 00:27:21.314
job is safe.

00:27:21.362 --> 00:27:24.294
Although he has the means to quit working altogether, he continues to work

00:27:24.332 --> 00:27:25.350
in order to fit in.

00:27:25.420 --> 00:27:28.026
I don't think there is one scene in the book where we actually see a

00:27:28.048 --> 00:27:29.130
character getting work done.

00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:32.106
Apparently, this wasn't originally supposed to be the case when Ellis set

00:27:32.128 --> 00:27:33.260
out to write the book.

00:27:33.790 --> 00:27:38.774
– [Brett Easton Ellis] When I started working on American Psycho, I was actually very interested in

00:27:38.832 --> 00:27:41.918
– [Brett Easton Ellis] Wall Street, and I really wasn't interested in the business aspect of it.

00:27:41.924 --> 00:27:45.774
– [Brett Easton Ellis] And so when I started doing research on the book, I hung out with guys who

00:27:45.892 --> 00:27:49.134
– [Brett Easton Ellis] wworkedorked in firms and on the stock exchange and stuff.

00:27:49.172 --> 00:27:50.746
– [Brett Easton Ellis] I would ask them questions about their jobs.

00:27:50.778 --> 00:27:52.526
– [Brett Easton Ellis] No one really wanted to talk about their jobs.

00:27:52.558 --> 00:27:56.306
– [Brett Easton Ellis] They wanted to talk about clothing, their house in the Hamptons, who could

00:27:56.328 --> 00:27:57.822
– [Brett Easton Ellis] get a better table, which restaurant.

00:27:57.886 --> 00:27:59.310
– [Brett Easton Ellis] It was really about status.

00:27:59.390 --> 00:28:02.978
– [Brett Easton Ellis] And that's when the book began to shift for me and began to take shape.

00:28:02.984 --> 00:28:03.750
As I alluded to before.

00:28:03.820 --> 00:28:05.714
Bateman is an unreliable narrator.

00:28:05.762 --> 00:28:08.966
It isn't always clear how much time passes in between his stories or what

00:28:08.988 --> 00:28:10.006
goes on in between.

00:28:10.108 --> 00:28:12.866
It seems to be a different day when he talks about his health club visit,

00:28:12.898 --> 00:28:16.086
his trip to the video store to rent Body Double because he's turned on by

00:28:16.108 --> 00:28:18.822
the scene of the woman getting killed by the power drill and his next date

00:28:18.876 --> 00:28:19.586
with Courtney.

00:28:19.698 --> 00:28:23.106
Then I believe it's a different day when he gets a shiatsu massage and stops

00:28:23.138 --> 00:28:25.914
at a news end where the vendor alerts him that his nose is bleeding.

00:28:25.962 --> 00:28:28.478
Patrick doesn't make a big deal out of it, but I would speculate that the

00:28:28.484 --> 00:28:30.970
nosebleed is a symptom of his increased cocaine usage.

00:28:31.050 --> 00:28:33.566
The first time we see him do it, it seems like he's just trying to fit in

00:28:33.588 --> 00:28:36.350
with Price, but we would soon see him seeking it out on his own.

00:28:36.420 --> 00:28:39.678
The idea is furthered later that day when Bateman meets actor Tom Cruise in

00:28:39.684 --> 00:28:42.898
the elevator at his building and Tom alerts him to another nosebleed, which

00:28:42.904 --> 00:28:45.022
he casually tries to rectify with a handkerchief.

00:28:45.086 --> 00:28:48.158
During the elevator ride, Bateman sucks up to Tom, telling him how much he's

00:28:48.174 --> 00:28:49.138
a fan of his work.

00:28:49.224 --> 00:28:51.906
Tom acts like he doesn't really want to talk to Bateman, treating him with

00:28:51.928 --> 00:28:54.658
two or three word answers, not too different from the way that Bateman

00:28:54.674 --> 00:28:56.866
treats the coworkers that he doesn't really respect.

00:28:56.978 --> 00:28:59.766
He returns home to find messages on his answering machine from that

00:28:59.788 --> 00:29:02.774
evening's dinner date, Patricia, who wants to change the plans and take

00:29:02.812 --> 00:29:04.166
Patrick to a concert instead.

00:29:04.268 --> 00:29:07.626
He is vehemently against this and claims that he doesn't like live music.

00:29:07.728 --> 00:29:11.050
This has always struck me as strange because he does like recorded music.

00:29:11.120 --> 00:29:14.218
The Huey Lewis scene is easily the most famous scene in the movie, and there

00:29:14.224 --> 00:29:17.434
are no less than three chapters in the book completely dedicated to just

00:29:17.472 --> 00:29:18.746
Patrick reviewing music.

00:29:18.848 --> 00:29:21.502
After pondering this for a while, I don't think there's an obvious reason

00:29:21.556 --> 00:29:23.198
that he feels differently about live music.

00:29:23.284 --> 00:29:26.218
Perhaps he doesn't like the improvisational nature of it, and not knowing

00:29:26.234 --> 00:29:28.654
what to expect gives him a sense of not having control.

00:29:28.772 --> 00:29:31.742
Perhaps being a Wall Street banker, he doesn't have connections in the music

00:29:31.796 --> 00:29:35.346
industry as he does in others, and if he can't get special VIP treatment, he

00:29:35.368 --> 00:29:37.310
doesn't want to be seen in the crowd with the commoners.

00:29:37.390 --> 00:29:40.594
Whatever the case may be, he uses manipulation tactics to get her to change

00:29:40.632 --> 00:29:44.706
her mind, such as suggesting that he'll find someone else to go to Dorsia

00:29:44.738 --> 00:29:45.238
with him.

00:29:45.324 --> 00:29:48.854
Dorsia is the hottest restaurant in the city, and even Bateman has so far

00:29:48.892 --> 00:29:50.630
been unable to get a reservation there.

00:29:50.700 --> 00:29:54.178
Upon hearing this magic word, Patricia changes her tune and agrees to dinner

00:29:54.194 --> 00:29:56.550
with him, so he tells her to be over at 08:00 P.m..

00:29:56.620 --> 00:29:59.638
After getting off the phone, he hurriedly calls Dorsia to see if he can get

00:29:59.644 --> 00:30:02.546
a table, but is unable to get anything but a busy signal for several minutes.

00:30:02.578 --> 00:30:05.566
This process gives him an adrenaline rush, which he refers to as

00:30:05.588 --> 00:30:06.858
the rarest of occurrences.

00:30:06.954 --> 00:30:09.758
We've only seen him experience this kind of emotion a couple of times

00:30:09.844 --> 00:30:13.002
comparing business cards and now trying to get a table at Dorsia.

00:30:13.066 --> 00:30:16.238
When it comes to committing crimes or talking to women, he's cool as a

00:30:16.244 --> 00:30:17.390
Manhattan's winter night.

00:30:17.460 --> 00:30:20.474
He even sends his bloody articles to a dry cleaner with no reservations

00:30:20.522 --> 00:30:21.370
about getting caught.

00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:24.914
But put him in a situation where his social status is on the line and watch

00:30:24.952 --> 00:30:26.706
the sweat beads begin to form.

00:30:26.808 --> 00:30:29.778
When he finally gets through and requests a table for two, the voice on the

00:30:29.784 --> 00:30:30.962
other line laughs at him.

00:30:31.016 --> 00:30:34.582
Bateman has finally found a privilege that money can't buy, and while most

00:30:34.636 --> 00:30:37.474
others might be turned off by the rude treatment from the restaurant worker,

00:30:37.522 --> 00:30:40.614
this only fuels his desire to get into Dorsia in the future.

00:30:40.732 --> 00:30:44.214
For the night, he gets reservations at Barcadia, which at other times is

00:30:44.252 --> 00:30:45.650
referred to as Arcadia.

00:30:45.730 --> 00:30:48.806
Actually, it's not really clear if these are two separate restaurants or if

00:30:48.828 --> 00:30:50.722
this is another example of a mental slip.

00:30:50.786 --> 00:30:52.998
Bateman accidentally misidentifying the name of it half the time.

00:30:53.004 --> 00:30:56.026
While waiting for his date to arrive, he makes a decision

00:30:56.058 --> 00:30:56.938
about her fate.

00:30:57.034 --> 00:31:00.606
I come to the conclusion that Patricia is safe tonight and I am not going to

00:31:00.628 --> 00:31:04.094
unexpectedly pull out a knife and use it on her just for the sake of doing

00:31:04.132 --> 00:31:07.594
so that I am not going to get any pleasure watching her bleed from slits

00:31:07.642 --> 00:31:11.058
that I've made by cutting her throat or slicing her neck open or gouging her

00:31:11.064 --> 00:31:11.650
eyes out.

00:31:11.720 --> 00:31:14.702
She's lucky, even though there's no real reasoning behind the luck.

00:31:14.766 --> 00:31:16.862
It could be that she's safe because of her wealth.

00:31:16.926 --> 00:31:18.782
Her family's wealth protects her tonight.

00:31:18.846 --> 00:31:21.326
Or it could be that it's simply my choice.

00:31:21.438 --> 00:31:24.438
He waits until they're in the cab to break the news to Patricia, making up a

00:31:24.444 --> 00:31:27.222
story to explain how the reservation at Dorsia got messed up.

00:31:27.276 --> 00:31:30.482
Upon hearing this, she turns away and pouts for the entire evening.

00:31:30.546 --> 00:31:32.802
The situation doesn't seem to be lost on Bateman.

00:31:32.866 --> 00:31:36.226
She was only using him to go to the hottest destination possible and for

00:31:36.268 --> 00:31:40.438
sex, and he was only using her as a good looking accessory and for sex.

00:31:40.534 --> 00:31:43.418
However, he decides he no longer wants to sleep with her because he is

00:31:43.424 --> 00:31:46.506
rubbed the wrong way by her behavior and decides to get revenge on her by

00:31:46.528 --> 00:31:49.606
taking her to the nightclub Tunnel after dinner and ignoring her there.

00:31:49.648 --> 00:31:52.638
He goes straight to the bar and gets his favorite drink, J and B on the rocks.

00:31:52.644 --> 00:31:56.446
J and B is a brand of scotch, and back then they advertised it as

00:31:56.468 --> 00:31:59.278
being a high class beverage, so it makes sense that Bateman would want to

00:31:59.284 --> 00:32:00.494
associate himself with it.

00:32:00.532 --> 00:32:02.942
I guess today people see it as more of a cheap brand.

00:32:03.006 --> 00:32:06.030
But at the bar, he realizes that the entire club is empty.

00:32:06.110 --> 00:32:09.730
We are, except for the occasional hard body, literally the only two people

00:32:09.800 --> 00:32:13.554
in Tunnel New Sensation becomes The Devil Inside, and the music is full

00:32:13.592 --> 00:32:17.094
blast, but it feels less loud because there isn't a crowd reacting to it.

00:32:17.132 --> 00:32:19.330
And the dance floor looks vast when empty.

00:32:19.410 --> 00:32:22.006
These are the same two songs that played the first time we saw Bateman at

00:32:22.028 --> 00:32:24.642
this club, right before he and Price went to purchase the coke.

00:32:24.706 --> 00:32:27.318
This makes me wonder if the empty club is merely one of Bateman's hallucinations. 

00:32:27.334 --> 00:32:31.606
In particular, the song The Devil Inside by INXS brings

00:32:31.638 --> 00:32:35.098
to mind a later hallucination of his involving the devil, which we'll get to

00:32:35.184 --> 00:32:36.214
the name of the band, INXS

00:32:36.262 --> 00:32:39.738
also feels significant because the pronunciation is a phonetic

00:32:39.754 --> 00:32:42.986
play on the words In Excess and pertaining to the point I've been driving

00:32:43.018 --> 00:32:44.030
home throughout this video.

00:32:44.100 --> 00:32:47.850
Bateman is a man pushed to psychopathy by living an excessive lifestyle.

00:32:47.930 --> 00:32:50.814
He attempts to mimic what Timothy Price would do by wandering off and

00:32:50.852 --> 00:32:53.630
finding Ricardo, the man that sold them the cocaine in the basement.

00:32:53.710 --> 00:32:56.818
Ricardo is with a woman who compliments him on his wallet, and he tells her

00:32:56.824 --> 00:33:00.034
that he'd like to do unmentionable things to her and maybe cut her arms off.

00:33:00.072 --> 00:33:03.446
But again, the loud music seems to muffle his voice, but we can see that

00:33:03.468 --> 00:33:06.002
he's becoming more comfortable with the off color remarks.

00:33:06.066 --> 00:33:09.814
Again, this takes after Price's behavior, but Bateman takes it too far, and

00:33:09.852 --> 00:33:13.094
I'm guessing he takes the cocaine usage too far as well, because this is

00:33:13.132 --> 00:33:14.530
where things really get weird.

00:33:14.610 --> 00:33:17.446
After getting them drinks, Patricia comes back and goes on a long winded

00:33:17.478 --> 00:33:19.402
apology for her behavior earlier in the night.

00:33:19.456 --> 00:33:22.550
But Bateman basically tunes her out and starts thinking about expensive

00:33:22.630 --> 00:33:26.070
material things he wants in an almost robotic, methodical fashion.

00:33:26.150 --> 00:33:29.694
Patricia is never mentioned again, so it's unclear if he simply dumped her

00:33:29.732 --> 00:33:32.154
or changed his mind about her being safe for the evening.

00:33:32.202 --> 00:33:35.354
The next scene features Bateman taking bloody articles to the dry cleaners.

00:33:35.402 --> 00:33:37.146
So I'll let you be the judge.

00:33:37.258 --> 00:33:40.174
His usual dry cleaners had sent his stuff back last time, still bloodstained.

00:33:40.212 --> 00:33:42.798
So this time he goes in person to complain.

00:33:42.894 --> 00:33:46.082
He's unable to communicate with the Chinese speaking woman who works there.

00:33:46.136 --> 00:33:49.074
In his frustration, he threatens to kill her, and it's unclear if she

00:33:49.112 --> 00:33:49.842
understands him.

00:33:49.896 --> 00:33:52.102
He daydreams about firebombing the whole place.

00:33:52.236 --> 00:33:54.210
I am not condoning such an action.

00:33:54.290 --> 00:33:57.698
This is just an educational character analysis documentary.

00:33:57.794 --> 00:34:00.754
When another customer walks in, he quickly tries to recompose himself,

00:34:00.802 --> 00:34:03.506
demonstrating that he's still attempting to keep up the appearance of a sane

00:34:03.538 --> 00:34:04.370
member of society.

00:34:04.450 --> 00:34:06.742
It turns out to be a woman from his building, Victoria.

00:34:06.806 --> 00:34:08.214
He mistakes her for a Samantha.

00:34:08.262 --> 00:34:11.658
At first, the misidentification motif goes both ways, it seems.

00:34:11.744 --> 00:34:14.922
He asks her if she can try to talk to the screeching Chinese woman behind

00:34:14.976 --> 00:34:17.914
the counter, explaining that he got cranapple juice on his sheets and needs

00:34:17.952 --> 00:34:18.994
the stains removed.

00:34:19.062 --> 00:34:22.126
He doesn't seem concerned about his weak excuse for why his sheets are

00:34:22.148 --> 00:34:22.750
stained red.

00:34:22.820 --> 00:34:26.234
I think he's so cocky about his high place up in society that he genuinely

00:34:26.282 --> 00:34:29.646
doesn't believe that he could ever be suspected or caught, and society kind

00:34:29.668 --> 00:34:30.698
of proves him right.

00:34:30.804 --> 00:34:33.438
There are other similar moments in the movie, like the one where he's

00:34:33.454 --> 00:34:38.126
loading a body into the trunk of a cab and the only reaction he gets is

00:34:38.318 --> 00:34:42.066
-[Guy on Street] OOH, where did you get that overnight bag? 

00:34:42.088 --> 00:34:45.046
As he's leaving, Samantha tries to set up a date with him the following week, but he makes

00:34:45.068 --> 00:34:47.250
several excuses as to why he's busy because she's a little overweight.

00:34:47.330 --> 00:34:50.406
That afternoon, Bateman's increasing frequency of hallucinations are

00:34:50.428 --> 00:34:53.558
confirmed when he mistakes a college student for a homeless girl and puts a

00:34:53.564 --> 00:34:54.870
dollar into her coffee cup.

00:34:54.950 --> 00:34:58.342
Then in the cab, he says he hallucinated the whole city into mountains,

00:34:58.406 --> 00:34:59.622
volcanoes and jungles.

00:34:59.686 --> 00:35:03.014
He describes his lunch appointment as a permanent hallucination involving

00:35:03.062 --> 00:35:06.766
waking dreams, although he never tries to diagnose what's going on with

00:35:06.788 --> 00:35:10.330
himself. This is almost certainly an example of cocaine induced psychosis.

00:35:10.410 --> 00:35:15.406
This is the process of cocaine use, altering chemicals in the brain with

00:35:15.428 --> 00:35:18.894
symptoms such as seeing, hearing or feeling things that are not there and

00:35:18.932 --> 00:35:20.150
inappropriate behavior.

00:35:20.250 --> 00:35:23.854
The increasingly severe psychosis may be reflected in the next Patty Winter Show

00:35:23.902 --> 00:35:26.446
topic real life Rambos, which Bateman sees.

00:35:26.478 --> 00:35:29.406
In the morning before having drinks at Harry's with Todd Hamlin and George Reeves. 

00:35:29.438 --> 00:35:32.934
Paul Owens stops by their table and confuses Bateman for another

00:35:32.972 --> 00:35:36.886
associate, Marcus Halberstam, whose job, fashion sense and hairdo are all

00:35:36.908 --> 00:35:39.602
very similar to Patrick's, making it an understandable mistake.

00:35:39.666 --> 00:35:42.290
He gets Owen's card, and the two plan to have drinks sometime.

00:35:42.370 --> 00:35:45.878
As Owen leaves, one of his friends looks back and seems to recognize Bateman

00:35:45.894 --> 00:35:48.870
with a panicked look, which in turn makes Bateman uneasy.

00:35:48.950 --> 00:35:51.962
One evening, Courtney invites Bateman out to dinner with another couple

00:35:52.016 --> 00:35:54.986
because her boyfriend, Luis Carruthers is out of town on business.

00:35:55.088 --> 00:35:58.202
Before going out, he has Jean study the menu and prepare three different

00:35:58.256 --> 00:35:59.470
possible orders for him.

00:35:59.540 --> 00:36:01.838
In the cab, he thinks he sees Donald Trump's car.

00:36:01.924 --> 00:36:05.246
Not only is this another example of mistaken identity, but it's also one of

00:36:05.268 --> 00:36:07.918
the many examples of Bateman idolizing Donald Trump.

00:36:08.004 --> 00:36:11.586
Keep in mind, this was the 1980s, and at the time, Trump was a young real

00:36:11.608 --> 00:36:14.946
estate developer who basically achieved celebrity status due to his life of

00:36:14.968 --> 00:36:15.966
wealth and excess.

00:36:16.078 --> 00:36:19.618
He was basically the ultimate yuppie, the very thing that Bateman aspired to be.

00:36:19.624 --> 00:36:23.266
Apparently, in his later years, Trump shifted gears to become some kind

00:36:23.288 --> 00:36:24.358
of politics guy.

00:36:24.444 --> 00:36:26.070
This is still a politics free channel.

00:36:26.140 --> 00:36:29.206
I just felt it necessary to remind you that Bateman's idol at the time was

00:36:29.228 --> 00:36:32.102
known for different reasons, because this reverence does come up again later. 

00:36:32.156 --> 00:36:35.106
The restaurant that they go to is called Deck Chairs, which serves

00:36:35.138 --> 00:36:38.454
Post California cuisine, and the couple that they dine with are named 

00:36:38.502 --> 00:36:39.510
Scott and Anne Smiley.

00:36:39.590 --> 00:36:42.246
Also, for the first time in a while, we're given a clue about the dates

00:36:42.278 --> 00:36:45.226
because Scott mentions that he just picked up the new Phil Collins album and

00:36:45.248 --> 00:36:47.530
the song Groovy Kind of Love sounds really good.

00:36:47.600 --> 00:36:50.734
Groovy Kind of Love is on Phil Collins Buster soundtrack, which came out

00:36:50.772 --> 00:36:52.462
September 19, 1988.

00:36:52.516 --> 00:36:55.322
And Bateman does mention that it's a Monday, so the date is Monday,

00:36:55.386 --> 00:36:57.438
September 19, 1988.

00:36:57.524 --> 00:37:00.170
Scott and Anne are both graduates of Camden University.

00:37:00.250 --> 00:37:03.506
Camden is the setting of Brett Easton Ellis's previous novel, The Rules of Attraction

00:37:03.528 --> 00:37:06.802
and if you've read that novel, this scene probably felt very familiar.

00:37:06.856 --> 00:37:09.794
It contained a scene where Patrick's brother Sean and his

00:37:09.832 --> 00:37:13.014
girlfriend Lauren go out for post California cuisine with the very same

00:37:13.052 --> 00:37:14.434
couple, Anne and Scott's.

00:37:14.482 --> 00:37:16.550
The entire interaction is exactly the same.

00:37:16.620 --> 00:37:18.882
It starts with Anne deciding not to drink alcohol.

00:37:18.946 --> 00:37:21.874
Then a Bateman brother talks with Scott about his new CD player.

00:37:21.922 --> 00:37:25.102
Then Scott talks about how he just bought a new Phil Collins CD.

00:37:25.186 --> 00:37:28.666
These similarities are far too specific to be considered a coincidence, and

00:37:28.688 --> 00:37:31.626
given Patrick's psychosis, I have to believe that this whole evening is

00:37:31.648 --> 00:37:32.950
marred in hallucination.

00:37:33.030 --> 00:37:36.246
It seems that Sean told him his story at some point about his awkward double

00:37:36.278 --> 00:37:39.726
date with the Smileys and Patrick during a wild and powdery evening with

00:37:39.748 --> 00:37:43.454
Courtney somehow convinced his overly medicated brain that this was his own

00:37:43.492 --> 00:37:46.986
experience. His tendency to mix stories he has heard into his own narrative

00:37:47.018 --> 00:37:49.930
is an important clue for helping us decipher future events.

00:37:50.010 --> 00:37:53.202
We've already seen him start to struggle with distinguishing reality, like

00:37:53.256 --> 00:37:56.386
during this dinner when he tells Anne, did anyone ever tell you that you

00:37:56.408 --> 00:37:59.758
look exactly like Garfield, but run over and skinned, and then someone threw

00:37:59.774 --> 00:38:03.126
an ugly Ferragamo sweater over you before they rushed you to the vet? 

00:38:03.148 --> 00:38:05.558
Which is definitely going to become my new rejection line.

00:38:05.644 --> 00:38:06.226
Just kidding.

00:38:06.258 --> 00:38:09.474
But this line does show that Patrick has definitely stopped differentiating

00:38:09.522 --> 00:38:12.594
between things that actually happened and things that he only fantasized about.

00:38:12.642 --> 00:38:15.986
We can see that much by the fact that Anne doesn't react to his rude

00:38:16.018 --> 00:38:19.578
comment. Meanwhile, Courtney is nearly falling asleep at the table due to

00:38:19.584 --> 00:38:22.682
some drugs that she's taken, and Bateman is not enjoying the evening at all.

00:38:22.736 --> 00:38:25.834
He thinks to himself that no female intimacy is worth sitting through this

00:38:25.872 --> 00:38:27.414
dinner. And his mind begins to wander.

00:38:27.462 --> 00:38:30.606
To chopping up Anne and Scott, the videotapes he forgot to return and the

00:38:30.628 --> 00:38:33.662
adult video that he watched recently, which he can't get his mind off of,

00:38:33.716 --> 00:38:36.718
like the drugs he's been taking, the adult content is starting to become an

00:38:36.724 --> 00:38:39.534
addiction, which we can see by the fact that he starts thinking about it at

00:38:39.572 --> 00:38:40.782
inappropriate times.

00:38:40.916 --> 00:38:43.918
At some point, Courtney disappears to use the bathroom, and Patrick finds

00:38:43.934 --> 00:38:46.146
her asleep in the coatcheck room 30 minutes later.

00:38:46.248 --> 00:38:49.538
This is where the best move is to make sure she gets home safely and let her

00:38:49.544 --> 00:38:51.218
beg for your forgiveness the next day.

00:38:51.304 --> 00:38:54.886
But that's not what happens, because Bateman is becoming more depraved and

00:38:54.908 --> 00:38:57.234
more deviant by the day during their encounter, later that night,

00:38:57.282 --> 00:39:00.966
he hears her say, Luis is a despicable twit, but this

00:39:00.988 --> 00:39:02.690
was partially just his imagination.

00:39:02.770 --> 00:39:06.186
She actually says, is it a receptacle tip? I think she meant, is it a

00:39:06.208 --> 00:39:09.350
reservoir tip? But as we know, Courtney isn't the smartest.

00:39:09.430 --> 00:39:12.474
They get into a huge argument about the method of contraception, something

00:39:12.512 --> 00:39:15.198
Courtney is concerned about only because it affects how she looks in a

00:39:15.204 --> 00:39:16.810
bikini and she starts crying.

00:39:16.890 --> 00:39:19.806
Patrick and Courtney's ability to be intimate with each other would soon be

00:39:19.828 --> 00:39:20.526
cut short.

00:39:20.628 --> 00:39:23.470
Luis Carruthers was coming home from Phoenix.
(IMPACT)
♪ Mysterious Music ♪

00:39:32.610 --> 00:39:36.078
Shortly after Luis returns from his business trip in Phoenix, and Bateman

00:39:36.094 --> 00:39:38.862
shows up to work hungover after another debaucherous evening.

00:39:38.926 --> 00:39:42.078
The Patty Winter's topic that morning was descendants of the Donner party.

00:39:42.094 --> 00:39:45.186
And if Bateman really is hallucinating Patty Winter's topics, it's a grim

00:39:45.218 --> 00:39:46.630
foreshadowing of things to come.

00:39:46.700 --> 00:39:49.478
Because the Donner party was a group of settlers who got snowbound in the

00:39:49.484 --> 00:39:54.018
mountains and had to resort to cannibalism.

00:39:54.124 --> 00:39:56.374
– [Danny Torrance] You mean they ate each other up.
They had to in order to survive.

00:39:56.502 --> 00:40:00.106
During the meeting, McDermott, still salty about Bateman insulting his red

00:40:00.128 --> 00:40:03.642
snapper pizza at Pastels, brings in a Xeroxed article about Donald Trump

00:40:03.696 --> 00:40:06.790
where Trump claims that Pastels has the best pizza in Manhattan.

00:40:06.870 --> 00:40:09.994
Seeing this actually causes Bateman to admit how he'll have to try the pizza again now.

00:40:10.042 --> 00:40:13.166
I think this exchange demonstrates more than any other that

00:40:13.188 --> 00:40:15.422
Bateman truly has no personality of his own.

00:40:15.476 --> 00:40:18.622
Everything is dictated by what's hip and something as subjective as personal

00:40:18.676 --> 00:40:19.902
taste is treated as fact.

00:40:19.956 --> 00:40:21.598
So long as the rich and trendy agree.

00:40:21.684 --> 00:40:24.722
Bateman continues to go through his routine, and the violent thoughts become

00:40:24.776 --> 00:40:25.742
more and more frequent.

00:40:25.806 --> 00:40:28.802
He thinks about taking a hammer and ice pick to a girl at the video store

00:40:28.856 --> 00:40:31.986
and tells him masseuse that he'd like to transfuse a hard body's blood with

00:40:32.008 --> 00:40:32.594
dog blood.

00:40:32.642 --> 00:40:36.326
The Patty winter segments get more bizarre with segments like UFOs that kill.

00:40:36.348 --> 00:40:39.762
He also spends some time with Marcus Halberstan's girlfriend.

00:40:39.826 --> 00:40:42.914
Perhaps he's trying to learn more about Marcus in order to better emulate him.

00:40:42.962 --> 00:40:46.114
His next supposed victims are two black kids he saw on the subway station.

00:40:46.162 --> 00:40:49.274
And he also takes credit for killing Evelyn's neighbor, whose head

00:40:49.312 --> 00:40:52.186
he sawed off with an electric mini chainsaw before storing it in his freezer. 

00:40:52.208 --> 00:40:55.674
This one is more likely to be real because Evelyn calls one night

00:40:55.712 --> 00:40:59.050
and admits she's shaken up by it and her building is surrounded by police.

00:40:59.130 --> 00:41:03.534
One afternoon, Patrick does some early Christmas shopping for himself, and

00:41:03.572 --> 00:41:06.746
everything he buys is some form of blade, a pair of scissors, a letter

00:41:06.778 --> 00:41:07.962
opener, and a cheese knife.

00:41:08.026 --> 00:41:11.522
While there's probably no limit to how early a psychopath does their early

00:41:11.576 --> 00:41:15.214
Christmas shopping for themselves, I'm going to call this late November.

00:41:15.342 --> 00:41:18.494
That evening, he has a date planned with Courtney when his actual girlfriend

00:41:18.542 --> 00:41:21.506
Evelyn calls, still shaken by her neighbor's death and demands to have

00:41:21.528 --> 00:41:22.210
dinner with him.

00:41:22.280 --> 00:41:25.526
During their meal at Barcadia, he fantasizes about seeing Evelyn with

00:41:25.548 --> 00:41:26.162
another woman.

00:41:26.226 --> 00:41:27.922
He doesn't bother to cancel with Courtney.

00:41:27.986 --> 00:41:30.786
The rumors about their affair have put a strain on Courtney and Evelyn's

00:41:30.818 --> 00:41:32.838
relationship, but Bateman doesn't care.

00:41:32.924 --> 00:41:36.154
The conversation meanders from rumors about where Tim Price has been to

00:41:36.192 --> 00:41:39.734
Evelyn mistaking another diner for Ivana Trump, which only infuriates Patrick.

00:41:39.782 --> 00:41:43.146
Evelyn starts gushing about weddings, claiming she won't settle for

00:41:43.168 --> 00:41:44.726
less than a diamond engagement ring.

00:41:44.758 --> 00:41:47.386
And Patrick says he'd want to blow her fat mother's head off at the ceremony.

00:41:47.408 --> 00:41:50.702
Obviously, he didn't really say that out loud, because again,

00:41:50.756 --> 00:41:51.770
there's no reaction.

00:41:51.850 --> 00:41:54.942
As the conversation continues, it seems that they are not connecting at all.

00:41:54.996 --> 00:41:58.366
Either Patrick is lying about all of his lines, or Evelyn isn't listening to

00:41:58.388 --> 00:41:59.326
a word he says.

00:41:59.428 --> 00:42:01.806
In reality, I imagine it's a little bit of both.

00:42:01.908 --> 00:42:04.602
On a Tuesday, Patrick is attending a black tie party.

00:42:04.666 --> 00:42:07.902
An exclusive and luxurious event like this seems like something that early

00:42:07.956 --> 00:42:11.046
Bateman would be very interested in, but he appears to have grown bored with

00:42:11.068 --> 00:42:13.602
this kind of thing and spends the whole party looking for drugs.

00:42:13.666 --> 00:42:16.598
Even running into Courtney doesn't seem to spark his interest as much, and

00:42:16.604 --> 00:42:19.538
he blows her off by telling her he has to go return some videotapes.

00:42:19.554 --> 00:42:22.706
As she tries to warn him to stay away from Luis, who apparently suspects

00:42:22.738 --> 00:42:25.786
something between the two. He ends up walking the streets, where he starts a

00:42:25.808 --> 00:42:27.418
conversation with a bum named Al.

00:42:27.504 --> 00:42:31.226
Bateman gets his attention by offering money and food, but then just ends up

00:42:31.248 --> 00:42:32.058
scolding him.

00:42:32.144 --> 00:42:34.406
– [Patrick Bateman]Why don't you get a job? You're so hungry. Why don't you get a job?

00:42:34.438 --> 00:42:36.926
– [Al] I lost my job.

00:42:37.028 --> 00:42:44.426
– [Patrick Bateman] Why? You drinking? Is that why you lost it? Insider trading? Just joking.

00:42:44.538 --> 00:42:48.126
His immediate assumption that the man lost his job for alcohol abuse is

00:42:48.148 --> 00:42:51.826
either extremely ironic or some form of self conscious projection, because

00:42:51.848 --> 00:42:55.074
Bateman has completely lost control of his life due to substance abuse and

00:42:55.112 --> 00:42:56.078
his other addictions.

00:42:56.174 --> 00:42:59.474
After offering to help the guy out, he suddenly becomes very angry because

00:42:59.512 --> 00:43:02.786
of the man's stench, calls him a loser and stabs him in the eye, causing it

00:43:02.808 --> 00:43:03.538
to burst open.

00:43:03.624 --> 00:43:06.706
He continues to go at the guy with a knife, blinding him but being careful

00:43:06.738 --> 00:43:07.698
to keep him alive.

00:43:07.794 --> 00:43:10.614
However, at this point, I have to question the validity of anything that

00:43:10.652 --> 00:43:11.830
Patrick says he does.

00:43:11.900 --> 00:43:15.074
And there's very good reason to believe that some or most of this horrible

00:43:15.122 --> 00:43:17.098
encounter with Al never even happened.

00:43:17.184 --> 00:43:18.890
But again, we'll get to that.

00:43:18.960 --> 00:43:21.142
He decides to go to McDonald's to get a milkshake.

00:43:21.206 --> 00:43:23.946
Normally, he wouldn't be caught dead at McDonald's, but he sees it as

00:43:23.968 --> 00:43:25.578
somewhere that Al would probably go.

00:43:25.664 --> 00:43:29.226
He is still supposedly covered in red splatters of crime scene evidence, but

00:43:29.248 --> 00:43:30.750
that doesn't cause him to back down.

00:43:30.820 --> 00:43:33.018
He's just that cocky about not getting caught.

00:43:33.114 --> 00:43:36.442
The high from what he did to Al begins to wear off, and he finds himself

00:43:36.506 --> 00:43:37.438
still wanting more.

00:43:37.524 --> 00:43:39.610
What we're seeing is the behavior of an addict.

00:43:39.690 --> 00:43:40.586
With any addiction.

00:43:40.618 --> 00:43:43.938
It begins when the user engages in a behavior that sends dopamine to the

00:43:43.944 --> 00:43:45.262
reward circuit in the brain.

00:43:45.326 --> 00:43:48.514
As the behavior continues, the brain adapts to it, which reduces the high

00:43:48.552 --> 00:43:49.794
that the person feels from it.

00:43:49.832 --> 00:43:51.470
This effect is known as tolerance.

00:43:51.550 --> 00:43:54.646
At this stage, the person may try to increase usage to get the same high

00:43:54.668 --> 00:43:55.554
that they once felt.

00:43:55.602 --> 00:43:58.534
And these adaptations in the brain usually cause the person to gain less

00:43:58.572 --> 00:43:59.766
pleasure from other things.

00:43:59.868 --> 00:44:03.602
The National Institute of Health uses examples like food, adult activities

00:44:03.666 --> 00:44:04.914
and social interactions.

00:44:04.962 --> 00:44:07.622
And these examples actually perfectly apply to Bateman.

00:44:07.686 --> 00:44:11.766
He used to enjoy dining out and extravagant $300 meals, but now he's bored

00:44:11.798 --> 00:44:13.670
by them and finds himself at McDonald's.

00:44:13.750 --> 00:44:17.578
We see him turn down a social function in order to satisfy his blood lust.

00:44:17.674 --> 00:44:21.006
Normally, one addiction is enough to wreck havoc on a person's life, but for

00:44:21.028 --> 00:44:26.762
Bateman, there are several addictions substances sadism spending and adult

00:44:26.826 --> 00:44:28.398
activities, just to name a few.

00:44:28.484 --> 00:44:31.346
And by the way, the only reason Bateman is getting a dopamine rush from

00:44:31.368 --> 00:44:34.830
hurting people is because there is something seriously wrong with his brain.

00:44:34.910 --> 00:44:37.042
So don't try it.

00:44:37.096 --> 00:44:38.450
It will not make you feel good.

00:44:38.520 --> 00:44:41.986
If you want to feel a nice charge, try going to the gym or subscribing to

00:44:42.008 --> 00:44:44.214
CZsWorld and leaving a like on this video.

00:44:44.332 --> 00:44:47.666
Now, I mentioned how a person may increase their usage to try to overcome

00:44:47.698 --> 00:44:51.186
desensitization. And in Bateman's case, he's able to do this by combining

00:44:51.218 --> 00:44:53.238
his addictions for a longer dopamine hit.

00:44:53.324 --> 00:44:56.234
But he's still able to maintain the appearance of having some level of

00:44:56.272 --> 00:44:59.734
control over his life, perhaps because everyone in his circles is so shallow

00:44:59.782 --> 00:45:02.854
that they're only concerned with material things and unable to recognize

00:45:02.902 --> 00:45:03.718
his symptoms.

00:45:03.814 --> 00:45:06.874
I don't think there's one scene in the entire novel where he and his friends

00:45:06.912 --> 00:45:10.286
or he and his girlfriend have an actual conversation about each other,

00:45:10.308 --> 00:45:12.366
Such as how they're feeling or how they're doing.

00:45:12.468 --> 00:45:15.466
Case in point on another day, Bateman is having lunch with Christopher Armstrong,

00:45:15.498 --> 00:45:18.606
who works with him at Pierce and Pierce, and Armstrong doesn't even

00:45:18.628 --> 00:45:21.302
know who he is, mistaking him for someone named Taylor.

00:45:21.386 --> 00:45:23.970
This must be the most egregious mistaken identity yet.

00:45:24.040 --> 00:45:26.622
The two went to school together, and they're now out at lunch.

00:45:26.686 --> 00:45:29.806
Armstrong has just returned from a vacation in the Bahamas, and when Bateman

00:45:29.838 --> 00:45:33.118
asks how it was, he just goes into this almost prepared sounding pitch.

00:45:33.214 --> 00:45:36.598
Armstrong has nothing to say about anything he or his family did in the

00:45:36.604 --> 00:45:39.862
Bahamas. It's more like he's reciting a paragraph directly out of a travel guide.

00:45:39.916 --> 00:45:43.506
 It's almost exactly the same detached, passive voice that Bateman

00:45:43.538 --> 00:45:46.134
uses when talking about the things that he supposedly likes.

00:45:46.182 --> 00:45:49.258
There are three chapters in American Psycho where Bateman simply reviews the

00:45:49.264 --> 00:45:51.462
discography of one of his favorite recording artists.

00:45:51.526 --> 00:45:54.726
The subjects are genesis, Whitney Houston, and, of course, Huey Lewis and the News.

00:45:54.758 --> 00:45:59.534
– [Patrick Bateman] When Sports came out in 83, I think they really came into their

00:45:59.572 --> 00:46:01.562
– [Patrick Bateman] own commercially and artistically.

00:46:01.706 --> 00:46:04.830
– [Huey Lewis] Yeah, I actually think he's correct there.

00:46:04.900 --> 00:46:08.126
Bateman's so called opinions sound almost as if they're ripped directly out

00:46:08.148 --> 00:46:09.626
of a music magazine's critique.

00:46:09.658 --> 00:46:12.546
And they're clearly written in language that is not congruent with how he

00:46:12.568 --> 00:46:15.710
speaks in conversation or even in his interior monologue.

00:46:15.790 --> 00:46:18.530
I guess if you want to work at Pierce and Pierce, you have to be completely

00:46:18.600 --> 00:46:19.822
devoid of personality.

00:46:19.886 --> 00:46:23.006
The Patty Winter Show that day covers Toddler Tragedies, where the parents

00:46:23.038 --> 00:46:26.326
of children who are kidnapped and oft too soon are analyzed by a panel of

00:46:26.348 --> 00:46:28.786
psychiatrists. That one's got to be a hallucination.

00:46:28.818 --> 00:46:31.430
It's way too dark to ever be considered for this type of show.

00:46:31.500 --> 00:46:34.758
It seems to be an indication that Bateman is not mentally stable, which we

00:46:34.764 --> 00:46:38.262
see shortly after when he mistakes a gay pride parade for a Halloween parade.

00:46:38.326 --> 00:46:39.366
On the way to Wall Street,

00:46:39.398 --> 00:46:42.826
this morning, due to gridlock, I had to get out of the company car and was

00:46:42.848 --> 00:46:46.058
walking down Fifth Avenue to find a subway station when I passed what I

00:46:46.064 --> 00:46:49.386
thought was a Halloween parade, which was disorienting since I was fairly

00:46:49.418 --> 00:46:50.574
sure this was May.

00:46:50.692 --> 00:46:54.030
Since I've been keeping track of the dates, I can tell you it most certainly

00:46:54.100 --> 00:46:54.958
is not May.

00:46:55.044 --> 00:46:58.094
As we've established, it's definitely past September at this point, and

00:46:58.132 --> 00:46:59.338
possibly into December.

00:46:59.434 --> 00:47:02.674
Since when is anybody only fairly sure of what month it is? I might be

00:47:02.712 --> 00:47:05.826
fairly sure of the day of week sometimes, but I've never been unsure where

00:47:05.848 --> 00:47:06.690
we are in the year.

00:47:06.760 --> 00:47:07.918
That's ridiculous.

00:47:08.014 --> 00:47:11.506
I think this is another example of Bateman's psychosis, the losing track of

00:47:11.528 --> 00:47:13.022
time that I alluded to earlier.

00:47:13.086 --> 00:47:15.986
He's losing his grip on reality, and this makes him an increasingly

00:47:16.018 --> 00:47:19.638
unreliable narrator sure you could just as well argue that any of the

00:47:19.644 --> 00:47:22.562
previous mentions of the date are just as likely to be mental slips.

00:47:22.626 --> 00:47:26.018
But I believe that this is the mental slip, because as time goes on, he's

00:47:26.034 --> 00:47:27.990
getting more and more detached from reality.

00:47:28.070 --> 00:47:31.718
Plus, there is a Christmas party a few chapters later. Anyway, the cat calls

00:47:31.734 --> 00:47:35.114
from the men discussed him so much that he goes home, changes suits, gives

00:47:35.152 --> 00:47:37.430
himself a pedicure, and tortures a dog to death.

00:47:37.510 --> 00:47:40.846
All of these are coping strategies, but like I said, it's getting harder and

00:47:40.868 --> 00:47:43.758
harder for him to cope, because he's becoming more and more used to the

00:47:43.764 --> 00:47:46.634
things that give his brain dopamine when putting on the expensive clothes

00:47:46.682 --> 00:47:48.974
he's purchased no longer has the same effect it once did.

00:47:49.012 --> 00:47:51.322
He's got to move on to more extreme solutions.

00:47:51.386 --> 00:47:54.466
We see further evidence of him getting bored with his extravagant life on

00:47:54.488 --> 00:47:57.394
another evening when he cancels his dinner reservations with Evelyn because

00:47:57.432 --> 00:48:00.222
the thought of sitting alone with her for that long fills him with nameless dread.

00:48:00.286 --> 00:48:04.246
He seems to be in almost a depressive state, but that changes when

00:48:04.268 --> 00:48:06.914
he's invited to a U2 concert by Luis Carruthers.

00:48:06.962 --> 00:48:10.726
Under normal circumstances, since he's not a fan of Luis and not a fan of

00:48:10.748 --> 00:48:14.022
live music, he would probably just say no.

00:48:14.156 --> 00:48:17.654
However, I think he agrees to go because Luis is also bringing Paul Owen,

00:48:17.702 --> 00:48:20.474
who of course, is handling the Fisher account, and that's something that

00:48:20.512 --> 00:48:21.258
interests him.

00:48:21.344 --> 00:48:24.394
There are probably only a few things that keep Bateman going at this point

00:48:24.432 --> 00:48:27.834
in the story the things that money is not able to buy him, the prestigious

00:48:27.882 --> 00:48:31.210
Fisher account and the equally distinguished reservation at Dorsia.

00:48:31.290 --> 00:48:34.554
During the outing, Owen continues to mistake Bateman for Marcus Halberstem,

00:48:34.602 --> 00:48:37.514
and he even thinks that Evelyn is Marcus's girlfriend, Cecilia.

00:48:37.562 --> 00:48:40.846
Evelyn doesn't notice because she's too busy glaring at Courtney, who she is

00:48:40.868 --> 00:48:42.622
suspicious of having an affair with Patrick.

00:48:42.686 --> 00:48:45.874
Patrick seems to be keeping up this identity ruse for a reason, and sure

00:48:45.912 --> 00:48:47.854
enough, he would use the mix up to his advantage later.

00:48:47.902 --> 00:48:51.518
Despite having front row seats, nobody is interested in the concert.

00:48:51.614 --> 00:48:55.586
That is, until Bateman sees the backdrop turn red, giving him a huge surge

00:48:55.618 --> 00:48:56.162
of feeling.

00:48:56.226 --> 00:48:59.506
He thinks he's receiving a message directly from the front man, Bono.

00:48:59.618 --> 00:49:03.186
The music slows down, and the rest of the audience and band members fade away.

00:49:03.218 --> 00:49:09.114
Bono tells him, I am the devil, and I am just like you.

00:49:09.232 --> 00:49:12.166
As you may recall, this is probably not the first time one of these bizarre

00:49:12.198 --> 00:49:14.102
hallucinations makes reference to the devil.

00:49:14.166 --> 00:49:17.526
At this point, I think we can confidently say that Bateman is psycho.

00:49:17.638 --> 00:49:18.726
American Psycho!

00:49:18.758 --> 00:49:21.274
There's also another interesting parallels with The Rules of Attraction here,

00:49:21.322 --> 00:49:24.458
because at one point, Sean Bateman also sees the devil while he's tripping. 

00:49:24.474 --> 00:49:27.738
After this, Patrick comes back down to reality and decides it's

00:49:27.754 --> 00:49:29.918
finally time to ask Paul about that Fisher account.

00:49:30.004 --> 00:49:30.718
If you think about it.

00:49:30.724 --> 00:49:34.050
It's funny that he wants the Fisher account so badly, yet he never actually works. 

00:49:34.120 --> 00:49:37.330
It's just another status symbol, like everything in his life.

00:49:37.400 --> 00:49:40.054
Owen explains that he didn't even do anything special to get it.

00:49:40.092 --> 00:49:41.554
It just kind of fell into his lap.

00:49:41.602 --> 00:49:45.046
The next time we see Bateman, he's in the throes of a major anxiety attack.

00:49:45.148 --> 00:49:47.110
One Thursday afternoon.
(Impact)

00:49:55.770 --> 00:49:58.010
Bateman finds himself running around New York.

00:49:58.080 --> 00:50:00.342
He can't remember where he had lunch or with whom.

00:50:00.406 --> 00:50:03.466
He calls Jean, his secretary, desperate for help, and when he tells her he

00:50:03.488 --> 00:50:06.342
won't make it to the office, she shows genuine sadness and concern.

00:50:06.406 --> 00:50:09.578
She may be the only one who ever shows these kinds of genuine emotions.

00:50:09.674 --> 00:50:13.742
He does so much insane shit on this afternoon that I can't even fully list

00:50:13.796 --> 00:50:15.550
everything, so I'll just mention a few.

00:50:15.620 --> 00:50:18.718
He shoplifts a can of ham and eats it directly out of the container in the

00:50:18.724 --> 00:50:19.470
lobby of a building.

00:50:19.540 --> 00:50:23.154
He obsessively states, I've got to return my videotapes over and over, and

00:50:23.192 --> 00:50:26.974
he buys five vials of drugs and eats them whole before ranting to the dealer

00:50:27.022 --> 00:50:28.590
about BMW engines.

00:50:28.670 --> 00:50:32.578
Then, just as quickly as this insanity began, it ends, and Bateman finds

00:50:32.594 --> 00:50:35.282
himself at the Yale Club with McDermott and Van Patton.

00:50:35.346 --> 00:50:38.322
They talk fashion, the gym, massages, the usual.

00:50:38.386 --> 00:50:42.018
He also sees Luis Carruthers there and has an idea to kill him, wondering

00:50:42.034 --> 00:50:45.314
if he'd be able to spend more time with Courtney if he did so or if Courtney

00:50:45.362 --> 00:50:48.050
only likes him for the thrill of going behind Luis back.

00:50:48.130 --> 00:50:50.834
He decides to go through with it and follows him into the bathroom.

00:50:50.882 --> 00:50:54.282
But when he puts his gloved hands around Luis' neck to choke him,

00:50:54.346 --> 00:50:56.478
Luis kisses his wrist and touches his face.

00:50:56.564 --> 00:50:59.566
Bateman is so shocked that he can't bring himself to choke him.

00:50:59.588 --> 00:51:03.262
And Luis, a closeted homosexual, has been pining for Bateman for so long

00:51:03.316 --> 00:51:06.434
that he's unable to interpret this as anything other than Bateman coming on

00:51:06.472 --> 00:51:06.994
to him.

00:51:07.112 --> 00:51:09.410
–[Luis] God. Patrick.

00:51:09.990 --> 00:51:12.900
–[Luis] Why here? I've seen you looking at me.

00:51:13.830 --> 00:51:18.094
–[Luis] I've noticed your hot body.

00:51:18.222 --> 00:51:22.066
Unsure of how to respond, Bateman washes his hand, the gloves still on, and

00:51:22.088 --> 00:51:22.930
he seething.

00:51:23.000 --> 00:51:26.086
Luis asks him where he's going, and he tells him he's got to return some videotapes. 

00:51:26.108 --> 00:51:29.606
Whenever he experiences this kind of discomfort, he's got to

00:51:29.628 --> 00:51:32.722
blow off the steam and improve his mood by releasing some dopamine.

00:51:32.786 --> 00:51:35.986
He works out three times in one day, makes some obscene and threatening

00:51:36.018 --> 00:51:39.366
telephone calls to fuel his arousal, and walks around town with a briefcase

00:51:39.398 --> 00:51:40.582
full of knives and guns.

00:51:40.646 --> 00:51:43.686
He decides not to kill the first two targets he comes across on the basis

00:51:43.718 --> 00:51:44.618
that they're too easy.

00:51:44.704 --> 00:51:47.770
His addiction is forcing him to search for a bigger and bigger rush.

00:51:47.850 --> 00:51:51.082
Finally, he comes across a gay man walking an expensive shar pei. 

00:51:51.146 --> 00:51:54.506
The man flirts with Patrick, who exchanges pleasantries until he randomly

00:51:54.538 --> 00:51:57.022
slices the dog's stomach open and disembowels him.

00:51:57.076 --> 00:52:00.198
He also stabs the owner and shoots him with the silencer to make sure he's

00:52:00.234 --> 00:52:00.830
really dead.

00:52:00.910 --> 00:52:04.194
I'm a little skeptical that this really happened, given that this takes

00:52:04.232 --> 00:52:06.318
place openly in the streets of Manhattan.

00:52:06.414 --> 00:52:09.714
This is still not enough to satiate him, however, so he goes to the store

00:52:09.752 --> 00:52:13.058
and buys some cereal with an expired coupon, which apparently gives him a thrill.  

00:52:13.064 --> 00:52:15.570
His crimes are becoming more and more bizarre.

00:52:15.650 --> 00:52:19.202
He ends this rampage by running up and down Broadway with an open umbrella,

00:52:19.266 --> 00:52:20.482
screaming like a banshee.

00:52:20.546 --> 00:52:23.474
I wish they would have put that in the movie on some other evening.

00:52:23.522 --> 00:52:27.098
His craving for all things degenerate reaches an all time high after a

00:52:27.104 --> 00:52:29.654
dinner with Courtney that he refers to as infuriating.

00:52:29.702 --> 00:52:32.826
He drops her off at Nell's nightclub and tells her he'll return once he goes

00:52:32.848 --> 00:52:33.878
and gets some drugs.

00:52:33.974 --> 00:52:35.946
This does not end up being the case.

00:52:36.048 --> 00:52:39.066
He has his limo driver take him to the Meatpacking District so he can look

00:52:39.088 --> 00:52:42.046
for a blonde girl who seems suitable and bribes her to come back to his

00:52:42.068 --> 00:52:45.674
place. By the way, he seems to have no problem with his limo driver bearing

00:52:45.722 --> 00:52:48.842
witness to his illegal activities, and this is never addressed.

00:52:48.906 --> 00:52:51.374
Perhaps he's been given a generous tip to keep his mouth shut.

00:52:51.422 --> 00:52:53.266
Or maybe none of this even happens.

00:52:53.368 --> 00:52:56.802
This is one of many examples of Bateman's disregard of low paid service

00:52:56.856 --> 00:52:57.710
industry workers.

00:52:57.790 --> 00:53:01.022
Back at his apartment, he orders the girl to respond to the name Christie,

00:53:01.086 --> 00:53:04.434
then calls an escort service, and soon after, another woman, Sabrina,

00:53:04.482 --> 00:53:05.590
arrives to join them.

00:53:05.660 --> 00:53:08.358
He sits her down and gives her an expensive wine as they listen to the

00:53:08.364 --> 00:53:11.442
Broadway cast recording of Les Misérables on the stereo.

00:53:11.506 --> 00:53:15.206
Les Misérables comes up many times throughout American Psycho, 20 times to be exact.

00:53:15.228 --> 00:53:19.066
It was a popular Broadway show at the time, and it too, deals with a

00:53:19.088 --> 00:53:21.914
character who learns of the special privileges afforded to the rich by society.

00:53:21.952 --> 00:53:24.230
Eventually, things move to the bedroom.

00:53:24.310 --> 00:53:27.934
Round one is innocuous enough, but after a short rest, Bateman comes back

00:53:27.972 --> 00:53:31.978
for round two, which involves accessories such as a nail gun, sharpened coat

00:53:31.994 --> 00:53:34.458
hanger, butter knife, matches and a cigar.

00:53:34.554 --> 00:53:38.206
The next day, Sabrina leaves with a limp and Christie with scratches and a

00:53:38.228 --> 00:53:38.842
black eye.

00:53:38.906 --> 00:53:41.986
As Christmas draws closer, Bateman spends most of his time going into

00:53:42.008 --> 00:53:45.394
cocktail parties and Christmas shopping, a pleasurable activity for him

00:53:45.432 --> 00:53:48.626
because it's another opportunity to gain favor with others by showing off

00:53:48.648 --> 00:53:49.342
his fortune.

00:53:49.406 --> 00:53:52.658
He opts to do the shopping himself rather than letting Jean do it because of

00:53:52.664 --> 00:53:54.514
the rush he gets by making the purchases.

00:53:54.642 --> 00:53:57.718
Instead, he has Jean send out holiday cards and do some research on the

00:53:57.724 --> 00:53:58.710
Fisher account for him.

00:53:58.780 --> 00:54:01.686
He also mentions that he's been taking the sleeping pill Halcion in

00:54:01.708 --> 00:54:04.786
increasing amounts during the day, because even though his body has adapted

00:54:04.818 --> 00:54:07.962
to it and it no longer makes him sleep, it does seem to ward off total madness.

00:54:08.016 --> 00:54:12.026
Just not quite enough to prevent the idea of taking an escort to

00:54:12.048 --> 00:54:14.250
Evelyn's Christmas party from crossing his head.

00:54:14.320 --> 00:54:16.682
Thankfully for her, he does not end up doing that.

00:54:16.736 --> 00:54:20.330
The day of the party, he gets drinks at Rusty's in order to fortify himself,

00:54:20.410 --> 00:54:22.010
as if it's going to be that unbearable.

00:54:22.090 --> 00:54:25.438
After this, he jumps a Japanese delivery boy on a bike and gives him a

00:54:25.444 --> 00:54:29.438
surprise Christmas gift that is a slit to the throat, and this is kind of

00:54:29.444 --> 00:54:30.910
where I lose sympathy for him.

00:54:30.980 --> 00:54:34.414
Taking out motorists is one thing, but targeting an innocent boy on a bike?

00:54:34.462 --> 00:54:35.422
That's just despicable.

00:54:35.486 --> 00:54:38.526
He opens up the food boxes that were being delivered and he finds Chinese

00:54:38.558 --> 00:54:43.318
food, not Japanese, and feels not remorse but irritation because he got the

00:54:43.324 --> 00:54:45.240
wrong type of Asian in his mind.

00:54:47.530 --> 00:54:50.438
He looks at the receipt and makes a note to get revenge on the person who

00:54:50.444 --> 00:54:53.974
had ordered the food, as if this was somehow her fault, then shrugs and

00:54:54.012 --> 00:54:56.598
apologizes to the delivery boy before walking away.

00:54:56.684 --> 00:54:59.194
I don't think it's going to do much good now, though.

00:54:59.312 --> 00:55:02.266
At the Christmas party that evening, he starts to get nervous because he

00:55:02.288 --> 00:55:05.734
sees too much red and is also off put by the short people dressed as elves

00:55:05.782 --> 00:55:08.902
serving appetizers and questions whether or not he is hallucinating them.

00:55:08.976 --> 00:55:09.722
This is telling.

00:55:09.786 --> 00:55:12.558
There are probably a lot of hallucinations that he doesn't mention to the

00:55:12.564 --> 00:55:15.454
reader, considering he's at a point where he's now questioning if real

00:55:15.492 --> 00:55:17.150
things are hallucinations or not.

00:55:17.220 --> 00:55:20.346
He only realizes that they're real when Evelyn mentions that she has names

00:55:20.378 --> 00:55:23.566
for all the elves, such as Rudolph, Blitzen and Donner.

00:55:23.598 --> 00:55:24.866
Patrick corrects her.

00:55:24.968 --> 00:55:27.390
I... those are the names of reindeer, not elves.

00:55:27.470 --> 00:55:29.522
Blitzen was a reindeer, he tells her.

00:55:29.576 --> 00:55:31.634
The only Jewish one, Peterson reminds them.

00:55:31.672 --> 00:55:35.026
I think Peterson is talking about blintzes, which are a traditional Jewish

00:55:35.058 --> 00:55:36.466
recipe similar to crepes.

00:55:36.498 --> 00:55:37.826
It's not important to my analysis.

00:55:37.858 --> 00:55:39.538
I just thought it was an underrated joke.

00:55:39.634 --> 00:55:42.806
He tells Evelyn to be grateful that he came to her Christmas party when

00:55:42.828 --> 00:55:44.674
there are so many others that he could have attended.

00:55:44.722 --> 00:55:47.254
Imagine skipping out on your own girlfriend's Christmas party.

00:55:47.302 --> 00:55:50.406
The affair with Courtney is one thing, but that's just asking for trouble.

00:55:50.518 --> 00:55:53.786
Speaking of whom, Bateman sees Courtney talking to Paul Owen across the room.

00:55:53.808 --> 00:55:56.822
He has the impression that she's flirting with Paul, which honestly

00:55:56.886 --> 00:55:59.822
wouldn't be surprising given her shallowness and attraction to social status.

00:55:59.876 --> 00:56:03.566
However, she moves on, and Bateman uses the opportunity to get

00:56:03.588 --> 00:56:06.238
closer to the man who is handling the coveted Fisher account.

00:56:06.324 --> 00:56:09.438
In talking to Owen, he learns that there's a limo waiting for him outside to

00:56:09.444 --> 00:56:12.270
take Paul and his girlfriend Meredith to Nell's after the party.

00:56:12.350 --> 00:56:15.406
Paul still thinks that Bateman is Marcus Halberstam, and when Evelyn

00:56:15.438 --> 00:56:17.886
approaches them, he's under the impression that she's Halberstam's

00:56:17.918 --> 00:56:18.942
girlfriend Cecilia.

00:56:19.006 --> 00:56:22.854
So just to review, Owen is at Evelyn's Christmas party, yet he still

00:56:22.892 --> 00:56:25.282
believes that Evelyn is actually Cecilia.

00:56:25.346 --> 00:56:28.662
I don't even think I'm going to attempt to explain how this is possible.

00:56:28.796 --> 00:56:32.086
When Evelyn starts to call him Patrick, he creates a big spectacle of

00:56:32.108 --> 00:56:35.282
calling her Cecilia, then kisses her and pulls her into the kitchen.

00:56:35.346 --> 00:56:37.494
He's clearly invested in keeping up the ruse.

00:56:37.542 --> 00:56:40.982
He doesn't want Paul to find out that he's not really Marcus Halberstam.

00:56:41.046 --> 00:56:43.866
It seems that he's already planning on using Paul's confusion to his

00:56:43.888 --> 00:56:46.870
advantage in the future, though it's not clear if he's already decided

00:56:46.950 --> 00:56:48.298
exactly what he's going to do.

00:56:48.384 --> 00:56:51.502
To further sell the illusion, he asks Evelyn to leave the party with them.

00:56:51.556 --> 00:56:54.554
He's worried about her possibly tipping Paul off, or the real

00:56:54.602 --> 00:56:56.938
Marcus Halberstam showing up and shattering the mirage.

00:56:57.034 --> 00:57:00.142
Evelyn doesn't want to leave her own party, but he wins her over by saying

00:57:00.196 --> 00:57:03.618
that leaving would be daring, which she likes the idea of, and he's able to

00:57:03.624 --> 00:57:05.486
play to her desires by calling her

00:57:05.518 --> 00:57:07.390
Mrs. Bateman. And finally she agrees.

00:57:07.470 --> 00:57:10.606
Outside, he shoves her into Paul Owens limo and tries to convince the driver

00:57:10.638 --> 00:57:12.414
that Paul said he could take it for the evening.

00:57:12.462 --> 00:57:14.982
The driver doesn't believe him and tells him that this would break company protocol. 

00:57:15.036 --> 00:57:18.454
Patrick considers trying to take another limo, but because he

00:57:18.492 --> 00:57:22.294
specifically wants Paul Owens, he bribes the man with $200, which is enough

00:57:22.332 --> 00:57:25.074
to get him to comply and drive them to Club Chernobyl.

00:57:25.122 --> 00:57:28.054
Once again, we see the lack of chemistry between Patrick and Evelyn.

00:57:28.102 --> 00:57:30.982
She's acting almost just as crazy and entitled as her boyfriend.

00:57:31.046 --> 00:57:33.866
She finds a present in the limo, a necklace, which Paul had left for

00:57:33.888 --> 00:57:35.462
Meredith, and assumes that it's hers.

00:57:35.526 --> 00:57:38.426
She takes a fortune cookie out of Patrick's pocket and assumes it's some

00:57:38.448 --> 00:57:41.406
kind of clue that Patrick will be taking her to lunch at that restaurant in

00:57:41.428 --> 00:57:41.924
the future.

00:57:41.924 --> 00:57:45.166
She thinks she's in a game and everything is some kind of hint, despite the

00:57:45.188 --> 00:57:48.458
obvious fact that Patrick doesn't really care for her at all and only keeps

00:57:48.474 --> 00:57:50.158
her around as a hot trophy girl.

00:57:50.244 --> 00:57:53.226
When she realizes that they're headed to Club Chernobyl, she pouts and tries

00:57:53.258 --> 00:57:56.126
to get the driver to change course to the Rainbow Room, which Patrick does

00:57:56.148 --> 00:57:58.506
not want to do because he knows he won't be able to score drugs.

00:57:58.538 --> 00:58:00.770
Then there this is textbook addict behavior.

00:58:00.850 --> 00:58:03.734
He's always thinking ahead to where he can get his next fix.

00:58:03.852 --> 00:58:06.934
When they arrive at Chernobyl, the doorman lets them in immediately because

00:58:06.972 --> 00:58:08.402
he saw that they got out of a limo.

00:58:08.466 --> 00:58:11.462
There's probably no better moment to illustrate how wealth and special

00:58:11.516 --> 00:58:13.606
privileges are tied to each other in late eighties

00:58:13.628 --> 00:58:14.246
New York.

00:58:14.348 --> 00:58:17.546
Once inside, he sends Evelyn to go get some champagne at the bar so he can

00:58:17.568 --> 00:58:18.454
go look for a dealer.

00:58:18.502 --> 00:58:21.366
After obtaining some powder, he comes across a hardbody and considers

00:58:21.398 --> 00:58:24.646
ditching Evelyn for her, showing that all of the effort and begging required

00:58:24.678 --> 00:58:27.242
to get her out here was never about spending time with her.

00:58:27.296 --> 00:58:30.254
He just wanted her to stay away from the party, and moreover, he probably

00:58:30.292 --> 00:58:31.710
liked being able to control her.

00:58:31.780 --> 00:58:34.766
However, she comes back whining that they should leave because they have the

00:58:34.788 --> 00:58:36.170
wrong brand of champagne.

00:58:36.250 --> 00:58:39.694
In many ways, I think Evelyn would be just as crazy as Patrick in terms of

00:58:39.732 --> 00:58:43.726
psychotic consumerism, if not for the whole Patrick being a serial killer thing.

00:58:43.758 --> 00:58:47.182
Looking for a private spot, he drags her to the unisex bathroom,

00:58:47.246 --> 00:58:50.066
where he gets into it with another couple over whose turn it is to take the

00:58:50.088 --> 00:58:51.198
next available stall.

00:58:51.294 --> 00:58:53.890
The other couple is in there for the same reason that Patrick is.

00:58:53.960 --> 00:58:57.094
Patrick insults the girl and calls her a bitch, to which her boyfriend just

00:58:57.132 --> 00:58:58.822
laughs and refuses to defend her.

00:58:58.876 --> 00:59:01.586
This starts a fight between the woman and her boyfriend, and that causes

00:59:01.618 --> 00:59:04.338
Evelyn to get mad at Patrick for starting it, and she tells him that she'll

00:59:04.354 --> 00:59:05.174
just wait outside.

00:59:05.292 --> 00:59:08.178
Patrick doesn't really care about Evelyn being there anymore, and he orders

00:59:08.194 --> 00:59:09.798
her to just take the limousine and leave.

00:59:09.884 --> 00:59:13.206
As he gets down to it, he can hear her sobbing to the other girl about how

00:59:13.228 --> 00:59:15.322
he made her walk out of her own Christmas party.

00:59:15.386 --> 00:59:18.558
When he finishes and walks back out to the club, Evelyn is gone and the

00:59:18.564 --> 00:59:19.530
crowd has changed.

00:59:19.610 --> 00:59:22.650
It's less Wall Street guys and more punk rockers and rappers.

00:59:22.730 --> 00:59:26.142
When he tries hitting on some girls, his social class actually works against

00:59:26.196 --> 00:59:29.610
him. One of them sneers and says, Go back to Wall Street, and the one with

00:59:29.620 --> 00:59:31.662
the nose ring says, F*cking Yuppie.

00:59:31.726 --> 00:59:35.074
He also makes an embarrassing attempt to talk to some black guys by using

00:59:35.112 --> 00:59:38.066
the words freshest and deafest to no avail, Yo!

00:59:38.168 --> 00:59:41.650
For the first time, his normally coveted lifestyle is working against him, 

00:59:41.720 --> 00:59:44.486
and this is meant to show how much further he can fall when his

00:59:44.508 --> 00:59:48.230
dominant position over others is no longer a reliable source of joy for him.

00:59:48.300 --> 00:59:51.122
American Psycho takes place near the end of the Yuppie trend.

00:59:51.186 --> 00:59:54.966
It's currently late 1988, and the Yuppie lifestyle was already starting to

00:59:54.988 --> 00:59:59.306
decline after a stock market crash in 1987 reminded people how volatile the

00:59:59.328 --> 01:00:00.202
economy could be.

01:00:00.256 --> 01:00:03.706
The end was already near, and after a short economic recession began in

01:00:03.728 --> 01:00:07.822
1990, Time magazine would post a mock obituary called

01:00:07.876 --> 01:00:10.366
The Birth and Maybe Death of Yuppie Dumb in April 1991.

01:00:10.468 --> 01:00:14.154
Lucky for Patrick Bateman, he's able to find another hard body dancing alone

01:00:14.202 --> 01:00:15.870
who is more receptive to him.

01:00:15.940 --> 01:00:19.454
That is the last we see of Bateman in 1988, and the next part of his

01:00:19.492 --> 01:00:21.682
timeline caused a lot of confusion for me.

01:00:21.736 --> 01:00:24.546
At the beginning of the next chapter, he claims it's now mid May.

01:00:24.648 --> 01:00:28.354
However, three chapters later, it's Sean's 23rd birthday, which would put us

01:00:28.392 --> 01:00:29.810
in March 1989.

01:00:29.880 --> 01:00:33.346
I considered the possibility that the story is being told out of order, but

01:00:33.368 --> 01:00:36.598
there is clearly a linear sequence of events, plus the structure of the

01:00:36.604 --> 01:00:38.818
novel almost feels like a journal that he's creating.

01:00:38.834 --> 01:00:39.334
in his head.

01:00:39.356 --> 01:00:41.206
He's journaling it all as he goes along.

01:00:41.308 --> 01:00:44.214
I think the more likely explanation is that Bateman's mind is really

01:00:44.252 --> 01:00:45.894
starting to get distorted by the drugs.

01:00:45.942 --> 01:00:48.966
We already established one instance of this when he mistook the Gay Pride Parade

01:00:48.998 --> 01:00:50.374
as a Halloween parade.

01:00:50.422 --> 01:00:53.866
According to the British Psychological Society, the consumption of drugs and

01:00:53.888 --> 01:00:57.858
alcohol has long been known to warp time experiences and many drug users

01:00:57.894 --> 01:01:01.758
support similar experiences following marijuana, cocaine and alcohol use.

01:01:01.844 --> 01:01:05.326
So I'm just going to call this early 1989 and this is supported by what

01:01:05.348 --> 01:01:06.394
happens in this chapter.

01:01:06.442 --> 01:01:09.726
He continues further down the same path he was on, being warped by his

01:01:09.748 --> 01:01:12.010
addictions and corrupting more of his thoughts.

01:01:12.090 --> 01:01:15.406
He wakes up from a dream that he describes as being lit like an adult's film

01:01:15.438 --> 01:01:18.606
where he was with girls made of cardboard, perhaps a symbol of how he sees

01:01:18.638 --> 01:01:19.950
their lives as disposable.

01:01:20.030 --> 01:01:22.622
He also meets with his lawyer about some bogus rape charges.

01:01:22.686 --> 01:01:25.558
And although Bateman is never shy about the crimes he's committed in the

01:01:25.564 --> 01:01:29.206
narration, it's not hard to imagine that these charges aren't so bogus and

01:01:29.228 --> 01:01:32.662
he's simply done so much bad stuff that he can't keep track of it all.

01:01:32.716 --> 01:01:35.766
He also describes an anxiety attack suffered in a grocery store which he

01:01:35.788 --> 01:01:36.766
worked off at the gym.

01:01:36.818 --> 01:01:40.246
His gym time seems to be increasing and ironically, this is the one healthy

01:01:40.278 --> 01:01:42.010
coping mechanism that he seems to have.

01:01:42.080 --> 01:01:45.146
But there's only so much time he can spend at the gym and it's not enough to

01:01:45.168 --> 01:01:47.578
outweigh all of the mental issues that are piling up.

01:01:47.664 --> 01:01:50.606
So he ends up beating a girl, begging for bus money because she had

01:01:50.628 --> 01:01:52.670
misspelled the word disabled on her sign.

01:01:52.740 --> 01:01:56.074
He's living a double life, switching back and forth between despicable

01:01:56.122 --> 01:01:58.634
criminal to high class yuppie in the blink of an eye.

01:01:58.682 --> 01:02:01.458
To this point, he has been able to keep the two lives separate and all of

01:02:01.464 --> 01:02:02.654
his victims are strangers.

01:02:02.702 --> 01:02:06.146
And that would all change, however, after a chance encounter with someone

01:02:06.248 --> 01:02:06.962
from his past. 
(Impact)
♪ 80s Music ♪

01:02:07.016 --> 01:02:19.756
Bateman spends the day with some female models, which ends in a dinner at Nells.

01:02:19.778 --> 01:02:22.424
Where they're joined by McDermott and another associate.

01:02:22.472 --> 01:02:24.424
The models are mind numbingly dumb.

01:02:24.472 --> 01:02:28.968
They can't even name a single planet making guesses like the moon or comet.

01:02:29.064 --> 01:02:30.956
He ends up going home with this girl, Daisy.

01:02:30.988 --> 01:02:34.204
But before getting in the cab, he runs into his ex girlfriend from Harvard,

01:02:34.252 --> 01:02:36.540
Bethany, and she invites him to get lunch sometime.

01:02:36.620 --> 01:02:40.464
Since Bateman always has his mind on his next fix, he interprets this as

01:02:40.502 --> 01:02:41.248
something more.

01:02:41.334 --> 01:02:44.164
When he gets back home with Daisy that night, she admits that her

01:02:44.202 --> 01:02:47.156
ex-boyfriend had spotted them and couldn't understand why she was hanging out

01:02:47.178 --> 01:02:47.776
with a yuppie.

01:02:47.808 --> 01:02:51.348
Another example of the glamour of the yuppie lifestyle starting to wear off.

01:02:51.434 --> 01:02:53.896
After they hook up, he tells her she should leave.

01:02:54.078 --> 01:02:58.504
I think if you stay, something bad will happen.

01:02:58.702 --> 01:03:02.052
This doesn't line up with anything we've come to expect from his character.

01:03:02.116 --> 01:03:04.776
There have always been certain women who have been off limits for him

01:03:04.798 --> 01:03:07.548
because they're part of his social circle, but this is the first time that

01:03:07.554 --> 01:03:09.244
we've seen him show pity for someone.

01:03:09.362 --> 01:03:12.476
Of course, Daisy doesn't know he's a serial killer and assumes he's talking

01:03:12.498 --> 01:03:14.824
about the two of them getting too emotionally involved.

01:03:14.872 --> 01:03:18.380
So she leaves, and for a short time, Bateman resumes with normal activities,

01:03:18.460 --> 01:03:22.092
like taking in a Mets game, renting videotapes, and purchasing luxurious items.

01:03:22.156 --> 01:03:25.984
That is, until the day that would change everything his dinner with

01:03:26.022 --> 01:03:26.780
Paul Owen.

01:03:26.860 --> 01:03:29.676
At some point, he rents an abandoned loft building in the Hell's Kitchen

01:03:29.708 --> 01:03:32.464
neighborhood of Manhattan in preparation for his meeting with Paul.

01:03:32.512 --> 01:03:35.716
He also lays newspapers down over the floor of his apartment to protect the

01:03:35.738 --> 01:03:36.672
white stained oak.

01:03:36.736 --> 01:03:38.176
Using the name Marcus Halberstam.

01:03:38.208 --> 01:03:40.064
He makes reservations at Texarkana.

01:03:40.112 --> 01:03:43.476
The place is mostly dead, which Paul mocks him for, but this may be the one

01:03:43.498 --> 01:03:47.048
time that the popularity of a restaurant is not what Patrick is looking for.

01:03:47.134 --> 01:03:50.024
Owen is really drunk and out of it, and he doesn't offer anything but

01:03:50.062 --> 01:03:51.928
useless information about the Fisher account.

01:03:52.014 --> 01:03:55.268
He keeps changing the topic back to discussions of material items, which

01:03:55.294 --> 01:03:58.392
Bateman ironically, would do himself under normal circumstances.

01:03:58.456 --> 01:04:01.784
This scene certainly came from one of Brett Easton Ellis's own experiences

01:04:01.832 --> 01:04:04.904
talking to Wall Street guys in preparation of writing American Psycho.

01:04:04.952 --> 01:04:07.996
Halfway through the meal, Patrick switches from beer to soda so he can stay

01:04:08.018 --> 01:04:09.452
sober for what he's about to do.

01:04:09.506 --> 01:04:11.292
But Paul continues to get more drunk.

01:04:11.356 --> 01:04:14.624
So drunk that Patrick is able to convince him to pay the entire check, make

01:04:14.662 --> 01:04:18.236
him admit how dumb he is, and get him back to the apartment, which is staged

01:04:18.268 --> 01:04:19.548
for a gruesome scene.

01:04:19.644 --> 01:04:24.032
He takes some Valium, grabs an axe from the bathroom and puts on a raincoat.

01:04:24.096 --> 01:04:26.464
By this point, Paul is completely inebriated.

01:04:26.512 --> 01:04:29.424
The whole scene highlights how he isn't really much different from Patrick

01:04:29.472 --> 01:04:32.240
himself in terms of personality or lack thereof.

01:04:32.320 --> 01:04:35.256
The main difference is that Paul has something that Patrick doesn't, and for

01:04:35.278 --> 01:04:37.300
this, Bateman is jealous and vengeful.

01:04:37.380 --> 01:04:41.220
Paul's last words demonstrate his shallow taste in music, just like Bateman

01:04:41.300 --> 01:04:45.576
Anyway. I used to hate Iggy Pop, but now that he's so commercial, I like him

01:04:45.598 --> 01:04:47.176
a lot better than...

01:04:47.208 --> 01:04:49.132
The ax hits him in mid sentence, straight in the face.

01:04:49.186 --> 01:04:53.020
It's thick blade chopping sideways into his open mouth, shutting him up.

01:04:53.090 --> 01:04:54.828
The scene in the movie is a little bit different.

01:04:54.914 --> 01:04:58.424
Patrick puts on a Huey Lewis CD and starts to explain their discography

01:04:58.472 --> 01:05:00.204
before chopping his coworker to bits.

01:05:00.252 --> 01:05:03.696
This is a combination of three parts from the book the Paul Owen death that

01:05:03.718 --> 01:05:06.608
I just described, the chapter where Bateman reviews Huey Lewis and the News

01:05:06.614 --> 01:05:09.856
and a line that Bateman has during his facial appointment, which he

01:05:09.878 --> 01:05:12.976
may or may not have actually said about his desire to wear a big yellow

01:05:13.008 --> 01:05:13.968
smiley face mask.

01:05:13.984 --> 01:05:17.476
And then put on Bobby McFerrin's Don't Worry, Be Happy while transfusing a

01:05:17.498 --> 01:05:18.724
girl's blood with a dog.

01:05:18.842 --> 01:05:22.368
Anyway, he takes Paul's keys and heads to his apartment, not realizing he's

01:05:22.384 --> 01:05:25.172
still wearing the spattered raincoat until he's already in the cab.

01:05:25.236 --> 01:05:28.580
He ends up destroying it, the raincoat that is in Paul's fireplace.

01:05:28.660 --> 01:05:31.576
He also changes Paul's answering machine message to say that he's gone to London,

01:05:31.598 --> 01:05:34.616
and since their voices sound similar, Bateman thinks that they'll be

01:05:34.638 --> 01:05:35.924
indistinguishable on the phone.

01:05:35.982 --> 01:05:38.968
Another example of him taking advantage of mistaken identity.

01:05:39.064 --> 01:05:42.556
One of the most often asked questions in both the movie and book is if this

01:05:42.578 --> 01:05:45.848
scene actually happened, because later on, an investigator hears that

01:05:45.864 --> 01:05:49.320
Paul Owen has actually been seen in London, and his lawyer eventually claims that

01:05:49.330 --> 01:05:51.068
he had dinner with Paul in London.

01:05:51.164 --> 01:05:55.004
Perhaps Patrick and Paul did really have the dinner at Texarkana and Paul

01:05:55.052 --> 01:05:58.076
told him that he was planning a London trip and then Patrick hallucinated

01:05:58.108 --> 01:05:58.720
the rest.

01:05:58.870 --> 01:06:02.176
This would not be so different from Patrick hearing the story about Sean's

01:06:02.208 --> 01:06:05.124
awkward double date with Ann and Scott Smiley, but then, through his

01:06:05.162 --> 01:06:07.840
hallucinations, incorporates it into his own narrative.

01:06:07.920 --> 01:06:11.428
So did he really kill Paul? I have my own theory about this, which I'll come

01:06:11.434 --> 01:06:12.992
back to when we get to the next clue.

01:06:13.056 --> 01:06:16.436
later in the story. Bateman packs some suits and travel items from Paul's

01:06:16.468 --> 01:06:19.432
belongings into a briefcase and steals a few things for himself, then leaves.

01:06:19.486 --> 01:06:22.616
Back at his own apartment, he throws Owen in a sleeping bag and

01:06:22.638 --> 01:06:26.132
drags it downstairs into a taxi and over to the loft he had rented.

01:06:26.196 --> 01:06:29.384
He places Owen in the bathtub and pours quick climb over him, a chemical

01:06:29.432 --> 01:06:32.664
compound which was thought to help dry out the subject, break it down faster

01:06:32.712 --> 01:06:33.800
and reduce the smell.

01:06:33.880 --> 01:06:38.252
Paul is later determined to have gone missing on the 24 June 1989, which

01:06:38.306 --> 01:06:41.296
obviously doesn't fit our timeline because we're most likely still in the

01:06:41.318 --> 01:06:42.448
first quarter of that year.

01:06:42.534 --> 01:06:46.044
The cloud of confusion that hangs over this time frame may be intentional

01:06:46.092 --> 01:06:49.072
because it adds to the mystery when the detective eventually comes around

01:06:49.126 --> 01:06:52.404
and none of the dates or accounts of who was with who that night seem to add up.

01:06:52.442 --> 01:06:55.908
Basically, the inconsistencies around this time can be considered a wash.

01:06:55.914 --> 01:06:59.652
Later in the evening, Patrick can't sleep, and Evelyn calls him,

01:06:59.706 --> 01:07:02.464
complaining that he never picked her up for dinner that night, which Patrick

01:07:02.512 --> 01:07:06.024
seems genuinely confused by, and he tells her that they never had plans and

01:07:06.062 --> 01:07:08.292
besides, he had to return some videotapes.

01:07:08.356 --> 01:07:11.432
She invites him to go to dinner the next night instead, which he turns down,

01:07:11.486 --> 01:07:12.808
claiming that he has to work.

01:07:12.894 --> 01:07:15.668
You practically own that damn company, she moans.

01:07:15.764 --> 01:07:18.588
What work? What work do you do? I don't understand.

01:07:18.754 --> 01:07:19.656
Thank you, Evelyn.

01:07:19.688 --> 01:07:21.308
That's what I've been saying this whole time.

01:07:21.394 --> 01:07:24.956
Evelyn also proposes the idea of going away over the summer to Edgartown or

01:07:24.978 --> 01:07:25.592
the Hamptons.

01:07:25.656 --> 01:07:27.144
Both are vacation home destinations

01:07:27.192 --> 01:07:29.304
in the region, which he actually agrees to.

01:07:29.362 --> 01:07:32.556
But before that vacation happens, Patrick finds himself in a Paul Smith

01:07:32.588 --> 01:07:35.520
store to buy a tie for his brother's upcoming birthday when he comes across

01:07:35.590 --> 01:07:38.784
Luis Carruthers, who tries to start a conversation in Luis's head.

01:07:38.822 --> 01:07:41.856
There's still a romantic connection between the two, and he confesses his

01:07:41.878 --> 01:07:42.452
love for it.

01:07:42.506 --> 01:07:45.876
Patrick quickly chooses a tie to buy and exits, but Carruthers catches him

01:07:45.898 --> 01:07:48.260
as he's trying to hail a cab, begging him to just talk.

01:07:48.330 --> 01:07:51.956
So Patrick motions his already open switchblade in Luis's direction, and he

01:07:51.978 --> 01:07:53.392
backs away into the cab.

01:07:53.456 --> 01:07:56.916
The accountant and trustee of Patrick's father both call him up shortly

01:07:56.948 --> 01:08:00.920
before Sean's birthday in March 1989 and tell him that Sean is in New York,

01:08:00.990 --> 01:08:03.976
suggesting that they use this as an opportunity to find out what Sean is

01:08:03.998 --> 01:08:04.888
doing with his life.

01:08:04.974 --> 01:08:07.916
They suggest luring him into a dinner meeting by indicating that their

01:08:07.938 --> 01:08:09.336
mother's health has deteriorated.

01:08:09.448 --> 01:08:12.796
Patrick doesn't think his emotionally numb brother will care much, so they

01:08:12.818 --> 01:08:13.628
devise a plan.

01:08:13.714 --> 01:08:16.156
They'll make it look like they need to discuss the allocation of their

01:08:16.178 --> 01:08:17.000
mother's estate.

01:08:17.080 --> 01:08:19.936
When choosing a restaurant for this meeting, Patrick thinks to himself that

01:08:19.958 --> 01:08:23.072
Dorsia is out of the question because it's booked for at least the next

01:08:23.126 --> 01:08:25.500
month and Sean doesn't deserve to go there anyways.

01:08:25.580 --> 01:08:29.372
So he's astonished when Sean calls him and tells him to meet at Dorsia,

01:08:29.436 --> 01:08:32.080
explaining he had spoken to the owner and gotten the table.

01:08:32.160 --> 01:08:35.712
This is the most embarrassing situation that Patrick could have imagined.

01:08:35.776 --> 01:08:39.264
As the older one with a career and list of accomplishments, he sees himself

01:08:39.312 --> 01:08:40.592
as being above his brother.

01:08:40.656 --> 01:08:43.808
Yet Sean is easily able to get into the restaurant Patrick has been pining

01:08:43.824 --> 01:08:45.160
over for more than a year now.

01:08:45.230 --> 01:08:48.308
Sean shows up half an hour late, and they won't seat him until his brother's

01:08:48.324 --> 01:08:51.860
arrival, which Patrick describes as his worst fear becoming reality.

01:08:51.940 --> 01:08:54.852
Sean continues to get on Patrick's nerves by ordering the most expensive

01:08:54.916 --> 01:08:57.916
combination of items possible, which consists of lobster as both an

01:08:57.938 --> 01:09:01.164
appetizer and entree, knowing full well that Patrick is going to be paying

01:09:01.202 --> 01:09:03.980
the check, then proceeds not to touch any of his food.

01:09:04.050 --> 01:09:06.956
To make matters worse, Patrick mentions that he needs to meet someone at

01:09:06.978 --> 01:09:10.336
Nell's by midnight, and Sean downplays him, claiming that Nell's isn't that

01:09:10.358 --> 01:09:13.024
hip anymore, which causes Patrick to change his story.

01:09:13.142 --> 01:09:16.828
In his insecurity, he makes up a story about going to a Trump party and acts

01:09:16.844 --> 01:09:18.412
like he knows Donald's personally.

01:09:18.476 --> 01:09:21.508
At the end of the meal, he watches Sean's eyes and makes sure he's looked at

01:09:21.514 --> 01:09:23.700
the check before allowing the waitress to take it.

01:09:23.770 --> 01:09:27.584
However, it's really a lose lose situation because Sean gets satisfaction

01:09:27.632 --> 01:09:29.060
over wasting his brother's money.

01:09:29.130 --> 01:09:32.464
If that dinner was bad for Bateman's ego, his next documented appointment

01:09:32.512 --> 01:09:33.920
would be catastrophic.

01:09:34.000 --> 01:09:37.156
He is extremely nervous for his lunch with his old Harvard girlfriend

01:09:37.188 --> 01:09:40.420
Bethany, and he even admits that it's probably because of fear of rejection.

01:09:40.500 --> 01:09:41.844
Based on the hot summer weather.

01:09:41.892 --> 01:09:44.376
This must take place in summer 1989.

01:09:44.478 --> 01:09:47.788
Before the date, he purchases and reads a trendy short story collection in

01:09:47.794 --> 01:09:49.340
case they run out of things to talk about.

01:09:49.410 --> 01:09:52.588
He also writes her a poem, like he did during their relationship but it

01:09:52.594 --> 01:09:55.512
comes out incredibly crude, racist and derogatory.

01:09:55.576 --> 01:09:58.232
He makes her read it out loud at the restaurant which is extremely

01:09:58.296 --> 01:10:00.812
uncomfortable and she has to laugh it off as a joke.

01:10:00.876 --> 01:10:04.172
He completely embarrasses himself for the entire first half of the meal

01:10:04.236 --> 01:10:07.424
shaking and twitching his way through a litany of cringeworthy jokes and

01:10:07.462 --> 01:10:11.332
loud outbursts such as his reaction to the waiter reading the specials you

01:10:11.386 --> 01:10:13.620
have. I think you mean the restaurant has.

01:10:13.690 --> 01:10:14.500
I correct him.

01:10:14.570 --> 01:10:17.140
You don't have sun dried tomatoes? The restaurant does.

01:10:17.210 --> 01:10:20.788
You don't have the poblano chilies? The restaurant does just, you know, clarify. 

01:10:20.874 --> 01:10:25.256
He overreacts to everything Bethany says namely when she tells him

01:10:25.278 --> 01:10:29.256
she's dating the chef co-owner of Dorsia which, as you can imagine, has a

01:10:29.278 --> 01:10:30.856
devastating effect on him.

01:10:30.958 --> 01:10:35.304
Yes, my brain does explode and my stomach bursts open inwardly- a spastic

01:10:35.352 --> 01:10:36.952
acidic, gastric reactions;

01:10:37.016 --> 01:10:41.064
stars and planets, whole galaxies made up entirely of little white chef hats

01:10:41.112 --> 01:10:42.712
race over the film of my vision.

01:10:42.776 --> 01:10:46.076
He gets uncharacteristically jealous and basically berates her for her

01:10:46.098 --> 01:10:48.908
choice of partner which confuses her because she had been under the

01:10:48.914 --> 01:10:51.008
impression that he was friends with the guy in college.

01:10:51.094 --> 01:10:54.224
So perhaps he could have gotten into Dorsia at all of this time which is

01:10:54.262 --> 01:10:56.560
probably even more infuriating for him.

01:10:56.630 --> 01:10:59.596
He switches tactics and tries to convince her that her boyfriend is gay,

01:10:59.628 --> 01:11:00.432
which doesn't work.

01:11:00.486 --> 01:11:03.504
To cap it off, she insists on paying for lunch which is extremely

01:11:03.552 --> 01:11:04.740
emasculating for him.

01:11:04.810 --> 01:11:06.948
After lunch, he invites her back to his place.

01:11:07.034 --> 01:11:10.996
At first she refuses, but because it's 100 degrees Fahrenheit outside the

01:11:11.018 --> 01:11:14.376
air conditioning is broken at her office and primarily because she had too

01:11:14.398 --> 01:11:17.448
many drinks at lunch he is able to convince her to go home with him.

01:11:17.534 --> 01:11:18.644
Big mistake.

01:11:18.772 --> 01:11:21.864
She tells him he's hung his David Onica painting upside down which

01:11:21.902 --> 01:11:24.820
challenges his supposedly superior knowledge of pop culture.

01:11:24.900 --> 01:11:25.928
That's strike one.

01:11:26.014 --> 01:11:28.920
He still can't get over the fact that she's dating Robert Hall.

01:11:29.000 --> 01:11:30.316
That's strike two.

01:11:30.418 --> 01:11:33.768
And three, apparently, he gets his nail gun and when she sees him holding

01:11:33.784 --> 01:11:35.564
it, she screams and makes a run for it.

01:11:35.602 --> 01:11:37.420
But he strikes her with it and knocks her out.

01:11:37.490 --> 01:11:40.876
Then he nails her fingers to some wooden boards in the living room maces her

01:11:40.898 --> 01:11:44.280
eyes and places a coat over her head to drown out some of the screams.

01:11:44.360 --> 01:11:47.428
After covering her hands and nails, he brings out his handicam so he can

01:11:47.434 --> 01:11:50.528
film the rest of the torture which consists of him undressing her cutting

01:11:50.544 --> 01:11:52.224
off her nipple, cutting out her tongue.

01:11:52.272 --> 01:11:55.204
I'm not allowed to say the rest of it, but the point is, this is one of his

01:11:55.242 --> 01:11:56.756
most brutal offerings yet.

01:11:56.858 --> 01:12:00.564
Maybe the worst part is that she doesn't die until what I assume is the next

01:12:00.602 --> 01:12:04.420
day, Thursday, he saws her arm off and apparently loses track of the hand.

01:12:04.490 --> 01:12:07.796
It's a small detail, but if he can lose track of a hand in a sky rise

01:12:07.828 --> 01:12:10.824
apartment you'd have to think it would be pretty easy to lose track of which

01:12:10.862 --> 01:12:12.824
events have and have not really happened.

01:12:12.942 --> 01:12:15.940
Later on, his scrambled brain continues to get much worse.

01:12:16.020 --> 01:12:18.124
He also notes that Bethany's lips are bitten off.

01:12:18.162 --> 01:12:21.176
It's not clear if he did that or if she did it to herself.

01:12:21.288 --> 01:12:24.264
Before long, Bateman's ability to switch back to a functional member of

01:12:24.322 --> 01:12:28.300
society would be tested as he had to attend a high end Wall Street party

01:12:28.380 --> 01:12:29.680
that evening.
(Impact)
♪ Mysterious Music ♪
 

01:12:39.020 --> 01:12:42.356
In the evening, after the debacle with Bethany Bateman attends a party

01:12:42.388 --> 01:12:44.920
hosted by Morgan Stanley, another financial company.

01:12:44.990 --> 01:12:47.756
And he tells a colleague he sees there that he's trying to stay away from

01:12:47.778 --> 01:12:49.960
people and avoid speaking to people entirely.

01:12:50.040 --> 01:12:53.036
Either this is a funny excuse to just not want to talk to this particular

01:12:53.138 --> 01:12:56.556
guy or he has so little self control at this moment that he doesn't even

01:12:56.578 --> 01:12:58.124
trust himself not to kill the dude.

01:12:58.172 --> 01:13:00.112
I just love all of Bateman's excuses, though.

01:13:00.166 --> 01:13:03.248
After the party, he sits in a cab with McDermott and Courtney discussing the

01:13:03.254 --> 01:13:05.680
differences in bottled waters for, like, ten minutes.

01:13:05.750 --> 01:13:07.968
You know, I'm starting to understand why he lost it now.

01:13:07.974 --> 01:13:11.344
In his head, he contemplates how he'll dispose of Bethany's skeleton

01:13:11.392 --> 01:13:14.244
and decides the incinerator down the hall from his apartment is the best

01:13:14.282 --> 01:13:17.856
choice. They stop at an ATM, but Courtney's card doesn't work which Patrick

01:13:17.888 --> 01:13:20.640
is able to recognize as damage caused by cutting cocaine.

01:13:20.720 --> 01:13:23.816
Though he doesn't always talk about it I can only imagine how bad his own

01:13:23.838 --> 01:13:26.884
drug use has gotten because he claims this has happened to him multiple

01:13:26.932 --> 01:13:30.356
times. When he gets home that night, he examines Bethany's body and smashes

01:13:30.388 --> 01:13:33.124
her jaw open with her own arm until her head caves.

01:13:33.252 --> 01:13:36.616
That weekend, a hard body told him that rap is the only thing she listens to.

01:13:36.648 --> 01:13:39.996
So after beating the shit out of her he went to the record store and

01:13:40.018 --> 01:13:42.152
picked up $90 worth of rap CDs.

01:13:42.216 --> 01:13:44.764
The next Monday, he was sitting in his office at 8PM.

01:13:44.802 --> 01:13:48.064
Doing the Crossword and listening to these CDs trying to fathom their

01:13:48.102 --> 01:13:50.384
popularity. I can actually relate to Bateman here.

01:13:50.422 --> 01:13:54.480
I try to fathom the popularity of rap music just about every time I hear it.

01:13:54.550 --> 01:13:58.144
Although he usually just likes anything that's hip and hip hop literally has

01:13:58.182 --> 01:13:58.912
hip in the name.

01:13:58.966 --> 01:14:01.764
I think the reason that he doesn't like rap is because it's not the right
 
1583
01:14:01,802 --&gt; 01:14:02,464
kind of hip.

01:14:02.512 --> 01:14:05.792
The hip that he wants to be associated with consists of expensive suits,

01:14:05.856 --> 01:14:08.304
expensive restaurants and classy bars and clubs.

01:14:08.352 --> 01:14:10.372
The music he likes seems to be high production value-

01:14:10.426 --> 01:14:11.564
Pop and pop rock.

01:14:11.632 --> 01:14:15.672
Rap in the 80s was rapidly gaining popularity but it was still far less

01:14:15.726 --> 01:14:19.540
glamorous and featured themes such as life in the ghetto, being a gangster,

01:14:19.620 --> 01:14:21.460
and even advocating for the poor.

01:14:21.540 --> 01:14:23.476
Definitely not Bateman's scene.

01:14:23.588 --> 01:14:27.116
However, despite him probably being upset about rap, he's relieved to hear

01:14:27.138 --> 01:14:30.620
that Evelyn is in Boston for the week, and his favorite guy, Donald Trump,

01:14:30.690 --> 01:14:33.788
makes an appearance on his favorite show, the Patty Winter Show.

01:14:33.874 --> 01:14:36.232
This does seem like a believable Patty Winter segment.

01:14:36.296 --> 01:14:39.628
However, the next segment of the show is about women who have been tortured.

01:14:39.724 --> 01:14:43.436
That one is probably a hallucination and an indicator that Patrick's

01:14:43.468 --> 01:14:46.416
personal account of events is not too reliable during this part of the

01:14:46.438 --> 01:14:49.956
story. He did have dinner plans with Madison Gray and David Campion that

01:14:49.978 --> 01:14:53.076
evening. But at the last minute, he discovered that Luis Carruthers would

01:14:53.098 --> 01:14:53.860
be joining them.

01:14:53.930 --> 01:14:57.476
So he canceled and decided to ask his secretary Jean out to dinner instead.

01:14:57.578 --> 01:15:01.200
He tells her to choose any restaurant she'd like, and she chooses Dorsia.

01:15:01.280 --> 01:15:04.504
Not wanting to admit that he can't get them in, he calls the restaurant, and

01:15:04.542 --> 01:15:07.816
when the maître d' inevitably tells him that they're booked, he acts as if he

01:15:07.838 --> 01:15:09.204
really did get the reservation.

01:15:09.332 --> 01:15:11.576
You didn't give a name? They know me.

01:15:11.678 --> 01:15:14.616
Patrick is more tense than ever when calling Dorsia.

01:15:14.648 --> 01:15:18.136
He can casually dispose of bodies with relative ease, but getting Dorsia

01:15:18.168 --> 01:15:19.852
reservations will always rattle him.

01:15:19.906 --> 01:15:22.876
When they arrive, he looks over the podium into the reservation book and

01:15:22.898 --> 01:15:25.260
finds the name of a couple with a table at 09:00 p.m..

01:15:25.330 --> 01:15:28.656
His plan works, but even after they're seated, he's so nervous that the

01:15:28.678 --> 01:15:32.016
words in the menu look like hieroglyphics to him, and for good reason.

01:15:32.118 --> 01:15:34.672
Eventually, the couple whose table he stole shows up.

01:15:34.726 --> 01:15:38.304
He thinks to himself that if the couple was less attractive or less well

01:15:38.342 --> 01:15:41.588
dressed, he might have at least been able to bribe his way into keeping the table.

01:15:41.594 --> 01:15:45.396
But since everyone in the whole restaurant looks like models, he has

01:15:45.418 --> 01:15:47.504
no leverage, and he's forced to leave with Jean.

01:15:47.552 --> 01:15:51.392
Bateman is humiliated, but Jean is laughing and skipping down the streets.

01:15:51.456 --> 01:15:54.400
She thinks he's funny and spontaneous, and Patrick is surprised.

01:15:54.480 --> 01:15:57.044
He's not used to going out with women who would appreciate the fun

01:15:57.082 --> 01:15:59.232
experience of trying to steal a dinner reservation.

01:15:59.296 --> 01:16:02.156
The women that he usually goes out with would judge him on his inability to

01:16:02.178 --> 01:16:02.968
get the reservation.

01:16:03.064 --> 01:16:06.776
They end up at Arcadia with an A and are one of only five tables filled there.

01:16:06.808 --> 01:16:10.076
It's not hip, but for Jean, it doesn't really matter, and at the end

01:16:10.098 --> 01:16:11.992
of the night, she invites him up for a drink.

01:16:12.056 --> 01:16:15.816
This is an opportunity for him to satiate his bloodlust, but something stops

01:16:15.848 --> 01:16:18.832
him, and he makes up weird excuses about why he can't go up.

01:16:18.886 --> 01:16:22.028
He wants to go home and watch David Letterman, but not on cable.

01:16:22.124 --> 01:16:25.516
Realizing this doesn't make sense, he reverts to his tried and true excuse

01:16:25.548 --> 01:16:26.544
to get out of anything.

01:16:26.662 --> 01:16:28.972
He's got to go return some videotapes.

01:16:29.036 --> 01:16:31.984
As he says goodbye to Jean, he imagines the scene as a movie.

01:16:32.032 --> 01:16:35.136
I almost hear the swelling of an orchestra, can almost hallucinate.

01:16:35.168 --> 01:16:38.496
The camera panning low around us, fireworks bursting in slow motion overhead. 

01:16:38.528 --> 01:16:42.176
The 70 millimeter image of her lips parting to the subsequent

01:16:42.208 --> 01:16:45.512
murmur of I want you in dolby sound throughout the date.

01:16:45.566 --> 01:16:48.788
There are signs that he really may have feelings for Jean, and maybe that's

01:16:48.804 --> 01:16:51.124
why he wants to protect her by not going upstairs.

01:16:51.172 --> 01:16:53.576
He knows her life could be in danger if he does.

01:16:53.678 --> 01:16:57.004
As the hottest months of summer roll by, he has intense dreams about

01:16:57.042 --> 01:16:59.864
vivisection, the practice of cutting open live subjects.

01:16:59.912 --> 01:17:02.440
Yeah, okay, Bateman, go ahead and blame that one on the heat.

01:17:02.520 --> 01:17:05.944
One morning in August, he's paid a visit at his office by Detective Donald Kimball. 

01:17:05.992 --> 01:17:09.104
In the movie, Kimball is played by Willem Defoe, but in the book,

01:17:09.142 --> 01:17:10.812
Kimball is the same age as Bateman.

01:17:10.876 --> 01:17:12.140
Just use your imagination.

01:17:12.220 --> 01:17:15.596
Throughout the interrogation, he plays it extremely cool, a stark contrast

01:17:15.628 --> 01:17:17.324
to his behavior at lunch with Bethany.

01:17:17.372 --> 01:17:20.656
He even allows Kimball to smoke inside his office, whereas he had loudly

01:17:20.688 --> 01:17:23.328
voiced his disgust for the patrons smoking in the restaurant.

01:17:23.424 --> 01:17:26.496
Kimball has been hired by Meredith Powell to investigate the disappearance

01:17:26.528 --> 01:17:27.312
of Paul Owen.

01:17:27.376 --> 01:17:29.504
Supposedly, Paul had been spotted in London.

01:17:29.552 --> 01:17:32.868
But unsurprisingly, the witness who saw him there may have been confusing

01:17:32.884 --> 01:17:34.136
him with somebody else.

01:17:34.238 --> 01:17:37.544
Even while trying to clear his name, Bateman can't help but make up a few

01:17:37.582 --> 01:17:39.524
lies to tarnish Owen's reputation.

01:17:39.652 --> 01:17:42.952
Then, when asked about the last time he was with Owen, he concocts a story

01:17:43.006 --> 01:17:46.952
about how they went to go see a new musical called Oh, Africa, Brave Africa.

01:17:47.016 --> 01:17:50.504
His lies could easily be disproven, but Bateman believes he's untouchable

01:17:50.552 --> 01:17:51.340
because of his money.

01:17:51.410 --> 01:17:54.796
And he may be right, because none of this ever comes back to bite him, it

01:17:54.818 --> 01:17:57.440
helps that Kimball doesn't actually suspect him in the first place.

01:17:57.510 --> 01:17:59.020
He's just covering his bases.

01:17:59.100 --> 01:18:02.464
He asks where Bateman was the night that Paul disappeared, and he spouts off

01:18:02.502 --> 01:18:04.572
something about a date with a girl named Veronica.

01:18:04.636 --> 01:18:07.980
Kimball realizes this doesn't match up with the info he's already collected.

01:18:08.060 --> 01:18:10.676
Apparently, the night of Paul's disappearance, he was scheduled to have

01:18:10.698 --> 01:18:13.908
dinner with Marcus Halberstam, as we would expect, because Bateman made the

01:18:13.914 --> 01:18:15.700
reservation under Halberstam's name.

01:18:15.770 --> 01:18:19.204
Naturally, the real Marcus Halberstam denied this and said he was with five

01:18:19.242 --> 01:18:22.048
other guys that night, one of which was Bateman himself.

01:18:22.144 --> 01:18:25.188
This whole mistaken identity thing has gotten so out of control that Bateman

01:18:25.204 --> 01:18:27.892
is given an alibi at a dinner he never even attended.

01:18:27.956 --> 01:18:31.396
Or maybe he did attend, and his mind is so out of sorts that he only thinks

01:18:31.428 --> 01:18:32.532
he killed Paul Owen.

01:18:32.596 --> 01:18:36.696
To demonstrate how scrambled his mind is during this very conversation, he's

01:18:36.728 --> 01:18:38.600
thinking about a hundred different things a minute.

01:18:38.680 --> 01:18:41.976
Bateman does offer some gossip, telling him that Meredith is now dating

01:18:42.008 --> 01:18:43.464
someone named Brock Thompson.

01:18:43.512 --> 01:18:44.440
If you're paying attention.

01:18:44.520 --> 01:18:47.488
This is very different from the last time we saw Meredith, which was at the

01:18:47.494 --> 01:18:48.880
Christmas party eight months ago.

01:18:48.950 --> 01:18:50.732
At the time, she was dating Paul Owen.

01:18:50.796 --> 01:18:53.824
I wonder if Patrick and Evelyn stealing their limo and Evelyn taking

01:18:53.862 --> 01:18:56.704
Meredith's Christmas gift contributed to the breakup, seeing as how

01:18:56.742 --> 01:19:00.392
relationships in these circles seem to hinge on access to material goods.

01:19:00.476 --> 01:19:03.764
Apparently, Meredith is looking for Paul not because she is concerned about

01:19:03.802 --> 01:19:06.420
him or cares about him, but because he owes her money.

01:19:06.490 --> 01:19:09.444
If we are to consider the possibility that Bateman did not actually kill

01:19:09.482 --> 01:19:12.708
Owen, this provides some incentive as to why he might be hiding out in

01:19:12.714 --> 01:19:16.024
London. By the way, Meredith seems to be a pretty big psycho in her own

01:19:16.062 --> 01:19:17.592
right, if you're keeping track at home.

01:19:17.646 --> 01:19:18.440
She dates Price.

01:19:18.510 --> 01:19:20.740
Who dumps her? Someone named Richard Cunningham.

01:19:20.820 --> 01:19:21.572
Van Patton.

01:19:21.636 --> 01:19:22.644
Then Paul Owen.

01:19:22.692 --> 01:19:24.420
And finally this Brock Thompson.

01:19:24.500 --> 01:19:28.156
In any event, Kimball doesn't seem to suspect foul play, and ironically, he

01:19:28.178 --> 01:19:29.832
thinks Paul is the one who went nuts.

01:19:29.896 --> 01:19:33.036
After Kimball leaves, Bateman gets his phone checked for wired taps, and he

01:19:33.058 --> 01:19:35.020
actually runs into Meredith and Brock that week.

01:19:35.090 --> 01:19:37.324
She doesn't even bring up Paul Owen once.

01:19:37.442 --> 01:19:40.604
Soon after, he begins an affair with a new girl named Jeanette.

01:19:40.652 --> 01:19:42.384
Jeanette is an interesting case.

01:19:42.502 --> 01:19:45.436
At first I thought she was just a new girl that Patrick was going to prey

01:19:45.468 --> 01:19:48.968
on, but I gradually started to wonder if she was almost more of an alter ego

01:19:49.004 --> 01:19:51.860
or fantasy version of the secretary, Jean, for example.

01:19:51.930 --> 01:19:55.264
When Patrick is being normal, he sees Jean, but when he's having his vile

01:19:55.312 --> 01:19:57.792
fantasies, he imagines himself out with Jeanette.

01:19:57.856 --> 01:20:01.392
It wouldn't be so different from the whole Arcadia versus Arcadia confusion.

01:20:01.456 --> 01:20:04.164
And it also makes me wonder if there's something to the fact that Patrick

01:20:04.212 --> 01:20:06.260
earlier on dated a girl named Patricia.

01:20:06.340 --> 01:20:08.756
There are even some character names that are just slightly changed.

01:20:08.788 --> 01:20:11.652
In the movie adaptation, Paul Owen is Paul Allen.

01:20:11.716 --> 01:20:13.512
Timothy Price is Timothy Bryce.

01:20:13.576 --> 01:20:15.576
Evelyn Richards is Evelyn Williams.

01:20:15.688 --> 01:20:18.152
Jeanette never interacts with any of the other characters.

01:20:18.216 --> 01:20:19.352
With one exception.

01:20:19.416 --> 01:20:22.348
In October, Patrick accidentally double books a date, and Evelyn and

01:20:22.354 --> 01:20:25.324
Jeanette end up at the same restaurant looking for him, and Evelyn gets

01:20:25.362 --> 01:20:28.876
pissed. She knows who Jean is, but they might not have ever met in person.

01:20:28.978 --> 01:20:31.996
So this might be another one of those questions that the author did not

01:20:32.018 --> 01:20:33.272
intend on being answered.

01:20:33.336 --> 01:20:36.052
This is also the part of the book where you have to in almost everything

01:20:36.106 --> 01:20:38.708
that Bateman says, so keep that in mind going forward.

01:20:38.794 --> 01:20:42.000
He's continuing on a steady trajectory towards complete insanity.

01:20:42.080 --> 01:20:45.380
He spends the rest of the summer of 89 doing all the stuff he usually does

01:20:45.450 --> 01:20:46.244
to reflect this.

01:20:46.282 --> 01:20:50.016
The Patty Winter Show in this era consists of repeats his nightly blood lust

01:20:50.048 --> 01:20:53.528
overflows into the daytime until finally he asks Evelyn to go with him on a

01:20:53.534 --> 01:20:54.788
getaway to the Hamptons.

01:20:54.884 --> 01:20:58.216
She has the key to Tim Price's vacation house, for some reason.

01:20:58.318 --> 01:21:00.184
So that's where they stay at first.

01:21:00.222 --> 01:21:02.280
This is actually a pretty normal couple's vacation.

01:21:02.360 --> 01:21:05.548
They did things like tennis, windsurfing and breakfast in bed.

01:21:05.634 --> 01:21:07.032
Then things get weird.

01:21:07.096 --> 01:21:12.216
He bought her a dog, which she named NutriSweet and fed dietetic chocolate truffles.

01:21:12.248 --> 01:21:16.224
Maybe the dog is imaginary because dogs definitely can't eat chocolate.

01:21:16.262 --> 01:21:19.536
As time went on, everything started to feel dull again and

01:21:19.558 --> 01:21:21.248
Patrick's weird behavior would return.

01:21:21.334 --> 01:21:24.908
He found himself roaming beaches, digging up crabs and eating sand.

01:21:25.004 --> 01:21:28.576
One morning he microwaved and ate a jellyfish, then fed the leftovers to the dog.

01:21:28.598 --> 01:21:31.092
Evelyn's old behavior also crept back up.

01:21:31.146 --> 01:21:34.132
She became obsessed with her beauty appointments, traveling back to the city

01:21:34.186 --> 01:21:35.940
more and more often to have them done.

01:21:36.010 --> 01:21:39.616
Bateman longs to kill again, so he takes advantage of her time away and uses

01:21:39.648 --> 01:21:40.944
it to drown NutraSweet.

01:21:40.992 --> 01:21:43.784
She doesn't even seem to remember that she even had a dog when she gets

01:21:43.822 --> 01:21:46.020
back, so it probably never existed.

01:21:46.100 --> 01:21:48.852
But maybe Evelyn is just that self obsessed.

01:21:48.916 --> 01:21:51.956
Bateman knows it's time to leave the Hamptons when he finds himself standing

01:21:51.988 --> 01:21:55.016
over their bed one night with an ice pick gripped in his fist, waiting for

01:21:55.038 --> 01:21:56.156
her to open her eyes.

01:21:56.258 --> 01:21:58.652
Somehow he comes to his senses before she wakes up.

01:21:58.706 --> 01:22:01.996
On September 3, one day before Labor Day that year, they would return to

01:22:02.018 --> 01:22:03.400
Manhattan by helicopter.

01:22:03.480 --> 01:22:06.508
Later that month, Patrick has dinner with a model named Elizabeth and two of

01:22:06.514 --> 01:22:07.068
her friends.

01:22:07.154 --> 01:22:10.048
Afterwards, they took a limousine to Nell's, and he left her there so he

01:22:10.054 --> 01:22:13.040
could go pick up Christy, the blonde corner girl from the previous year.

01:22:13.110 --> 01:22:16.960
She is hesitant because she got so dinged up last time, but he ups his offer

01:22:17.030 --> 01:22:23.184
so that it's too good to pass up her make her an offer that she can't

01:22:23.232 --> 01:22:26.352
refuse. He promises that nothing like last time would be repeated.

01:22:26.416 --> 01:22:29.328
Technically not wrong, because he would go way further.

01:22:29.424 --> 01:22:32.596
First, he forces Christy to wait in the limo as he goes back into Nell's and

01:22:32.618 --> 01:22:33.832
enjoys drinks with the others.

01:22:33.886 --> 01:22:36.984
He's treating her like a pet because that's probably what he thinks of her.

01:22:37.022 --> 01:22:40.136
He and Elizabeth don't emerge from Nell's until 230 in the morning.

01:22:40.238 --> 01:22:42.776
They go back to Bateman's apartment, where he tries to convince the two

01:22:42.798 --> 01:22:43.636
girls to hook up.

01:22:43.678 --> 01:22:47.096
Elizabeth is against the idea until the ecstasy he slipped into her wine

01:22:47.128 --> 01:22:47.692
kicks in.

01:22:47.746 --> 01:22:51.208
Patrick directs the scene again and eventually joins in himself.

01:22:51.304 --> 01:22:54.824
He and Elizabeth tie Christie up on the futon, but Bateman gets too hungry

01:22:54.872 --> 01:22:58.828
and bites off a slice of pepperoni, if you catch my drift.

01:22:58.924 --> 01:23:01.836
Soon Elizabeth has gained enough awareness to sense danger, and she's

01:23:01.868 --> 01:23:03.340
running for her life out of the bedroom.

01:23:03.420 --> 01:23:06.652
But she slips, allowing Patrick to get her in the neck with a butcher knife.

01:23:06.716 --> 01:23:10.368
Next, he tries to recharge Christy using a pair of jumper cables connected

01:23:10.384 --> 01:23:11.120
to a battery.

01:23:11.200 --> 01:23:15.248
I'll let you figure out how those were connected between that and the array

01:23:15.264 --> 01:23:16.436
of matches he drops on her.

01:23:16.458 --> 01:23:18.944
She gets more fried than the calamari at Dorsia.

01:23:18.992 --> 01:23:22.676
Still, she manages to survive until he mashes her up with a pair of pliers,

01:23:22.708 --> 01:23:24.008
laughing as he does so.

01:23:24.094 --> 01:23:27.428
Somehow, although it does not seem possible, Patrick's mental stability

01:23:27.524 --> 01:23:29.032
continues to worsen after this.

01:23:29.086 --> 01:23:31.480
Plus, he's nearly driven to do the unthinkable.

01:23:31.560 --> 01:23:34.540
After another confrontation with Luis Carruthers,
(Impact)
♪ Classical Music ♪

01:23:43.360 --> 01:23:45.512
Bateman resorts to seeing a psychiatrist.

01:23:45.576 --> 01:23:48.252
But it's unclear exactly what goes on in these sessions.

01:23:48.316 --> 01:23:51.260
We do know that he doesn't admit his murders to the psychiatrist.

01:23:51.340 --> 01:23:54.256
Eventually, he spills everything to his lawyer, but that's not until much later.

01:23:54.278 --> 01:23:58.044
One Sunday afternoon, after having brunch with Christie's remains,

01:23:58.092 --> 01:24:01.008
he's out shopping at Barney's after being chased out of the lady's shoe

01:24:01.024 --> 01:24:04.096
department for making a scene, and he has the sense that he's being followed

01:24:04.128 --> 01:24:06.896
and discovers it's Luis Carruthers who pretends that it's a random encounter.

01:24:06.928 --> 01:24:10.276
Patrick tries to ignore him, refusing to look him in the eyes as

01:24:10.298 --> 01:24:11.808
Carruthers begs for his attention.

01:24:11.904 --> 01:24:15.128
He claims he's transferring to the Arizona branch because he can't bear to

01:24:15.134 --> 01:24:18.356
be around Patrick, and has the idea that Patrick is hiding his true feelings

01:24:18.388 --> 01:24:19.000
towards him.

01:24:19.070 --> 01:24:22.456
Luis starts crying and making a huge scene, which draws the attention of a

01:24:22.478 --> 01:24:23.204
security guard.

01:24:23.252 --> 01:24:26.044
Bateman threatens to slit his throat, but this does no good, because

01:24:26.082 --> 01:24:29.400
Carruthers cries that if they can't be together, he'd prefer to die.

01:24:29.480 --> 01:24:32.956
Bateman feels his sanity starting to fade, and it eventually gets to the

01:24:32.978 --> 01:24:36.204
point that he kicks Luis in the face and tells him to face reality, which

01:24:36.242 --> 01:24:38.252
seems to at least get Luis to stop whining.

01:24:38.316 --> 01:24:41.728
As autumn progresses, Bateman's homicidal compulsion goes up and down.

01:24:41.814 --> 01:24:45.004
He's sleeping in 20 minutes intervals, likely due to the clash of substances

01:24:45.052 --> 01:24:45.728
in his system.

01:24:45.814 --> 01:24:47.692
One day, he roams Central Park Zoo.

01:24:47.756 --> 01:24:50.944
He sees a sign warning guests not to throw coins which can kill the animals

01:24:50.992 --> 01:24:53.876
if swallowed, so he tosses a handful of them into the seal tank when

01:24:53.898 --> 01:24:54.628
nobody's looking.

01:24:54.714 --> 01:24:58.164
He believes it's the audience's enjoyment of the seals that bothers him, not

01:24:58.202 --> 01:24:59.220
the seals themselves.

01:24:59.370 --> 01:25:02.248
The bar for what triggers him is getting lower and lower.

01:25:02.334 --> 01:25:05.624
At the bird exhibit, he perceives some kind of telepathic communication from

01:25:05.662 --> 01:25:06.532
a snowy owl.

01:25:06.596 --> 01:25:08.756
Huey Lewis's sport is a flawless masterpiece.

01:25:08.788 --> 01:25:12.596
You say, OH RLY? Then in the penguin habitat, he crouches behind a trash

01:25:12.628 --> 01:25:15.628
can where a kid is throwing away a candy wrapper and stabs him in the

01:25:15.634 --> 01:25:18.808
throat, then mingles into the crowd and watches the reaction when the kid's

01:25:18.824 --> 01:25:21.004
mother discovers what has become of her child.

01:25:21.122 --> 01:25:24.284
He falsely claims to be a doctor and basically holds the child and does

01:25:24.322 --> 01:25:25.752
nothing until police arrive.

01:25:25.816 --> 01:25:29.312
After all of this, despite this probably being the riskiest thing he's done,

01:25:29.366 --> 01:25:32.512
he feels unsatisfied because he's taking a life that doesn't have much

01:25:32.566 --> 01:25:35.696
history and will affect fewer people, as opposed to taking someone in their

01:25:35.718 --> 01:25:38.736
prime who has friends, a career, a spouse and a past.

01:25:38.838 --> 01:25:41.748
This is obviously debatable because I think a lot of people are going to be

01:25:41.754 --> 01:25:44.996
more upset about something happening to a kid due to genetic instincts that

01:25:45.018 --> 01:25:46.212
drive us to protect our young.

01:25:46.266 --> 01:25:49.092
So this may be another example of him being out of touch with human emotion.

01:25:49.146 --> 01:25:52.616
He ends up just standing there and watching everything unfold as

01:25:52.638 --> 01:25:55.736
he slowly blends back into the crowd, then walks away.

01:25:55.838 --> 01:25:59.012
Even Bateman's appearances at the office start to become more sporadic.

01:25:59.076 --> 01:26:02.084
It seems the only thing he wants to do is work out and get restaurant

01:26:02.132 --> 01:26:03.672
reservations, then cancel them.

01:26:03.726 --> 01:26:05.480
He's mindlessly feeding his addiction.

01:26:05.560 --> 01:26:08.668
His apartment begins to wreak of brain pulp, which he had scooped out of

01:26:08.674 --> 01:26:09.324
Christie's head.

01:26:09.362 --> 01:26:13.016
So he uses Paul Owen's apartment to host two escort girls who he tortures

01:26:13.048 --> 01:26:16.528
with mace, a nail gun, nail scissors, a steak knife, acid and an array of

01:26:16.534 --> 01:26:17.212
power tools.

01:26:17.276 --> 01:26:19.996
I guess he felt a little self conscious when they started complimenting

01:26:20.028 --> 01:26:21.372
Owen's night's apartment.

01:26:21.516 --> 01:26:28.420
- [Christy] This is nicer than your other apartment.
– [Patrick Bateman] It's not that nice 

01:26:28.420 --> 01:26:30.564
Since he has other plans later, he goes about his day as

01:26:30.602 --> 01:26:34.084
normal after this, almost as if killing gives him a reset on his ability to

01:26:34.122 --> 01:26:35.120
function in society.

01:26:35.200 --> 01:26:38.388
In mid-October, he gets a bunch of new deliveries to his apartment, one of

01:26:38.394 --> 01:26:41.376
which is a replacement for his David Onica painting, which he's now decided

01:26:41.408 --> 01:26:41.908
to sell.

01:26:41.994 --> 01:26:44.596
This was the painting that Bethany had laughed at him for hanging upside

01:26:44.628 --> 01:26:48.408
down, more evidence of his yearning for her approval even after her supposed death.

01:26:48.494 --> 01:26:52.456
He's on the phone when he hears a huge rat emerge from his toilet and

01:26:52.478 --> 01:26:53.604
steal a leftover pizza.

01:26:53.652 --> 01:26:54.408
When I made the Chuck E. Cheese video.

01:26:54.414 --> 01:26:56.828
 I never thought I'd be using this clip again.

01:26:56.914 --> 01:27:00.636
Actually, he specifies it's a giant rat, so let's use this one instead.

01:27:00.738 --> 01:27:03.772
He's on a lot of Halcyon, the sleep medication that wards off total

01:27:03.826 --> 01:27:06.492
insanity. So the rat doesn't bother him as much as it should.

01:27:06.546 --> 01:27:09.804
He just buys an extra large mouse trap and spends the night in his dad's old

01:27:09.842 --> 01:27:10.824
suite in the Carlisle.

01:27:10.872 --> 01:27:14.108
The next morning, he finds the rat alive in the trap and barricades it in

01:27:14.114 --> 01:27:16.480
the sink with plans to use it to freak out girls.

01:27:16.560 --> 01:27:20.228
Though freak out is probably an understatement, as you will soon find out.

01:27:20.314 --> 01:27:22.276
And that brings us to another night.

01:27:22.378 --> 01:27:25.476
This is probably my favorite book chapter that isn't in the movie because of

01:27:25.498 --> 01:27:29.008
its chaotic ridiculousness, but it also gave me an epiphany about what's

01:27:29.024 --> 01:27:32.004
really going on with Patrick Bateman, and I think it holds the answer to

01:27:32.042 --> 01:27:33.232
what happened to Paul Owen.

01:27:33.296 --> 01:27:34.548
So let's break it down.

01:27:34.634 --> 01:27:35.956
It starts at 06:30 p.m.

01:27:35.978 --> 01:27:38.736
With Bateman and McDermott discussing their reservations for a restaurant

01:27:38.768 --> 01:27:39.700
called 1500.

01:27:39.770 --> 01:27:42.604
We find out that Hamlin wants to join, but he wants to eat somewhere else,

01:27:42.642 --> 01:27:45.496
but he doesn't have a backup place in mind and he wants to invite Luis

01:27:45.528 --> 01:27:49.000
Carothers. Bateman obviously says no, then tries to back out altogether.

01:27:49.080 --> 01:27:52.316
Then he changes his mind, realizing that Hamlin might be able to supply them

01:27:52.338 --> 01:27:54.572
with drugs and Luis won't be making it after all.

01:27:54.626 --> 01:27:58.028
So McDermott goes to cancel the reservation at 1500, and Hamlin goes to make

01:27:58.034 --> 01:27:59.580
a new reservation at Zeus Bar.

01:27:59.650 --> 01:28:02.396
While he's on the other line, they realize Zeus Bar might be hard to get

01:28:02.418 --> 01:28:03.980
into and brainstorm a backup plan.

01:28:04.050 --> 01:28:07.268
Then Jeanette calls on the other line, upset, and Bateman invites her to

01:28:07.274 --> 01:28:08.212
meet them at Zeus Bar.

01:28:08.266 --> 01:28:11.268
Back in the main call, they couldn't get into Zeus Bar, so McDermott makes a

01:28:11.274 --> 01:28:11.738
09:00 P.m.

01:28:11.738 --> 01:28:12.880
Reservation at Kaktus.

01:28:12.960 --> 01:28:15.776
Jeanette calls again and he gets confused and tells her they'll be at Zeus Bar.

01:28:15.808 --> 01:28:18.976
Back in the main call, Van Patton joins and he's going to go to dinner too.

01:28:19.008 --> 01:28:22.228
Then Evelyn calls on the other line, already aware of their plans at

01:28:22.234 --> 01:28:24.404
Kaktus thanks to Hamlin's girlfriend spilling the beans.

01:28:24.452 --> 01:28:27.272
He tells her that it's actually Zeus Bar that they're going to, which again

01:28:27.326 --> 01:28:28.228
is incorrect.

01:28:28.324 --> 01:28:30.996
He returns to the main call, where they remind him that he's already invited

01:28:31.028 --> 01:28:33.080
Jeanette, so bringing Evelyn might be a problem.

01:28:33.150 --> 01:28:36.188
Van Patton suggests canceling Jeanette and inviting Evelyn to Zeus Bar at

01:28:36.194 --> 01:28:38.776
930, where again, they do not have reservations.

01:28:38.888 --> 01:28:41.704
Bateman doesn't want to see Evelyn, so he tries to get Hamlin's girlfriend

01:28:41.752 --> 01:28:43.436
to invite Evelyn to go somewhere else.

01:28:43.538 --> 01:28:47.036
She refuses because she now has one on one plans with Hamlin, so he just

01:28:47.058 --> 01:28:49.856
invites both of them to go with the main group, unable to remember if

01:28:49.878 --> 01:28:51.440
they're going to Kaktus or Zeus Bar.

01:28:51.510 --> 01:28:54.656
Van Patton no longer wants Mexican food and they can't remember who made the

01:28:54.678 --> 01:28:58.176
reservation. Van Patton suggests a different restaurant called 1969, but

01:28:58.198 --> 01:29:00.012
when he goes to make the reservation, it's full.

01:29:00.086 --> 01:29:01.060
It's now 830.

01:29:01.130 --> 01:29:04.068
They continue to argue for another hour, so it's 930.

01:29:04.154 --> 01:29:05.748
They've been on the phone for 3 hours.

01:29:05.834 --> 01:29:08.996
This includes them canceling the Kaktus reservation and remaking it, and

01:29:09.018 --> 01:29:12.360
Bateman getting confused and canceling a nonexistent table at Zeus Bar.

01:29:12.430 --> 01:29:14.212
1969 is now closed.

01:29:14.276 --> 01:29:16.052
1500 is now closed.

01:29:16.116 --> 01:29:18.372
McDermott remakes another Kaktus reservation.

01:29:18.436 --> 01:29:21.784
Then Bateman gets a call from Evelyn, who is already at Kaktus with

01:29:21.822 --> 01:29:23.700
Jeanette, and she's pissed.

01:29:23.780 --> 01:29:25.236
He tells her they'll be going there shortly.

01:29:25.268 --> 01:29:28.108
But then the men start talking about something else, and it's unclear if

01:29:28.114 --> 01:29:30.984
they'll ever actually go to Kaktus or if they just continue to improvise

01:29:31.032 --> 01:29:32.892
their plans for the entire night.

01:29:33.026 --> 01:29:37.116
The key takeaway for all of this for me is how Bateman easily loses track of

01:29:37.138 --> 01:29:39.020
who's going and where they have reservations.

01:29:39.100 --> 01:29:42.256
We even see him start to invent reservations in his head cannon that were

01:29:42.278 --> 01:29:43.328
never actually made.

01:29:43.414 --> 01:29:47.216
So if Bateman's brain can be disoriented that easily, who is to say that he

01:29:47.238 --> 01:29:51.044
wouldn't also lack the ability to keep track of who he actually killed and

01:29:51.082 --> 01:29:54.500
who he just fantasized about killing? Now, in order for this to be possible,

01:29:54.570 --> 01:29:58.068
it's very likely that he does actually kill sometimes.

01:29:58.074 --> 01:30:01.060
But he has the situational awareness not to get himself caught in the moment.

01:30:01.130 --> 01:30:04.576
So if he's with an important coworker like Paul Owen, for example, he'll

01:30:04.608 --> 01:30:06.212
create a fantasy story in his head.

01:30:06.266 --> 01:30:09.796
He knows Paul is going to London, so he fantasizes about killing Paul and

01:30:09.818 --> 01:30:11.352
making it look like he's in London.

01:30:11.416 --> 01:30:14.476
But looking back at it shortly after, he just remembers the fantasy and

01:30:14.498 --> 01:30:16.428
comes to believe that that's what he really did.

01:30:16.514 --> 01:30:19.468
As for the other victims, it's very hard to determine if any of them are

01:30:19.474 --> 01:30:22.892
real or not, because the insanity is taking a bigger and bigger hold on him.

01:30:22.946 --> 01:30:26.156
For example, that month he films himself eating a girl's brain, and The

01:30:26.178 --> 01:30:29.260
Patty Winter Show is about a machine that lets people talk to the dead.

01:30:29.340 --> 01:30:31.484
His next victim is one of his most disturbing.

01:30:31.532 --> 01:30:34.492
He's foaming at the mouth and stuffs her insides full of cheese.

01:30:34.556 --> 01:30:37.548
Then he sticks a habit trail into her you know, those tunnels for pet

01:30:37.564 --> 01:30:41.344
rodents to run in? He then unleashes the giant rat, who he's been starving

01:30:41.392 --> 01:30:44.352
for the last five days and its bone appetite.

01:30:44.416 --> 01:30:47.444
At the end, he cuts her in two with the chainsaw, which goes through her so

01:30:47.482 --> 01:30:50.596
fast that she's alive for a moment to see him remove her legs from the rest

01:30:50.618 --> 01:30:51.188
of her body.

01:30:51.274 --> 01:30:53.732
He places her femur and jawbone to bake in the oven.

01:30:53.796 --> 01:30:56.744
I would say that Halloween came early this year, but for Patrick, it seems

01:30:56.782 --> 01:30:59.016
to come early every day of every year.

01:30:59.118 --> 01:31:02.136
Speaking of which, for Halloween, the office has a Halloween party, and

01:31:02.158 --> 01:31:06.040
Bateman goes as a mass murderer using a combination of real and fake blood.

01:31:06.120 --> 01:31:07.308
Nobody knows any better.

01:31:07.394 --> 01:31:10.316
As usual, this seems to give him that mental reset, and he's able to

01:31:10.338 --> 01:31:12.600
function normally for a short while in early November.

01:31:12.680 --> 01:31:16.072
He describes this feeling as being halfway capable of being cheerful.

01:31:16.136 --> 01:31:18.456
So he accepts Evelyn's invitation to go out to dinner.

01:31:18.488 --> 01:31:21.596
And while the dinner did manage to be halfway normal by his standards, the

01:31:21.618 --> 01:31:23.720
same could not be said about dessert.

01:31:23.800 --> 01:31:26.976
That morning, he stole a urine old cake and covered it with chocolate syrup

01:31:27.008 --> 01:31:28.676
and packaged it in a fancy box.

01:31:28.778 --> 01:31:31.668
He pays the waiter to present the dessert at the end of the meal, as if he

01:31:31.674 --> 01:31:34.804
had called earlier and ordered it special, and he sees to it that her water

01:31:34.842 --> 01:31:36.400
glass is empty when it arrives.

01:31:36.480 --> 01:31:40.304
As Evelyn eats it, she tries to hide her gag by saying it was just mintier

01:31:40.352 --> 01:31:41.312
than she suspected.

01:31:41.376 --> 01:31:44.696
This could truly only work on someone like Evelyn, who favors the value of

01:31:44.718 --> 01:31:47.060
the food she's eating over how it actually tastes.

01:31:47.140 --> 01:31:50.436
She also doesn't want to do anything to upset Patrick because of what she's

01:31:50.468 --> 01:31:51.320
about to ask next.

01:31:51.390 --> 01:31:53.732
She wants him to make a firm commitment for marriage.

01:31:53.796 --> 01:31:57.436
Patrick realizes that the eye she's had on him over the last two years is

01:31:57.458 --> 01:32:00.924
rooted in something closer to greed than adoration and explains to her

01:32:00.962 --> 01:32:02.668
what's truly important to him.

01:32:02.834 --> 01:32:06.952
– [Patrick Bateman] I need to engage in homicidal behavior on a massive scale.

01:32:07.016 --> 01:32:10.304
– [Patrick Bateman] Cannot be corrected, but I have no other way to fulfill my needs.

01:32:10.422 --> 01:32:11.532
No. Huge surprise.

01:32:11.596 --> 01:32:14.684
But she doesn't get it and thinks he's asking her to get breast implants

01:32:14.732 --> 01:32:17.328
again, which is a hilarious little detail in its own right.

01:32:17.414 --> 01:32:19.504
He tells Evelyn that it's over between them.

01:32:19.542 --> 01:32:22.740
He doesn't want to be weighed down with a commitment at 27 years old.

01:32:22.810 --> 01:32:25.892
According to his earlier comments, he would have recently just turned 28.

01:32:25.946 --> 01:32:29.668
So again, he's unable to keep track of time, not even realizing his own age.

01:32:29.754 --> 01:32:32.584
It takes a while for Evelyn to understand that he's serious about this

01:32:32.622 --> 01:32:35.092
breakup, but when she does, she becomes hysterical.

01:32:35.156 --> 01:32:38.360
Although Bateman doesn't enjoy her suffering as much as he thought he would,

01:32:38.430 --> 01:32:41.576
as she's crying and begging to know what will make him stay with her, his

01:32:41.598 --> 01:32:43.640
mind just wanders to a hundred other things.

01:32:43.710 --> 01:32:46.428
He tells her that because of her outburst, he will not be paying for the

01:32:46.434 --> 01:32:49.436
meal, and he leaves. Free from the confines of his relationship.

01:32:49.538 --> 01:32:52.596
Hh moves on to try even wilder things that would make his previous

01:32:52.648 --> 01:32:53.900
adventures look minuscule.

01:32:53.980 --> 01:32:56.896
He would make the most of the limited time remaining in the era of the

01:32:56.918 --> 01:32:59.970
yuppie, the 1980s were coming to a close.
(Impact)
♪ Mysterious Music ♪

01:33:09.020 --> 01:33:13.800
I tried for close to an hour to explain what happens in the chapter entitled

01:33:13.880 --> 01:33:17.896
Tries to Cook and Eat Girl without getting myself age restricted or yeeted

01:33:17.928 --> 01:33:20.508
off this platform and came up with close to nothing.

01:33:20.594 --> 01:33:22.876
If you need the details, go read the book yourself.

01:33:22.978 --> 01:33:25.536
I'm just gonna skip right to the analysis part.

01:33:25.638 --> 01:33:29.296
At first, Bateman kept his vices relatively closed off from one another.

01:33:29.398 --> 01:33:33.520
Then we saw certain bedroom activities combined with certain sharp object

01:33:33.590 --> 01:33:34.636
related activities.

01:33:34.748 --> 01:33:37.808
Now we are seeing his love of dining thrown into the mix as well.

01:33:37.894 --> 01:33:41.648
Then he does all of that while watching videos, just like the average

01:33:41.664 --> 01:33:42.304
CZsWorld viewer.

01:33:42.352 --> 01:33:42.912
I'm kidding.

01:33:42.976 --> 01:33:45.312
So he's combining more and more of these addictions.

01:33:45.376 --> 01:33:48.400
His hobbies are becoming one big conglomerate activity.

01:33:48.480 --> 01:33:51.816
My takeaway is that he's no longer able to get the response he used to from

01:33:51.838 --> 01:33:53.012
doing them individually.

01:33:53.076 --> 01:33:56.200
So now, in order to get that bigger hit, he's got to stack them.

01:33:56.270 --> 01:33:59.336
He also considers for maybe the first time if he'll ever spend time in prison.

01:33:59.358 --> 01:34:02.504
He can't tell if he's cooking anything correctly because he's never

01:34:02.542 --> 01:34:04.372
cooked before and he's crying too hard.

01:34:04.446 --> 01:34:05.464
Another new development.

01:34:05.512 --> 01:34:09.036
As far as we know, he's now carrying firearms around, keeping them in his

01:34:09.058 --> 01:34:12.124
locker when he goes to the gym, and bringing them on his daily errands, like

01:34:12.162 --> 01:34:15.900
returning videos, ATM withdrawals and violating NYU students.

01:34:16.050 --> 01:34:19.904
A new Patty Winter centers around a man who set his daughter on fire while

01:34:19.942 --> 01:34:21.020
she was giving birth.

01:34:21.100 --> 01:34:24.076
In addition to the Patty Winter Show segment serving as an insanity

01:34:24.108 --> 01:34:26.400
indicator, some of his meals seem to do the same.

01:34:26.470 --> 01:34:29.360
He goes to dinner with associates and they all order shark.

01:34:29.440 --> 01:34:32.484
Perhaps that is fitting because Bateman is seeing everyone around him as

01:34:32.522 --> 01:34:33.168
potential prey.

01:34:33.184 --> 01:34:35.236
Now, this is the craziest he gets.

01:34:35.338 --> 01:34:37.284
This is the absolute bottom.

01:34:37.402 --> 01:34:40.668
After midnight that night, he shoots a saxophonist performing on an empty

01:34:40.704 --> 01:34:41.284
street corner.

01:34:41.332 --> 01:34:44.792
But his silencer doesn't work, and he fails to notice a police squad car

01:34:44.846 --> 01:34:45.880
traveling behind him.

01:34:45.950 --> 01:34:49.352
At first, he tries to walk away casually, then it breaks into a run.

01:34:49.406 --> 01:34:52.568
The cop car takes off after him, so he jumps into a taxi and orders the

01:34:52.574 --> 01:34:55.948
cabby to drive, apparently just expecting that the guy will obey him just

01:34:55.954 --> 01:34:58.520
because he's so used to bossing around to blue collar workers.

01:34:58.600 --> 01:35:02.156
The guy panics, so Bateman shoots him and jacks the car, losing control into

01:35:02.178 --> 01:35:04.328
a Korean deli and knocking over the cashier.

01:35:04.424 --> 01:35:07.628
A cop charges from seemingly nowhere and knocks him to the sidewalk, and the

01:35:07.634 --> 01:35:08.984
two wrestle for Bateman's gun.

01:35:09.032 --> 01:35:11.996
But while all this is going on, all he can think about is that there should

01:35:12.018 --> 01:35:15.232
be music playing because it reminds him of an action scene in a movie.

01:35:15.296 --> 01:35:18.064
It's very reminiscent of the end of his Dinner with Jean that summer.

01:35:18.112 --> 01:35:20.644
He gets the upper hand on the cop and shoots him twice in the head.

01:35:20.682 --> 01:35:24.036
But as he's trying to get away, more cop cars show up in an alley and it

01:35:24.058 --> 01:35:27.216
turns into a huge firefight in the romance scene with Jean Patrick

01:35:27.248 --> 01:35:30.580
hallucinated, something cinematic then eventually came back down to earth.

01:35:30.660 --> 01:35:33.720
So if you haven't figured it out yet, that is probably what's happening

01:35:33.790 --> 01:35:37.416
here, because this whole Manhattan chase is pretty unrealistic, especially

01:35:37.518 --> 01:35:38.890
when this happens.

01:35:44.960 --> 01:35:48.776
He runs off to Wall Street and, noticing he's in a more affluent area, tries

01:35:48.808 --> 01:35:50.712
to steal several Porsches, unsuccessfully.

01:35:50.776 --> 01:35:54.236
So like an action hero, he somersaults over an embankment to get to his

01:35:54.258 --> 01:35:54.956
office building.

01:35:55.058 --> 01:35:58.416
Another more clear cut sign that he's in a hallucination is that he sees his

01:35:58.438 --> 01:36:00.876
office building and the lights go off floor by floor.

01:36:00.908 --> 01:36:01.772
As he approaches.

01:36:01.836 --> 01:36:05.424
However, he realizes that this is his old building before his company moved.

01:36:05.472 --> 01:36:08.436
This may be an extension of the theme of characters misidentifying one

01:36:08.458 --> 01:36:11.412
another, now expanded to include buildings, which is actually more understandable.

01:36:11.466 --> 01:36:13.652
- [Security Guard] Burning the midnight oil Mr. Smith.

01:36:13.706 --> 01:36:19.636
Ripped to the night watchman and to a janitor that happened to be

01:36:19.658 --> 01:36:22.904
watching. Patrick goes to his real office, signs in with his real night

01:36:22.942 --> 01:36:26.420
watchman, goes up to his office, and that's when the calmness returns.

01:36:26.500 --> 01:36:29.892
He thinks he may really be in some trouble this time and calls his lawyer,

01:36:29.956 --> 01:36:31.664
Harold Carnes, but gets the machine.

01:36:31.732 --> 01:36:36.172
He leaves a message sobbing as he details everything he's done 30, 40, maybe

01:36:36.226 --> 01:36:37.272
100 murders.

01:36:37.336 --> 01:36:39.512
A helicopter combs the area with a searchlight.

01:36:39.576 --> 01:36:42.908
But just like that, night turns to day and it's morning.

01:36:42.994 --> 01:36:46.028
Bateman describes this as some kind of optical illusion, though it's

01:36:46.044 --> 01:36:47.420
probably just his psychosis.

01:36:47.500 --> 01:36:50.988
However, his voicemail message to Carnes would eventually be acknowledged.

01:36:51.084 --> 01:36:53.120
Until then, he returns to the real world.

01:36:53.190 --> 01:36:56.396
He takes Jeanette to dinner at Deck Chairs, stays home to watch Patty

01:36:56.428 --> 01:36:59.392
Winters, and then sleeps with Courtney while Luis is in Atlanta.

01:36:59.456 --> 01:37:02.836
One week before their wedding, Courtney seems depressed and tries to talk to

01:37:02.858 --> 01:37:05.828
Patrick about something, but he brushes her off, telling her she's going to

01:37:05.834 --> 01:37:08.116
marry Luis and that there's nothing to talk about.

01:37:08.218 --> 01:37:10.668
Whatever she was going to say, she keeps to herself.

01:37:10.784 --> 01:37:14.184
During a dinner at Smith and Wollensky with Craig McDermott, Bateman's mind

01:37:14.222 --> 01:37:17.668
wanders to his cousin from Washington, who recently bit a girl's earlobes off.

01:37:17.684 --> 01:37:21.224
If this is real, it's likely that Bateman's condition is hereditary, or

01:37:21.262 --> 01:37:23.628
at the very least, mental illness runs in the family.

01:37:23.714 --> 01:37:26.972
We know his mother is institutionalized for something, which we'll get to,

01:37:27.026 --> 01:37:30.184
and his little brother definitely exhibits a lot of sociopathic personality

01:37:30.232 --> 01:37:31.000
traits himself.

01:37:31.080 --> 01:37:34.576
McDermott, always one to criticize one's food choices, called Bateman a

01:37:34.598 --> 01:37:37.180
raving maniac for not ordering the hash browns.

01:37:37.260 --> 01:37:39.500
Been at PNP too long, he mutters.

01:37:39.580 --> 01:37:42.640
Another night, after seeing a musical with Jeanette, he describes another

01:37:42.710 --> 01:37:46.684
strange food order, eagle Carpaccia, which comes with a weird gazpacho

01:37:46.732 --> 01:37:49.796
containing raw chicken, something that no restaurant would ever serve for

01:37:49.818 --> 01:37:52.356
health and safety reasons, except maybe one.

01:37:52.458 --> 01:37:54.912
- [Futurama] Good, because it turns out I forgot to cook that chicken.

01:37:54.976 --> 01:37:58.612
Unlike Evelyn, who talks too much, Jeanette hardly seems to speak at all.

01:37:58.666 --> 01:38:01.128
Throughout the date, she keeps sighing and threatening to light her

01:38:01.134 --> 01:38:04.936
cigarette in the nonsmoking section. She drinks six champagne cures, and he

01:38:04.958 --> 01:38:08.616
even considers her behavior unsettling, causing black thoughts to form and

01:38:08.638 --> 01:38:12.036
expand in his head, It's almost like Jeanette is a reflection of Bateman's desires.

01:38:12.068 --> 01:38:13.784
Evelyn annoyed him.

01:38:13.822 --> 01:38:17.164
She talked too much, said the wrong things, had the wrong interests, and was

01:38:17.202 --> 01:38:18.908
resistant to almost everything he did.

01:38:18.994 --> 01:38:21.916
Jeanette, on the other hand, seems to go along with anything, and the more

01:38:21.938 --> 01:38:25.288
disturbing Bateman's headspace gets, the more disturbed he is by Jeanette's behavior.

01:38:25.304 --> 01:38:28.336
He even tells her to get a water when she's thirsty instead of

01:38:28.358 --> 01:38:29.180
more champagne.

01:38:29.260 --> 01:38:32.396
The following month, Patrick has a rare visit with his mother in the mental

01:38:32.428 --> 01:38:33.852
institution Sandstone.

01:38:33.916 --> 01:38:37.248
At least I assume this takes place in December because his mom asks him what

01:38:37.254 --> 01:38:38.300
he wants for Christmas.

01:38:38.380 --> 01:38:40.544
Patrick is under the impression that it's mid April.

01:38:40.592 --> 01:38:43.716
I'm not really sure either of them can be trusted to correctly relay the

01:38:43.738 --> 01:38:46.720
date. However, the previous events definitely took place in November.

01:38:46.800 --> 01:38:49.968
Courtney told Patrick to have a nice Thanksgiving, and we've not yet reached

01:38:49.984 --> 01:38:52.272
the chapter titled End of the 1980s.

01:38:52.346 --> 01:38:54.552
At first, it's unclear why he visits his mom.

01:38:54.606 --> 01:38:56.184
He doesn't seem to care about her at all.

01:38:56.222 --> 01:38:59.076
But throughout the encounter, he takes note of all the things he's bought

01:38:59.108 --> 01:39:02.328
her. It's likely that he's visiting to give her gifts, another win that he

01:39:02.334 --> 01:39:03.300
can hold over Sean.

01:39:03.380 --> 01:39:07.016
The next time we hear from Patrick, he claims that 161 days have passed

01:39:07.048 --> 01:39:10.412
since his night at Paul's apartment with the two escort girls.

01:39:10.466 --> 01:39:13.388
That puts us somewhere in February or March of the year 1990.

01:39:13.474 --> 01:39:15.736
That is, if you want to believe that he has his bearings.

01:39:15.768 --> 01:39:19.324
When he says 161 days have passed, I'm going to take his word for it this

01:39:19.362 --> 01:39:22.220
time because it's raining and rain during March in New York.

01:39:22.290 --> 01:39:22.908
Makes sense.

01:39:22.994 --> 01:39:25.060
Plus we don't really have anything else to go on.

01:39:25.130 --> 01:39:28.564
With no word of any bodies being discovered in any newspaper, he gets

01:39:28.602 --> 01:39:32.356
curious and starts asking peers if they heard about two mutilated women in

01:39:32.378 --> 01:39:35.828
Paul Owens apartment, which is absolutely hilarious to inquire about.

01:39:35.914 --> 01:39:39.216
He's practically begging to get caught, but nobody will throw a suspicious

01:39:39.248 --> 01:39:40.400
glance in his direction.

01:39:40.480 --> 01:39:43.656
Of course, given my theory that Paul's death was only a fantasy, he never

01:39:43.678 --> 01:39:46.536
would have acquired access to Paul's apartment, which means he never would

01:39:46.558 --> 01:39:47.592
have brought the girls there.

01:39:47.646 --> 01:39:48.980
That was also a fantasy.

01:39:49.060 --> 01:39:52.552
This is backed up by the fact that when he checks back in 161 days later,

01:39:52.606 --> 01:39:56.204
the building looks different and the keys no longer fit, maybe because they

01:39:56.242 --> 01:39:56.860
never did.

01:39:56.930 --> 01:40:00.268
A doorman lets him in anyway and he goes up to an apartment in the midst of

01:40:00.274 --> 01:40:01.432
a real estate showing.

01:40:01.496 --> 01:40:04.636
He notices the blinds are different but the furniture is the same and there

01:40:04.658 --> 01:40:07.628
are bouquets of flowers all over the apartment, which he can smell.

01:40:07.724 --> 01:40:11.376
Could this be proof that he actually did kill Paul and the realtor is using

01:40:11.398 --> 01:40:15.072
the flowers to cover the stench left behind by the bodies of the escorts? Or

01:40:15.126 --> 01:40:18.756
is that overthinking it? It is normal to want the apartment you're trying to

01:40:18.778 --> 01:40:19.988
sell to smell good.

01:40:20.074 --> 01:40:23.424
The realtor tells him that Paul does not live there, but she realizes

01:40:23.472 --> 01:40:25.300
something that causes her to tighten up.

01:40:25.370 --> 01:40:27.850
Perhaps most suspiciously, this happens.

01:40:29.820 --> 01:40:34.280
-[Realtor] You saw the ad in the Times, 
–[Patrick Bateman] No. Yeah.

01:40:34.430 --> 01:40:37.064
–[Patrick Bateman] I mean, yeah, in the Times.

01:40:37.182 --> 01:40:38.970
-[Realtor] There was no ad in the Times.

01:40:40.140 --> 01:40:41.880
-[Realtor] I think you should go now.

01:40:42.030 --> 01:40:45.260
This is the most compelling evidence that the murders are actually real.

01:40:45.330 --> 01:40:48.556
However, I'd make the counterargument that Patrick's behavior is just

01:40:48.578 --> 01:40:51.676
unnerving in general and she probably felt uncomfortable with him just

01:40:51.698 --> 01:40:53.660
showing up out of the blue and snooping around.

01:40:53.730 --> 01:40:57.004
She tells him not to come back and watches him all the way back to the door

01:40:57.052 --> 01:41:00.396
in the elevator, the smell of roses is overpowering, which symbolizes

01:41:00.428 --> 01:41:03.856
Patrick seriously feeling the paranoia of getting caught for the first time.

01:41:03.958 --> 01:41:07.248
But that doesn't stop him from slicing up three more women and according to

01:41:07.254 --> 01:41:09.792
him, storing their private parts in his gym locker.

01:41:09.856 --> 01:41:13.604
The chapter I mentioned earlier, entitled End of the 1980s actually takes

01:41:13.642 --> 01:41:15.508
place several months into the new year.

01:41:15.594 --> 01:41:19.316
He says the anniversary of Elvis's death was a month ago and Elvis died on

01:41:19.338 --> 01:41:20.276
August 16.

01:41:20.298 --> 01:41:22.276
So theoretically it's mid September.

01:41:22.388 --> 01:41:25.848
As always, there's a very good possibility that Patrick has no idea what the

01:41:25.854 --> 01:41:26.318
date is.

01:41:26.318 --> 01:41:29.704
But he does say on multiple occasions that this is September and the cool,

01:41:29.742 --> 01:41:31.288
sunny weather does match up.

01:41:31.374 --> 01:41:34.424
As for the chapter title, he might not be saying this is literally where

01:41:34.462 --> 01:41:37.852
1980 ends, as in, like, New Year's Eve, but this might be the first moment

01:41:37.906 --> 01:41:40.012
where it stops feeling like the 1980s.

01:41:40.066 --> 01:41:44.316
Much like how I always say that the 1990s ended in 2003 with the release of Hey Ya.

01:41:44.338 --> 01:41:48.108
He's now having horrible dreams where rich people in lavish settings

01:41:48.124 --> 01:41:49.680
are massacred in tragic ways.

01:41:49.750 --> 01:41:53.248
He's no longer able to distinguish a dream from reality, as evidenced by the

01:41:53.254 --> 01:41:56.480
ratio of brutal accidents he sees randomly walking around in the city.

01:41:56.550 --> 01:41:59.940
He meets Jean for lunch near Tower Records, and they seem to be dating now.

01:42:00.010 --> 01:42:02.752
As they discuss plans for dinner, he mentions that he's on a diet.

01:42:02.816 --> 01:42:05.876
Jean tells him that he already looks great, to which he replies, you can

01:42:05.898 --> 01:42:06.704
always be thinner.

01:42:06.752 --> 01:42:10.116
A callback to Evelyn's you can always be in better shape line way back in

01:42:10.138 --> 01:42:10.868
Chapter one.

01:42:10.954 --> 01:42:13.732
Somehow it seems that he's still holding a grudge against Evelyn.

01:42:13.796 --> 01:42:16.296
Jean tells him they don't have to go out to dinner if it's going to ruin his

01:42:16.318 --> 01:42:19.816
willpower, and he admits he's not very good at controlling it anyways, which

01:42:19.838 --> 01:42:21.816
has got to be the understatement of the year.

01:42:21.918 --> 01:42:25.196
Over coffee, he tells her a story about a Wall Street guy he saw marking up

01:42:25.218 --> 01:42:27.560
a wall in the bathroom who wrote Kill All Yuppies.

01:42:27.640 --> 01:42:30.636
Earlier I talked about how the yuppie lifestyle was already growing out of

01:42:30.658 --> 01:42:33.516
fashion in the eyes of the crowd at Club Chernobyl, and now it appears that

01:42:33.538 --> 01:42:35.548
even Wall Street people are sick of the fad.

01:42:35.644 --> 01:42:39.856
If the date is September 1990, that means we're only seven months away from

01:42:39.878 --> 01:42:42.764
that yuppie dumb obituary being published in Time magazine.

01:42:42.812 --> 01:42:46.256
Bateman has modeled his entire lifestyle around being hip and rich, but

01:42:46.278 --> 01:42:49.068
that's about to go out of style, which can't be good for his mental

01:42:49.084 --> 01:42:52.452
stability. As usual, Jean doesn't seem to care about any of that.

01:42:52.506 --> 01:42:55.236
She tells him that they need to talk about something, and anyone who's ever

01:42:55.258 --> 01:42:57.108
heard those words knows what comes next.

01:42:57.194 --> 01:43:00.564
She thinks she's in love with him, and he gives her very mixed signals in

01:43:00.602 --> 01:43:03.816
return. Usually he's much more direct with women, which could mean that he

01:43:03.838 --> 01:43:07.016
really does feel something for Jean but just doesn't know how to deal with

01:43:07.038 --> 01:43:10.424
that. Jean is worried that he's disappointed in her for confessing her love,

01:43:10.462 --> 01:43:13.564
but he secretly thinks that he could never be disappointed because he no

01:43:13.602 --> 01:43:15.740
longer finds anything worth looking forward to.

01:43:15.810 --> 01:43:18.540
Her reasons for liking him are also completely off the mark.

01:43:18.610 --> 01:43:22.156
She sees him as someone concerned with others mysterious, considerate, and shy.

01:43:22.178 --> 01:43:23.864
Summertime is Jean.

01:43:23.912 --> 01:43:28.416
He explains the lines separating appearance what you see and reality, what

01:43:28.438 --> 01:43:30.860
you don't, become, well, blurred.

01:43:30.940 --> 01:43:34.016
He's very aware of the illusionment he's been living in over the last couple

01:43:34.038 --> 01:43:37.152
of years, and although he seems to be giving her the cold shoulder at first,

01:43:37.206 --> 01:43:40.580
he has this realization that this could be a crucial moment in his life.

01:43:40.650 --> 01:43:43.940
For the first time, Jean seems stronger and less controllable than before,

01:43:44.010 --> 01:43:47.416
which he's attracted to and thinks to himself, why not end up with her?

01:43:47.518 --> 01:43:52.280
Well, before he could consider that, there would be one major roadblock.
(Impact)

01:44:01.260 --> 01:44:05.596
Sometime after he turns 29 in October, Patrick Bateman gets some news from

01:44:05.618 --> 01:44:07.208
Jeanette. She's pregnant.

01:44:07.304 --> 01:44:10.412
Something that's kind of surprising because he's been very particular about

01:44:10.466 --> 01:44:11.708
contraception in the past.

01:44:11.794 --> 01:44:14.588
On December 20, they argue about if they should carry forward with the

01:44:14.594 --> 01:44:17.212
pregnancy, which results in Jeanette getting a black eye.

01:44:17.276 --> 01:44:21.008
The next day, he gets his way and Jeanette is sobbing the entire car ride to

01:44:21.014 --> 01:44:22.012
the abortion clinic.

01:44:22.076 --> 01:44:25.180
According to Patrick, this is the fifth time he's aborted a pregnancy.

01:44:25.260 --> 01:44:28.676
With all of that being said, I can't say with much confidence that any of

01:44:28.698 --> 01:44:32.244
this actually happened, because the Patty Winter Show that day featured an

01:44:32.282 --> 01:44:33.760
interview with Big Foot.

01:44:33.840 --> 01:44:36.996
This is the last time we see Jeanette, so maybe the abortion is more symbolic.

01:44:37.018 --> 01:44:40.596
An abortion is immediate and irreversible, and he's ending his

01:44:40.618 --> 01:44:43.460
relationship with her in an immediate, irreversible way.

01:44:43.530 --> 01:44:46.656
Once again, he experiments with new ways to hurt her that don't include

01:44:46.688 --> 01:44:47.632
physical abuse.

01:44:47.696 --> 01:44:51.628
He buys a bunch of baby items a doll, a rattle, a teething ring, and a

01:44:51.634 --> 01:44:54.524
stuffed polar bear and leaves them in the back of the limo for her to find

01:44:54.562 --> 01:44:55.340
when she gets back.

01:44:55.410 --> 01:44:58.268
He's about to leave on a flight to Aspen that day, and Jeanette is going to

01:44:58.274 --> 01:44:59.784
be out of the country when he returns.

01:44:59.832 --> 01:45:03.068
He makes it very clear that he doesn't plan on seeing her again.

01:45:03.154 --> 01:45:05.116
He says Jeanette should be okay.

01:45:05.218 --> 01:45:07.072
She has her whole life in front of her.

01:45:07.126 --> 01:45:09.168
That is, if she doesn't run into me.

01:45:09.254 --> 01:45:12.464
If you want to interpret Jeanette as a fantasy version of Jean, this means

01:45:12.502 --> 01:45:15.164
he's decided to focus his efforts on being with the real Jean.

01:45:15.212 --> 01:45:18.192
If you want to interpret Jeanette as just a separate woman, then this means

01:45:18.246 --> 01:45:20.420
Patrick is just ending his relationship with her.

01:45:20.490 --> 01:45:24.356
Bateman gets back from Aspen in January 1991 and finally makes use of his

01:45:24.378 --> 01:45:28.148
piano for the first time, or at least the piano strings, which he uses on a

01:45:28.154 --> 01:45:29.072
couple of victims.

01:45:29.136 --> 01:45:31.972
The second week of February is especially unhinged.

01:45:32.036 --> 01:45:35.656
He starts drinking his own urine, sleeping under his futon, flossing his

01:45:35.678 --> 01:45:38.808
gums to bleeding, and he's nearly caught trying to send a girl's heart to

01:45:38.814 --> 01:45:40.036
her mother at FedEx.

01:45:40.148 --> 01:45:43.620
He also doesn't seem entirely done torturing Evelyn, because he sends her a

01:45:43.630 --> 01:45:47.516
box of flies with a note saying he never wants to see her again and to go on

01:45:47.538 --> 01:45:48.632
a diet... LOL.

01:45:48.696 --> 01:45:51.464
Meanwhile, his relationship with Jean seems to have blossomed.

01:45:51.512 --> 01:45:54.796
On Valentine's Day, he shows affection in the only way he knows how by

01:45:54.818 --> 01:45:57.308
having a bunch of expensive stuff delivered to her apartment.

01:45:57.404 --> 01:45:59.852
The shipment also includes hundreds of white roses.

01:45:59.916 --> 01:46:03.420
The white rose is said to symbolize loyalty, purity and innocence.

01:46:03.500 --> 01:46:05.104
I don't really know where that comes from.

01:46:05.142 --> 01:46:08.612
But if you look up white rose symbolism, a bunch of websites seem to agree

01:46:08.666 --> 01:46:09.476
on this fact.

01:46:09.578 --> 01:46:12.688
Of course, none of these characteristics apply to Bateman whatsoever.

01:46:12.784 --> 01:46:16.324
Is he attracted to these qualities in Jean? Or does he have a desire to

01:46:16.362 --> 01:46:18.228
clean up his act? Time will tell.

01:46:18.314 --> 01:46:21.496
That morning he also has his maid come in and clean up a bloody mess in his

01:46:21.518 --> 01:46:23.688
apartment. I'm guessing this is not real.

01:46:23.774 --> 01:46:26.500
Or maybe this maid is just incredibly subservient.

01:46:26.580 --> 01:46:29.416
It wouldn't be the first time that he hired someone to clean up after his carnage.

01:46:29.438 --> 01:46:33.016
He reads various articles in the Post, such as three people who

01:46:33.038 --> 01:46:36.824
disappeared aboard a yacht found hacked up and frozen in ice, and a maniac

01:46:36.872 --> 01:46:39.548
going around poisoning one liter bottles of Evian water.

01:46:39.634 --> 01:46:42.684
This is a lot like the first chapter, where Bateman and Price read about two

01:46:42.722 --> 01:46:45.776
people who disappeared on a yacht among a slew of other problems with their

01:46:45.798 --> 01:46:49.356
city. Both of these chapters take place on a pseudo holiday, April Fool's

01:46:49.388 --> 01:46:51.148
Day and Valentine's Day, respectively.

01:46:51.244 --> 01:46:53.692
We see this a lot in any traditional story structure.

01:46:53.756 --> 01:46:57.264
It's called the denouement, or falling action after the climax, where things

01:46:57.302 --> 01:46:58.220
go back to normal.

01:46:58.300 --> 01:47:01.472
Usually this happens after some kind of resolution with the main character,

01:47:01.536 --> 01:47:04.772
but Bateman stands out here because nothing has been resolved with him.

01:47:04.826 --> 01:47:08.436
And speaking of a return to the beginning, Timothy Price resurfaces for the

01:47:08.458 --> 01:47:11.892
first time since his disappearance in the tunnel at Tunnel nightclub almost

01:47:11.946 --> 01:47:12.584
four years ago.

01:47:12.622 --> 01:47:15.604
Now, early on, I discussed Bateman following in his footsteps.

01:47:15.652 --> 01:47:18.468
It's rumored that Price went to rehab, and it makes me wonder if Bateman

01:47:18.484 --> 01:47:19.640
will do the same at some point.

01:47:19.710 --> 01:47:21.256
Or maybe he already has.

01:47:21.358 --> 01:47:24.696
Price visits him in his office and has a smudge on his forehead that Bateman

01:47:24.728 --> 01:47:25.992
assumes is a hallucination.

01:47:26.056 --> 01:47:29.804
However, in 1991, the Catholic holiday Ash Wednesday was the day before

01:47:29.842 --> 01:47:30.604
Valentine's Day.

01:47:30.642 --> 01:47:33.512
So it's likely that this smudge was actually just leftover ash.

01:47:33.576 --> 01:47:36.300
Ash Wednesday is the day that Catholics give up something that they love.

01:47:36.370 --> 01:47:39.020
Maybe Price has decided to give up his use of drugs.

01:47:39.100 --> 01:47:42.096
When asked where he's been, he says that he's been back for a while, but he

01:47:42.118 --> 01:47:44.512
moved companies a callback to his complaints about his pay in the first chapter.

01:47:44.566 --> 01:47:48.256
One day after feeding portions of brain matter to dogs

01:47:48.288 --> 01:47:52.692
in the park, he passes by Al, the Hobo that he supposedly blinded in 1988.

01:47:52.746 --> 01:47:56.304
He's holding a sign that reads Vietnam Vet blinded in Vietnam.

01:47:56.432 --> 01:47:59.844
This would suggest that he was already blind and Bateman's assault on him

01:47:59.882 --> 01:48:00.980
was just imaginary.

01:48:01.060 --> 01:48:04.424
However, when Bateman goes up to him and whispers, you never were in

01:48:04.462 --> 01:48:07.752
Vietnam, the man pisses himself and begs him not to hurt him.

01:48:07.806 --> 01:48:10.856
We're constantly being shown conflicting evidence towards the validity of

01:48:10.878 --> 01:48:11.924
Patrick's outbursts.

01:48:11.972 --> 01:48:15.644
To top it all off, the Patty Winter Show that day features a cheerio being

01:48:15.682 --> 01:48:19.116
interviewed for close to an hour, and Patrick buys a chocolate bar that he

01:48:19.138 --> 01:48:20.460
claims has a bone in it.

01:48:20.530 --> 01:48:23.996
One evening, Bateman goes to a new club called World's End with Jean and

01:48:24.018 --> 01:48:25.176
another woman named Nina.

01:48:25.208 --> 01:48:28.844
When he finally runs into his lawyer, Harold Carnes, the two have not spoken

01:48:28.892 --> 01:48:30.892
since Bateman's unhinged voicemail confession.

01:48:30.956 --> 01:48:34.320
When he brings it up, it becomes clear that Carnes has, like many others,

01:48:34.390 --> 01:48:37.728
mistaken him with someone named Davis, and he thinks the whole thing is a joke.

01:48:37.734 --> 01:48:40.816
He doesn't even believe that Bateman would be capable of killing

01:48:40.848 --> 01:48:44.288
Paul Owen or even picking up an escort because he sees him as too much of a goody goody.

01:48:44.304 --> 01:48:46.884
Carnes thinks that Evelyn was the one to dump him.

01:48:46.922 --> 01:48:50.216
And when Bateman insists that the murders really did happen, he refuses to believe.

01:48:50.238 --> 01:48:54.584
-[Carnes] Because I had dinner with Paul Allen twice in London just ten days ago.

01:48:54.622 --> 01:48:58.264
Is it possible, given all the mistaken identity in this story, that

01:48:58.302 --> 01:49:02.696
Carnes actually had dinner with somebody else? Sure, but as I stated, I

01:49:02.718 --> 01:49:04.440
don't believe that Paul is really dead.

01:49:04.520 --> 01:49:05.212
To calm down.

01:49:05.266 --> 01:49:06.456
Patrick takes a halcion.

01:49:06.488 --> 01:49:09.672
He also tries to get Nina's number while Jean is in the bathroom downstairs.

01:49:09.736 --> 01:49:12.876
Even though he seems to recognize that Jean is special to him, he still has

01:49:12.898 --> 01:49:14.956
not departed from his adulterous ways.

01:49:15.058 --> 01:49:18.268
The next week, he reluctantly has a decadent breakfast with his former

01:49:18.284 --> 01:49:20.700
dealer, who got a real job and another colleague.

01:49:20.780 --> 01:49:24.288
Instead of his mind wandering to the usual degenerate stuff he's into, he

01:49:24.294 --> 01:49:27.936
thinks about helping America's schools and getting tickets to see Sting in

01:49:27.958 --> 01:49:28.796
Three Penny Opera.

01:49:28.828 --> 01:49:31.956
Just as very strange behavior for him, because helping schools sounds like

01:49:31.978 --> 01:49:34.916
something he preached about to virtue signal to his friends way back at the

01:49:34.938 --> 01:49:37.668
beginning, rather than something that he'd actually be interested in doing.

01:49:37.754 --> 01:49:40.864
And not long ago, he thought that Jeanette deserved to get an abortion

01:49:40.912 --> 01:49:42.328
because she thought Sting was cool.

01:49:42.414 --> 01:49:46.148
Is Patrick starting to gain some sensibility back? He finds himself reading

01:49:46.164 --> 01:49:49.496
the paper as he listens to his walkman in a taxi, just like he did back in

01:49:49.518 --> 01:49:50.072
Chapter One.

01:49:50.126 --> 01:49:52.372
But the cab driver keeps looking at him funny.

01:49:52.436 --> 01:49:55.788
Then the driver locks the doors and speeds down the highway, accusing him of

01:49:55.794 --> 01:49:58.184
being the guy who killed a fellow cabby named Soli.

01:49:58.232 --> 01:50:01.256
This could be the driver that Bateman took out before that big Manhattan

01:50:01.288 --> 01:50:01.992
chase scene.

01:50:02.056 --> 01:50:04.076
Perhaps parts of that scene were real.

01:50:04.178 --> 01:50:07.740
Ironically, he's finally been correctly identified, and it's by a nobody.

01:50:07.820 --> 01:50:11.356
The cab driver is holding a gun and robs him of his Rolex watch, his wallet,

01:50:11.388 --> 01:50:14.364
and his expensive sunglasses, knowing that he can't go to the police.

01:50:14.412 --> 01:50:16.160
Otherwise, the driver will just rat him out.

01:50:16.230 --> 01:50:19.284
There is no reward for turning in Bateman, so he's able to steal his stuff

01:50:19.322 --> 01:50:20.352
without repercussion.

01:50:20.416 --> 01:50:23.552
This is the first consequence that Patrick has ever paid for his actions,

01:50:23.616 --> 01:50:24.708
and it isn't much.

01:50:24.794 --> 01:50:27.988
However, he does lose his expensive material possessions, which are part of

01:50:27.994 --> 01:50:31.440
what makes him feel powerful. Before speeding off the cab driver calls him a

01:50:31.450 --> 01:50:33.924
yuppie scumbag, which makes Bateman choke up a sob.

01:50:33.972 --> 01:50:37.172
He's supposed to have power over blue collar people like a cab driver.

01:50:37.236 --> 01:50:40.536
All in all, the emotional damage to Bateman's ego probably ends up being the

01:50:40.558 --> 01:50:42.120
worst part of this ordeal for him.

01:50:42.190 --> 01:50:45.436
His insurance pays for a new gold Rolex, but his mind continues to deteriorate. 

01:50:45.458 --> 01:50:49.144
He believes his ATM machine is speaking to him with weird

01:50:49.192 --> 01:50:52.588
messages, and a park bench followed him home for six blocks and spoke to

01:50:52.594 --> 01:50:55.776
him. He spends the morning making a necklace out of a girl's vertebrae and

01:50:55.798 --> 01:50:57.452
wearing it to watch adult tapes.

01:50:57.516 --> 01:51:00.028
But he still returns to his usual debauchery.

01:51:00.124 --> 01:51:03.616
One Friday, a group of guys, including Price, McDermott, Goodrich and Farrell,

01:51:03.638 --> 01:51:06.432
go back to the first bar that we ever saw Bateman go to Harry's.

01:51:06.486 --> 01:51:09.876
And American Psycho comes to a close, with the guys debating where

01:51:09.898 --> 01:51:10.960
to get dinner reservations.

01:51:11.040 --> 01:51:14.196
As everyone bombards Bateman with questions of men's, fashion and other

01:51:14.218 --> 01:51:17.248
useless topics that he's made himself an expert in, he wonders if he's

01:51:17.264 --> 01:51:18.384
become a fitness junkie.

01:51:18.432 --> 01:51:21.528
Certainly an ironic thing for an actual junkie to be worried about.

01:51:21.614 --> 01:51:23.780
His favorite group is now the Talking Heads.

01:51:23.860 --> 01:51:26.520
It's a relatively normal topic on the Patty Winter Show.

01:51:26.590 --> 01:51:30.216
Does economic success equal happiness? The answer from the guys is a

01:51:30.238 --> 01:51:31.560
resounding definitely.

01:51:31.710 --> 01:51:34.984
Even though there's been no shortage of scenes lately with Bateman crying

01:51:35.032 --> 01:51:38.616
and depressed, we see him lose track of the calendar once more when scenes

01:51:38.648 --> 01:51:39.884
from President George H. W. Bush's inauguration

01:51:39.922 --> 01:51:43.596
come on screen, which Bateman thinks was earlier in

01:51:43.618 --> 01:51:44.050
the year.

01:51:44.050 --> 01:51:48.560
The inauguration took place in January 1989, and it is now early 1991.

01:51:48.630 --> 01:51:52.192
He also says he's 27 years old, even though he's 29 now.

01:51:52.246 --> 01:51:54.992
I think I'm going to say the same thing I got scammed out of two years during COVID.

01:51:55.046 --> 01:51:57.984
They debate the pros and cons of President Bush, and

01:51:58.022 --> 01:51:59.732
conversation eventually shifts to work.

01:51:59.786 --> 01:52:02.080
The Fisher account is no longer the hot commodity.

01:52:02.160 --> 01:52:04.468
Now the guys are more interested in the Shepherd account.

01:52:04.554 --> 01:52:07.220
He sees someone who looks like Marcus Halberstam paying a check.

01:52:07.290 --> 01:52:10.964
Above one of the doors, there's a sign that reads this is not an exit, which

01:52:11.002 --> 01:52:14.456
references the Jean Paul-Sartre play no Exit, in which the characters are

01:52:14.478 --> 01:52:16.168
trapped in a room that represents hell.

01:52:16.254 --> 01:52:19.816
Bateman may go on going through the motions of his life and probably will

01:52:19.838 --> 01:52:23.176
never face time for his crimes, but he'll always be crushed under the weight

01:52:23.208 --> 01:52:24.152
of his vices.

01:52:24.296 --> 01:52:28.328
However, the end of American Psycho is not the end of the Patrick Bateman

01:52:28.344 --> 01:52:29.516
timeline, so to speak.

01:52:29.618 --> 01:52:33.564
And no, I'm not talking about the atrocity known as American Psycho Two: All American Girl. 

01:52:33.602 --> 01:52:37.056
Where a little girl kills him with an ice pick and all of his

01:52:37.078 --> 01:52:38.284
crimes are exposed.

01:52:38.412 --> 01:52:41.468
No, if we're going by the canon, there were a few details about Bateman's

01:52:41.484 --> 01:52:45.436
life documented in the American Psycho 2000 emails, a viral marketing

01:52:45.468 --> 01:52:46.828
campaign used to promote the release.

01:52:46.844 --> 01:52:48.992
Of the movie adaptation of American Psycho.

01:52:49.056 --> 01:52:52.452
In our world, these emails were written by the movie marketing team, not

01:52:52.506 --> 01:52:53.472
Brett Easton Ellis.

01:52:53.536 --> 01:52:56.992
However, they were approved by Ellis, so some fans consider them canon.

01:52:57.056 --> 01:53:00.176
However, it is possible that these emails are more of a sequel to the movie

01:53:00.208 --> 01:53:03.236
than the novel, because they use movie variations of the character names

01:53:03.268 --> 01:53:05.172
like Paul Allen instead of Paul Owen.

01:53:05.236 --> 01:53:08.056
But it's easy to chalk that up to the fact that these emails are supposed to

01:53:08.078 --> 01:53:08.852
promote the movie.

01:53:08.916 --> 01:53:12.248
And other than that minor detail, the emails do better fit the timeline of

01:53:12.254 --> 01:53:15.596
the novel, because despite being set in the spring of 2000, they fill in

01:53:15.618 --> 01:53:18.588
details about what happened to Bateman right after the events of the book in 1991.

01:53:18.594 --> 01:53:22.380
Plus, they do make references to details that are only in the book,

01:53:22.450 --> 01:53:25.544
like Bateman's grandfather owning a property in Connecticut and Courtney

01:53:25.592 --> 01:53:26.552
moving to Arizona.

01:53:26.616 --> 01:53:29.328
So it's understood that this is supposed to be a follow up to the book.

01:53:29.414 --> 01:53:33.136
The emails describe Jean being there for Patrick at his lowest point, and by

01:53:33.158 --> 01:53:36.672
fall of 1991, she is pregnant with their first child.

01:53:36.806 --> 01:53:40.116
Even if it wasn't her initial intention to date Patrick for his money, she

01:53:40.138 --> 01:53:42.660
does grow accustomed to being rich pretty early on.

01:53:42.730 --> 01:53:44.384
At some point, they do get married.

01:53:44.432 --> 01:53:49.176
And in early to mid 1992, at the age of 30, Patrick Bateman would become a

01:53:49.198 --> 01:53:49.880
father.
(Impact)
♪ Classical Music ♪

01:53:58.620 --> 01:54:00.020
Patrick Bateman, Jr.

01:54:00.100 --> 01:54:02.228
Would become known by his initials, PB.

01:54:02.324 --> 01:54:05.436
As he grew up, he quickly learned to avoid anything that his father would

01:54:05.458 --> 01:54:06.312
find objectionable.

01:54:06.376 --> 01:54:09.848
He preferred browsing expensive catalogs to being entertained by the likes

01:54:09.864 --> 01:54:11.480
of Barney or Mickey Mouse.

01:54:11.560 --> 01:54:14.376
Patrick considered his son to be his best accessory.

01:54:14.488 --> 01:54:18.940
By spring of 1993, Jean started to not like the idea of flying commercially.

01:54:19.020 --> 01:54:22.204
She didn't want their son to be breathing the same air as the other flyers,

01:54:22.252 --> 01:54:25.068
and this pushed Patrick to look into purchasing a private jet.

01:54:25.164 --> 01:54:28.244
Before we get to the beginning of Patrick's email inbox, he does have two

01:54:28.282 --> 01:54:30.832
other appearances in the novels of Brett Easton Ellis.

01:54:30.896 --> 01:54:35.876
The first is in his conspiracy satire novel Glam-orama or Glamorama, I'm not

01:54:35.898 --> 01:54:36.468
really sure.

01:54:36.554 --> 01:54:39.700
That novel seems to take place entirely in 1996.

01:54:39.770 --> 01:54:42.916
So five years after the end of American Psycho, and it appears that he's

01:54:42.948 --> 01:54:46.516
still quite literally getting away with murder, the main character, a male

01:54:46.548 --> 01:54:49.876
model named Victor, is planning this big party for celebrities to celebrate

01:54:49.908 --> 01:54:51.172
the opening of a new club.

01:54:51.236 --> 01:54:54.396
Like Bateman, he lives a very glamorous lifestyle, as the title would suggest.

01:54:54.418 --> 01:54:58.268
One evening at a popular bar called Bowery Bar, which is absolutely

01:54:58.354 --> 01:55:01.436
filled with A list celebrities, Patrick is with a bunch of publicists and

01:55:01.458 --> 01:55:04.668
the sons of a well known movie producer, the first of his dabbling into the

01:55:04.674 --> 01:55:05.404
movie business.

01:55:05.522 --> 01:55:07.372
And he stops by to say hi to Victor.

01:55:07.436 --> 01:55:11.116
As they shake hands, Victor notices weird stains on the lapel of his Armani

01:55:11.148 --> 01:55:14.636
suit that costs as much as a car personally, I think that's all the evidence

01:55:14.668 --> 01:55:17.696
I need to assume that Patrick is still up to his old ways.

01:55:17.798 --> 01:55:20.932
Victor's girlfriend finds him strange, but Victor insists that he's a nice guy.

01:55:20.986 --> 01:55:24.240
I should also mention that Victor is not the smartest.

01:55:24.320 --> 01:55:27.556
Bateman does appear in one other Brett Easton Ellis book, but it's not

01:55:27.578 --> 01:55:28.752
really supposed to be canon.

01:55:28.816 --> 01:55:32.376
Lunar park is a mock memoir of the author himself, set in the late ninety s

01:55:32.398 --> 01:55:33.384
and early 2000s.

01:55:33.422 --> 01:55:36.216
Starting with the press tour for the release of Glam-orama, there are

01:55:36.238 --> 01:55:39.316
portions where Ellis interacts with his own characters, so this most likely

01:55:39.348 --> 01:55:42.788
takes place in the real world, not the Brett Houston Ellis verse.

01:55:42.884 --> 01:55:45.896
There are little information bits in the emails that fill the two year gap

01:55:45.928 --> 01:55:49.704
between Glam-orama and the main events of the American Psycho 2000 emails.

01:55:49.752 --> 01:55:53.612
In 1998, he left Jean and moved to a new apartment because he felt he needed

01:55:53.666 --> 01:55:54.236
a change.

01:55:54.338 --> 01:55:58.112
He ends up in a sky mansion in Central Park West, three floors with his own

01:55:58.166 --> 01:56:00.160
gym, sauna spa and media center.

01:56:00.230 --> 01:56:03.184
He considers this time away from Jean to be the most pleasant time of their relationship.

01:56:03.222 --> 01:56:07.196
He also makes getaways to a luxury hotel on the French Riviera

01:56:07.228 --> 01:56:10.916
and teaches PB to swim, dive and water ski at the same pavilion where he

01:56:10.938 --> 01:56:12.548
learned each of these skills as a boy.

01:56:12.634 --> 01:56:16.324
At the beginning of the new millennium, Patrick is 38 years old and PB is

01:56:16.362 --> 01:56:19.800
seven. By this time, Patrick has gotten much richer than he was in American

01:56:19.870 --> 01:56:23.576
Psycho. He has his own private jet called the Falcon 50, and he has all the

01:56:23.598 --> 01:56:27.544
latest in 90's communication technology, such as the satellite phone.

01:56:27.662 --> 01:56:29.060
Truly cutting edge.

01:56:29.140 --> 01:56:31.092
He no longer works at Pierce and Pierce.

01:56:31.156 --> 01:56:34.424
There was some kind of issue, and he explains it was better for all parties

01:56:34.472 --> 01:56:36.060
involved if he quietly left.

01:56:36.130 --> 01:56:38.796
I'd assume he just does investment banking on his own now.

01:56:38.898 --> 01:56:41.948
Despite that, he still has a new greatest rival, a billionaire from the

01:56:41.954 --> 01:56:43.592
Midwest named Davis Ferguson.

01:56:43.656 --> 01:56:47.056
He loathes Ferguson because he has stocks in a silica mining company that

01:56:47.078 --> 01:56:48.332
Bateman would like for himself.

01:56:48.396 --> 01:56:50.720
It's Paul Owen and the Fisher account all over again.

01:56:50.790 --> 01:56:54.128
Bateman has hired spies to keep tabs on Ferguson for the time being.

01:56:54.214 --> 01:56:57.136
Patty Winters seems to have gone off the air, but Bateman has found other

01:56:57.158 --> 01:56:58.124
talk shows to watch.

01:56:58.182 --> 01:56:59.796
He does not like modern music.

01:56:59.898 --> 01:57:03.364
The emails consist of personal journals that seem to be to himself and

01:57:03.402 --> 01:57:05.412
communications with a therapist named Dr. M

01:57:05.466 --> 01:57:08.688
 who has been hired to do virtual therapy sessions.

01:57:08.784 --> 01:57:11.796
He thinks that having gone to therapy will help him in his custody battle

01:57:11.828 --> 01:57:12.616
over his son.

01:57:12.718 --> 01:57:15.736
Bateman clearly doesn't care what the doctor has to say about him and has

01:57:15.758 --> 01:57:18.804
instructed him to shut up and follow Patrick's agenda during their virtual

01:57:18.852 --> 01:57:19.752
sessions. Dr. M

01:57:19.806 --> 01:57:22.584
doesn't think this will be helpful and questions the ethics of it, but

01:57:22.622 --> 01:57:25.900
Bateman replies, do you want the money or don't you? 

01:57:25.970 --> 01:57:27.736
And Dr. M has no further concerns.

01:57:27.848 --> 01:57:32.076
The main issue in the divorce proceedings is that Jean wants $189,000 per

01:57:32.098 --> 01:57:35.436
month in alimony, which Patrick finds ridiculous because she grew up in a

01:57:35.458 --> 01:57:36.704
$40,000 house.

01:57:36.822 --> 01:57:37.792
Patrick sends Dr. M

01:57:37.846 --> 01:57:40.780
To try to get information from Jean that could be used in the trial.

01:57:40.860 --> 01:57:44.176
As for friends and associates, Patrick has hired Marcus Halberstam to do

01:57:44.198 --> 01:57:47.632
something overseas in order to get away from New York after some quote

01:57:47.696 --> 01:57:50.980
unpleasant innuendo about Paul Owen attached itself to him.

01:57:51.050 --> 01:57:54.544
Surprisingly, Luis Carruthers is back after getting divorced from Courtney.

01:57:54.592 --> 01:57:57.876
Patrick found that Carruthers was useful at sucking valuable information out

01:57:57.898 --> 01:58:01.000
of media and entertainment figures and decided to make use of it.

01:58:01.070 --> 01:58:04.244
Price seems to have had his own brief marriage to Courtney, and Van Patton

01:58:04.292 --> 01:58:06.008
unsuccessfully tried to become a model.

01:58:06.094 --> 01:58:07.700
There's no mention of McDermott.

01:58:07.780 --> 01:58:11.112
Perhaps the biggest surprise is that by this time, Patrick has actually

01:58:11.166 --> 01:58:12.248
gotten his act together.

01:58:12.334 --> 01:58:13.176
So it seems.

01:58:13.278 --> 01:58:16.908
He indicates he's stopped doing cocaine because he sees the brand Prada as a

01:58:16.914 --> 01:58:18.104
much better status symbol.

01:58:18.152 --> 01:58:21.340
And he claims that he has mastered his rage and found better ways to channel

01:58:21.410 --> 01:58:23.740
his extreme dislike of people and things.

01:58:23.890 --> 01:58:27.744
But make no mistake, he may not go around killing people anymore, but he

01:58:27.782 --> 01:58:31.216
uses his cunning, personality, and abundance of power to torture and

01:58:31.238 --> 01:58:33.404
sometimes even destroy his victims.

01:58:33.452 --> 01:58:35.452
He is still an American Psycho.

01:58:35.516 --> 01:58:38.948
In February, PB goes to Palm Beach with friends and comes back wanting a

01:58:38.954 --> 01:58:40.112
bigger plane and a horse.

01:58:40.176 --> 01:58:43.588
He's allergic to horses, but Patrick gets him a racing horse to watch in the

01:58:43.594 --> 01:58:44.480
Kentucky Derby.

01:58:44.560 --> 01:58:46.784
The therapy sessions began on March 16.

01:58:46.832 --> 01:58:49.312
The next day, Patrick gets a call from Evelyn.

01:58:49.376 --> 01:58:51.956
Apparently, they're back in touch, and she's sorry to hear about the

01:58:51.978 --> 01:58:55.032
divorce, but uses it as an opportunity to take a dig at him.

01:58:55.086 --> 01:58:57.336
That's what happens when you don't marry one of your own.

01:58:57.438 --> 01:59:00.952
On the 24th, he meets her for lunch at the Carlyle Hotel, where she's living now.

01:59:01.006 --> 01:59:03.528
He's hungover from partying the previous night.

01:59:03.614 --> 01:59:04.748
Some things never change.

01:59:04.834 --> 01:59:08.044
She shoots her shot, telling him that she hasn't had a good time in three years.

01:59:08.082 --> 01:59:11.596
Patrick isn't surprised by this, given that all of her husbands were

01:59:11.618 --> 01:59:14.316
gay. And no, we don't know how many husbands she had.

01:59:14.418 --> 01:59:16.968
However, she gets upset and throws a Caesar salad at him.

01:59:16.994 --> 01:59:20.176
It crashes to the floor, and when he sees croutons, he leaves, telling her

01:59:20.198 --> 01:59:21.772
he can't have them due to his diet.

01:59:21.836 --> 01:59:25.436
Another major development for Bateman is that the 1990s seemed to have cured

01:59:25.468 --> 01:59:26.732
him of his homophobia.

01:59:26.796 --> 01:59:29.992
He's friends with a gay multibillionaire named Simone Derevny.

01:59:30.076 --> 01:59:34.176
On March 28, while aboard Simone's 200 foot boat, they plot against Davis

01:59:34.208 --> 01:59:37.476
Ferguson and joke about getting an even bigger boat when it's all over.

01:59:37.578 --> 01:59:40.764
Later, he eats lunch with Luis Carruthers, who is now out of the closet.

01:59:40.832 --> 01:59:44.276
Patrick has been using Luis to spy on Ferguson's son, who goes by

01:59:44.308 --> 01:59:46.936
Terry Davis and is also gay, though I'm not sure.

01:59:46.958 --> 01:59:50.680
Carruthers knows he's a spy, but Bateman is using him to get information.

01:59:50.830 --> 01:59:53.848
Ferguson was the country's largest owner of silicon mines, but the

01:59:53.854 --> 01:59:56.828
government tried to break up his monopoly in the 80s, so he sold the

01:59:56.834 --> 02:00:00.744
majority of the stocks to an offshore entity, secretly making his son Terry

02:00:00.792 --> 02:00:03.196
the chief beneficiary of the offshore company.

02:00:03.298 --> 02:00:07.744
So Terry has no idea how rich he is, and Ferguson must never let anyone find

02:00:07.782 --> 02:00:09.660
out, otherwise he's going to jail.

02:00:09.740 --> 02:00:10.956
Terry is also broke now.

02:00:10.988 --> 02:00:14.460
His father stopped giving him money after his 10th trip to rehab

02:00:14.540 --> 02:00:17.516
because of this when he received a lunch invite from the multibillionaire

02:00:17.548 --> 02:00:19.380
Simone Dereveni on March 31.

02:00:19.450 --> 02:00:23.040
He accepted, and Bateman watched the entire encounter from a nearby table.

02:00:23.120 --> 02:00:26.676
Simone handed him an envelope proving that Terry is the legal owner of the

02:00:26.698 --> 02:00:29.780
Tray Corporation, which is worth $2 billion.

02:00:29.930 --> 02:00:33.576
The next day is April 1, the twelve year anniversary of the beginning of

02:00:33.598 --> 02:00:34.484
American Psycho.

02:00:34.532 --> 02:00:37.816
Evelyn calls and tells him she's now being sued by a maid who slipped on an

02:00:37.838 --> 02:00:39.844
anchovy from the salad that she threw at Bateman.

02:00:39.892 --> 02:00:42.644
He doesn't seem too interested in talking to her, but there's a noticeable

02:00:42.692 --> 02:00:44.092
difference in how he treats her.

02:00:44.146 --> 02:00:47.532
He no longer blows up his unwanted relationships like he did in his twenties

02:00:47.586 --> 02:00:51.208
and he's clearly becoming more sly about destroying his enemies.

02:00:51.304 --> 02:00:54.588
The next day was Sunday, which is his day with PB, and the two went to the

02:00:54.594 --> 02:00:57.264
movies and saw PB's favorite, the 6th Sense.

02:00:57.382 --> 02:00:59.420
PB has excellent taste.

02:00:59.500 --> 02:01:02.240
Patrick is going to take him in the car, but the kid would rather walk,

02:01:02.310 --> 02:01:05.536
claiming he no longer wants to be seen in the car in case he runs into his friends. 

02:01:05.558 --> 02:01:08.348
PB has excellent taste.

02:01:08.444 --> 02:01:11.668
It's kind of funny how Patrick's entire life is him flexing his wealth for

02:01:11.674 --> 02:01:12.372
others to see.

02:01:12.426 --> 02:01:15.796
And while his son is equally concerned by what his peers think of him, 

02:01:15.818 --> 02:01:18.068
He doesn't want to be seen coming out of an expensive car.

02:01:18.154 --> 02:01:21.248
I would argue that the truly rich are those who receive the benefits of

02:01:21.274 --> 02:01:22.660
walking and public transportation.

02:01:22.740 --> 02:01:26.392
I guess 80s Bateman is superior to 2000s Bateman in that regard.

02:01:26.526 --> 02:01:29.748
Patrick asks his son what they will do if it rains, even though he clearly

02:01:29.764 --> 02:01:30.676
has a raincoat.

02:01:30.708 --> 02:01:34.436
So PB brings out some Metro tokens given to him by Jean, and Patrick doesn't

02:01:34.468 --> 02:01:37.452
know what they are, which is probably a mistake by the writers, because

02:01:37.506 --> 02:01:40.764
Patrick does use the subway in American Psycho, but we could explain this by

02:01:40.802 --> 02:01:43.068
saying that he was so coked up that he doesn't remember.

02:01:43.154 --> 02:01:46.832
On April 3, Patrick seems to be falling back into his own ways and makes this.

02:01:46.886 --> 02:01:50.688
Only this time he's motivated by his desire for a relationship with

02:01:50.694 --> 02:01:53.324
his son to keep his mask of sanity intact.

02:01:53.452 --> 02:01:56.604
On the fourth, the shares of the Tray Corporation are being sold to Simone

02:01:56.652 --> 02:01:57.244
Dereveny.

02:01:57.292 --> 02:02:00.356
Ferguson's name is not attached to these shares, so there's nothing he can

02:02:00.378 --> 02:02:03.796
do about it without exposing himself as the company's true operator and the

02:02:03.818 --> 02:02:05.588
criminal sanctions that go with that.

02:02:05.674 --> 02:02:09.156
In a moment of desperation, he calls Bateman, who agrees to meet with him at

02:02:09.178 --> 02:02:10.328
Le Cirque in New York.

02:02:10.414 --> 02:02:13.896
Meanwhile, Bateman has his place photographed for Architectural Digest

02:02:13.918 --> 02:02:17.348
New York issue, and it's a bigger invasion of privacy than he anticipated.

02:02:17.444 --> 02:02:20.744
One of the photographers says that Bateman's place is almost perfect other

02:02:20.782 --> 02:02:23.172
than the lighting fixtures, which gets under his skin.

02:02:23.236 --> 02:02:26.636
The yuppies may be long gone, but he's still fixated on having the best of

02:02:26.658 --> 02:02:27.158
the best.

02:02:27.218 --> 02:02:30.668
Davis arrives on the 6th and ends up looking foolish when he has to borrow a

02:02:30.674 --> 02:02:33.388
jacket from the Le Cirque management in order to fit the dress code.

02:02:33.474 --> 02:02:36.768
Bateman also bribes the coat girl to bring him an ugly jacket, just one of

02:02:36.774 --> 02:02:39.916
the many examples of the advanced warfare he now employs.

02:02:40.028 --> 02:02:43.136
Davis would like Bateman to outbid Simone for the trust shares, and in

02:02:43.158 --> 02:02:45.916
exchange, he's willing to share some of the revenue from the mining

02:02:45.948 --> 02:02:48.464
operation Bateman says he'll have to think about.

02:02:48.582 --> 02:02:52.256
A couple days later, on April 8, after stopping at his new favorite sandwich

02:02:52.288 --> 02:02:55.428
shop, he's held up by a beggar at the bank who asks for cash and tries to

02:02:55.434 --> 02:02:56.772
pull a stiletto blade on him.

02:02:56.826 --> 02:02:59.988
As the man looks down at his weapon, Bateman kicks him down, then kicks him

02:02:59.994 --> 02:03:01.092
in the jaw, breaking it.

02:03:01.146 --> 02:03:04.376
He stomps on the man's neck repeatedly until his trachea begins to turn pink.

02:03:04.398 --> 02:03:07.928
If Bateman really has stopped killing strangers in this new era, it

02:03:07.934 --> 02:03:10.248
seems that his killer instincts have not gone away.

02:03:10.334 --> 02:03:13.336
The next day, he goes out searching for the perfect lighting fixtures for

02:03:13.358 --> 02:03:16.316
his dining room, and he comes across the building that Dorsia used to be in

02:03:16.338 --> 02:03:19.704
before it shut down, where they are now building yet another new exclusive restaurant. 

02:03:19.752 --> 02:03:23.208
On the way back, there are no cabs, so he uses one of PB's

02:03:23.224 --> 02:03:26.536
transit tokens to hop on the subway, only it makes him claustrophobic.

02:03:26.568 --> 02:03:29.756
So he gets off early and just happens to come across the perfect lighting

02:03:29.788 --> 02:03:31.632
fixtures. By the stop that he gets off at.

02:03:31.686 --> 02:03:34.896
They aren't the most expensive ones, but perhaps his son has inspired him to

02:03:34.918 --> 02:03:37.776
realize that the most expensive things aren't always the best.

02:03:37.878 --> 02:03:40.948
In the morning, Bateman is woken up by a call from Simone telling him to

02:03:40.954 --> 02:03:41.920
come over immediately.

02:03:42.000 --> 02:03:44.832
When he arrives, he learns that Davis Ferguson is dead.

02:03:44.896 --> 02:03:47.972
According to Bateman's spies around the city, Ferguson had a heart attack

02:03:48.026 --> 02:03:51.096
when his son told him that he knew the Tray Corporation shares were in his name.

02:03:51.118 --> 02:03:54.996
When Ferguson collapsed through a glass table, terry ran downstairs

02:03:55.028 --> 02:03:58.244
and told the desk man, then immediately fled to Simone's apartment.

02:03:58.372 --> 02:04:01.928
In the original American Psycho, we saw Bateman follow Tim Price down the

02:04:01.934 --> 02:04:05.096
path of addiction and obsession in the name of becoming the most respected

02:04:05.128 --> 02:04:08.204
yuppie. Remember, at the beginning of the book, he claims Tim is the most

02:04:08.242 --> 02:04:09.432
interesting man he knows.

02:04:09.496 --> 02:04:13.724
Now, in the 2000 emails arc, he sees Davis Ferguson, a man richer than him,

02:04:13.762 --> 02:04:16.280
and makes the conscious choice not to go down that path.

02:04:16.360 --> 02:04:19.744
Ferguson has a damaged relationship with his son, and that ends up being his

02:04:19.782 --> 02:04:23.212
downfall. Bateman, on the other hand, wants to have a relationship with PB,

02:04:23.276 --> 02:04:26.512
and over the course of these two months, learns to prioritize the father son

02:04:26.566 --> 02:04:28.592
relationship over his revenge on Jean.

02:04:28.656 --> 02:04:32.036
At this point, he's willing to pay Jean in order to spare PB from any more

02:04:32.058 --> 02:04:36.852
heartache. But during a lunch with Jean on April 11, the situation changes.

02:04:36.986 --> 02:04:40.756
Jean announces that she's met someone new and that someone is Bateman's

02:04:40.788 --> 02:04:41.720
therapist,

02:04:41.790 --> 02:04:42.776
 Dr. Robert M.

02:04:42.878 --> 02:04:46.196
After being sent by Bateman to gather information on her, the two had fallen

02:04:46.228 --> 02:04:47.860
in love, and they were set to marry.

02:04:47.940 --> 02:04:51.176
Dr. M suggests ending their sessions so that the three of them can learn to

02:04:51.198 --> 02:04:51.800
become friends.

02:04:51.870 --> 02:04:54.144
But Bateman is overcome with rage.

02:04:54.212 --> 02:04:56.828
He breaks his wine glass in his hand and storms out.

02:04:56.914 --> 02:05:00.456
This is the closest we've seen him to losing his temper in the 2000 emails arc.

02:05:00.488 --> 02:05:03.896
arc. That afternoon, Detective Donald Kimball, who is now the police

02:05:03.928 --> 02:05:07.184
commissioner, comes over with some illegal cigars and wants Bateman to help

02:05:07.222 --> 02:05:08.528
him dispose of them.

02:05:08.614 --> 02:05:11.584
We don't know the exact nature of their relationship, but I got the idea

02:05:11.622 --> 02:05:15.072
that Bateman has Kimball under his thumb, and maybe he's paid him off to

02:05:15.126 --> 02:05:16.432
overlook certain things.

02:05:16.566 --> 02:05:19.744
Commissioner Kimball notices the cut on Bateman's hand, and Bateman jokes

02:05:19.792 --> 02:05:23.008
that he tried to kill a waiter, but the act slipped and shattered a dessert

02:05:23.024 --> 02:05:24.948
cart, and the two have a laugh about it.

02:05:25.034 --> 02:05:27.732
The final therapy email between Bateman and Dr. M

02:05:27.786 --> 02:05:31.572
demonstrates how Bateman was able to overcome his past ways and transition

02:05:31.636 --> 02:05:33.828
from serial criminal to puppet master.

02:05:33.924 --> 02:05:37.064
When asked how his doctor's relationship with Jean is going to affect him,

02:05:37.102 --> 02:05:38.980
Bateman turns on Caps Lock.

02:05:39.060 --> 02:05:44.076
Are you out of your goddamned mind? The fact that you are not, as we speak,

02:05:44.178 --> 02:05:48.844
pulling your laptop out of your, is a blinding testimony to how well I have

02:05:48.882 --> 02:05:54.044
mastered my own rage and learned to control my desire to destroy, maim, and

02:05:54.082 --> 02:05:58.288
kill, which would indicate to some that you have done a remarkable job in a

02:05:58.294 --> 02:05:59.328
rather brief time.

02:05:59.414 --> 02:06:02.304
But I think it would be safe to assume that the professional part of our

02:06:02.342 --> 02:06:03.696
relationship is over.

02:06:03.798 --> 02:06:06.912
Ironically, Bateman kind of gets what he wanted because it is Dr. M.

02:06:06.966 --> 02:06:10.448
Who convinces Jean that she should not be dependent on Bateman's alimony

02:06:10.464 --> 02:06:11.540
money in order to be happy.

02:06:11.610 --> 02:06:13.984
And with that resolved, they're able to finalize the divorce.

02:06:14.032 --> 02:06:17.552
With Jean receiving enough money to live comfortably, but not in splendor,

02:06:17.616 --> 02:06:20.148
he ends the communication with a final warning.

02:06:20.244 --> 02:06:22.180
Don't ever ask him to call you dad.

02:06:22.260 --> 02:06:23.752
That name's been taken.

02:06:23.886 --> 02:06:26.024
Ah. Bateman cares about someone.

02:06:26.142 --> 02:06:27.576
Only took 38 years.

02:06:27.678 --> 02:06:31.416
As for Davis Ferguson's fortune, his estate quickly accepted the offer that

02:06:31.438 --> 02:06:34.828
Simone made on behalf of Patrick Bateman, which I would assume puts him in

02:06:34.834 --> 02:06:37.964
the top 50 richest people in the USA, at least.

02:06:38.082 --> 02:06:41.452
The night of the 16th, Patrick is honored at the Manhattan Children's Center

02:06:41.506 --> 02:06:43.432
for a foundation gift that he contributed.

02:06:43.496 --> 02:06:46.656
Part of the event was a charity auction where A-list celebrities bid on

02:06:46.678 --> 02:06:50.204
various items, one of which was a small role in a new movie called

02:06:50.252 --> 02:06:51.724
Fashion Victims behind the scenes.

02:06:51.772 --> 02:06:53.696
Patrick has been getting into the movie business.

02:06:53.798 --> 02:06:55.740
He is applauded at the end of his speech.

02:06:55.820 --> 02:06:59.688
The next day, he leaves for a vacation in France and in his journal claims

02:06:59.724 --> 02:07:02.468
that he's at peace with himself and with the world.

02:07:02.554 --> 02:07:06.004
He goes to the hotel on the French Riviera, the one that he went to as a kid

02:07:06.042 --> 02:07:08.304
and brought PB to on several occasions.

02:07:08.432 --> 02:07:12.248
It is the one place on Earth where I can stand to be alone and in fact,

02:07:12.334 --> 02:07:17.096
welcome the opportunity to recharge, rethink, regroup, or just stare up into

02:07:17.118 --> 02:07:20.820
the sun until everything becomes white and peaceful, like in heaven.

02:07:20.900 --> 02:07:23.316
I don't recommend anyone try the stare into the sun part.

02:07:23.358 --> 02:07:26.984
It's implied that this is the coping mechanism that he's found to recharge,

02:07:27.032 --> 02:07:29.980
and it doesn't involve torture or the end of someone's life.

02:07:30.050 --> 02:07:33.244
On the plane, his steward asks if he'd like to watch a movie, and he puts in

02:07:33.282 --> 02:07:36.172
the newest release from one of the film companies that Bateman has acquired.

02:07:36.236 --> 02:07:37.852
It's described as following.

02:07:37.916 --> 02:07:42.016
The film begins with the sounds of a harp being gently plucked as drops of

02:07:42.038 --> 02:07:44.464
red fall slowly into a sea of white.

02:07:44.582 --> 02:07:48.388
You may recognize this as the opening of the film adaptation of American

02:07:48.474 --> 02:07:52.788
Psycho, which came out April 14, 2003, days before this plane ride to

02:07:52.794 --> 02:07:56.292
France. I would assume the in universe version of the movie is different from

02:07:56.346 --> 02:07:59.716
American Psycho in our world, kind of like how the Stab movies are not the

02:07:59.738 --> 02:08:01.292
exact same as the Scream movies.

02:08:01.376 --> 02:08:04.184
In this case, they probably changed the names of people a little more than

02:08:04.222 --> 02:08:07.368
just changing Paul Owen to Paul Allen so that nobody would notice the similarities.

02:08:07.374 --> 02:08:11.236
But then again, Bateman literally tried to confess his crimes

02:08:11.268 --> 02:08:13.004
at one point, and nobody would believe him.

02:08:13.042 --> 02:08:15.356
So who knows? Maybe they just left the names the same.

02:08:15.458 --> 02:08:18.796
I find it kind of fitting that Bateman ends up producing a movie about his

02:08:18.818 --> 02:08:23.748
adventures. Remember way back at the beginning of this video, I'm guessing

02:08:23.784 --> 02:08:27.084
that's like at least 2 hours ago when I discussed Brett Easton Ellis's

02:08:27.132 --> 02:08:29.132
inspiration for writing American Psycho.

02:08:29.196 --> 02:08:33.244
– [Interviewer] When you get into Patrick Bateman, and obviously you're not writing

02:08:33.292 --> 02:08:38.244
– [Interviewer] autobiographically, I assume Brett about dismemberment and murder.

02:08:38.282 --> 02:08:40.560
– [Brett Easton Ellis] But American Psycho is my most autobiographical novel.

02:08:40.640 --> 02:08:43.076
– [Interviewer] Can you explain that? 
– [Brett Easton Ellis] That's who I was.

02:08:43.178 --> 02:08:46.516
– [Brett Easton Ellis]I've never come clean about whether I think Patrick Bateman committed the

02:08:46.538 --> 02:08:50.196
– [Brett Easton Ellis]crimes or they were fantasies, and the book is completely open to

02:08:50.218 --> 02:08:52.404
– [Brett Easton Ellis]interpretation. You can make an argument either way about it.

02:08:52.442 --> 02:08:55.624
– [Brett Easton Ellis]There are clues that say it didn't happen, and then he was fantasizing about

02:08:55.662 --> 02:08:58.356
– [Brett Easton Ellis]this. And then there are other clues that suggest, well, hey, wait a minute,

02:08:58.388 --> 02:08:59.480
– [Brett Easton Ellis]maybe he did do these things.

02:08:59.550 --> 02:09:03.288
– [Brett Easton Ellis]But in terms of Patrick Bateman's loneliness and his alienation from the

02:09:03.294 --> 02:09:05.788
– [Brett Easton Ellis]society he was a part of, I don't think I've ever written anything more autobiographical.

02:09:05.794 --> 02:09:09.484
So Ellis wrote American Psycho as a way to vent his

02:09:09.522 --> 02:09:11.976
confusing feelings into the character Patrick Bateman.

02:09:12.008 --> 02:09:15.696
And now Bateman has made an autobiographical movie where he does the same.

02:09:15.798 --> 02:09:19.088
The film would star Christian Bale as Patrick Bateman, Reese Witherspoon as

02:09:19.094 --> 02:09:23.036
Evelyn Williams, Jared Leto as Paul Allen, and Willem Defoe as Detective Kim.

02:09:23.068 --> 02:09:27.540
It looks like Christian Bale reprised his role as Bateman in 2005.

02:09:27.690 --> 02:09:28.292
Oh, wait.

02:09:28.346 --> 02:09:30.544
No. That's Batman, not Bateman.

02:09:30.592 --> 02:09:33.796
If he ever writes a memoir, he should definitely title it From Bateman to Batman.

02:09:33.818 --> 02:09:37.556
Patrick Bateman is the ultimate example of what happens when a

02:09:37.578 --> 02:09:39.488
consumer gamifies materialism.

02:09:39.584 --> 02:09:43.096
The deeper he delves into the lifestyle he wants to portray, the further his

02:09:43.118 --> 02:09:44.260
mind becomes lost.

02:09:44.340 --> 02:09:48.040
With no self worth outside of his money and his image, he hits rock bottom.

02:09:48.110 --> 02:09:50.648
When the yuppie trend dies out in 1991.

02:09:50.734 --> 02:09:54.476
Despite blowing up all of his relationships, his secretary Jean is there for

02:09:54.498 --> 02:09:56.108
him, and together they have a child.

02:09:56.194 --> 02:09:59.676
And after getting back on his feet and gaining control over his vices, he

02:09:59.698 --> 02:10:02.796
realizes that he does have someone he actually cares about.

02:10:02.898 --> 02:10:06.220
As I stated, I do think that some of Bateman's crimes were real.

02:10:06.290 --> 02:10:09.840
So it's not exactly a perfect happy ending because the killer gets away.

02:10:09.910 --> 02:10:13.248
But as the author stated, it's really up to you to decide where you stand on

02:10:13.254 --> 02:10:13.754
the issue.

